// 111 道內建食譜的英文翻譯（2026-10-02 由 Claude 翻譯；結構跟 recipes-data.js 一一對應，items 是 [名稱, 份量, 備註]）
window.RECIPES_EN = {"g9":{"title":"Upside-Down Mango No-Bake Cheesecake","intro":"An “inverted” mango no-bake cheesecake flips the classic structure (cookie base, cheesecake middle, fruit or jelly top). It looks surprising and also solves the problem of the crumbly base breaking when you cut it.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Mango sauce and fruit","2 mangoes (dice 1; blend the other into purée and simmer briefly with 10 g sugar and 5 g lemon juice)"],["Cream cheese","150 g","cheesecake layer"],["Caster sugar","25 g","cheesecake layer"],["Whipping cream","100 g","cheesecake layer"],["Lemon juice","5 g","cheesecake layer"],["Gelatin sheets","3 g (soaked to soften)","cheesecake layer"],["Digestive biscuits","60 g","crunchy crumb topping"],["Unsalted butter (melted)","20 g","crunchy crumb topping"],["Toasted chopped almonds / pistachios","a little","crunchy crumb topping"]]}],"steps":["Move the biscuit crumbs that normally sit at the bottom to the top (or make them into a crunchy topping), so the first bite is crisp before you dig into the smooth cheesecake and mango.","[Layers, bottom to top] Layer 1 (bottom): a thick layer of fresh diced mango + concentrated mango sauce","Layer 2 (middle): rich, silky cheesecake mousse","Layer 3 (top): toasted buttery biscuit crumbs (nice and separate), a few chopped pistachios or toasted coconut flakes","Alternatively, use a silicone mold or mousse ring: put the fruit and jelly layer at the bottom of the mold, let it set, then turn it out so the glossy fruit sits on top with the cheesecake wrapped around the filling.","[Layers, top to bottom, after unmolding] Top: lots of fresh mango petals/dice and clear jelly","Middle: mango custard or pure concentrated mango purée (as a runny center)","Bottom (outer): light, fresh cheesecake mousse","Very bottom: a thin layer of digestive biscuit base or sponge cake","Mango base: mix the fresh mango sauce with the diced mango and spread it evenly in the bottom of glass dessert cups (about 1/3 of the height). Chill in the fridge.","Cheesecake mousse: soften the cream cheese at room temperature and beat with the sugar until completely smooth. Melt the softened gelatin over a water bath, stir it into the cheese mixture, then add the lemon juice. Whip the cream to soft peaks (thick but still flowing) and gently fold it into the cheese mixture in two additions.","Assemble: pipe the mousse over the mango layer until the cups are 80% full. Cover with cling film and chill for at least 3 hours to set.","Crunchy topping: crush the biscuits, mix with the melted butter and chopped nuts, and bake at 160°C for 6-8 minutes until golden and crisp. Let cool.","Before serving: take the set cups out of the fridge, scatter the crunchy crumbs generously on top and garnish with a mint leaf."],"tips":[]},"g33":{"title":"Chiayi Turkey Rice Sauce (300 g shallots)","intro":"Using 300 g of shallots (which gives about 400 ml of fragrant shallot oil), the sauce base is scaled up in proportion so the saltiness, sweetness, soy aroma and chicken-fat flavor stay perfectly balanced.","groups":[{"name":"B. Sauce base (for 300 g shallots)","items":[["Chicken cooking juices / rich chicken stock","600 ml"],["Naturally brewed soy sauce","8 Tbsp (about 120 ml)"],["Thick soy sauce (soy paste)","2 Tbsp (about 30 ml, for sweetness and body)"],["Rock sugar","2 Tbsp (about 30 g)"],["Rice wine","4 Tbsp (about 60 ml)"],["White pepper","1 tsp"],["Five-spice powder","1/4 tsp (don't overdo it — just a hint)"],["Salt","to taste (adjust after tasting)"]]},{"name":"Step 2: Cook the turkey rice sauce","items":[["Put the rock sugar in a deep pot and dry-fry over low heat until it just melts (light brown and fragrant)",""],["Pour the rice wine in around the edge of the pot",""],["Soy sauce and thick soy sauce",""],["Heat on low until it bubbles",""],["Cook until the soy smells toasty",""],["Add","600 ml chicken stock/juices, plus the white pepper and five-spice"],["Bring to a boil on high, then simmer uncovered on low","about 10–12 minutes, until the soy and stock blend into a mellow sauce base"],["Taste; if not salty enough, adjust with","a little salt (turkey rice sauce should be a bit stronger than soup, since it's poured over rice)"]]}],"steps":["[Step 1: Shallot oil — air-fryer, no babysitting] Put 300 g sliced shallots in a heatproof deep dish and pour in 400 ml of fat, making sure the oil covers the shallots.","Air-fry at 160°C for 12–15 minutes.","At minutes 6 and 10, pull out the basket and stir gently with chopsticks so they cook evenly.","As soon as the shallots turn light golden, take them out and let the residual heat finish them — they will slowly turn crisp and golden. (Don't wait until they're dark brown, or the carry-over heat will make them bitter.)","Pour about half the shallot oil and some of the crispy shallots (about 150-200 ml oil) back into the sauce pot and simmer gently for 2 minutes so the shallot aroma merges with the sauce.","Bottle the remaining shallot oil and crispy shallots and keep them in the fridge — great as an extra topping over rice, or for tossing noodles and stir-frying."],"tips":["Shallots: 300 g (thinly sliced, as even as possible)","Total fat: about 400 ml (use chicken fat you have; top up with unsalted lard or vegetable oil)","To serve: rice ➔ shredded/sliced turkey (or chicken) ➔ 2-3 Tbsp hot sauce (with shallot oil) ➔ a spoonful of crispy shallots ➔ a pinch of white pepper."]},"g41":{"title":"Korean Ssamjang (Lettuce-Wrap Dipping Sauce)","intro":"Ssamjang (쌈장) is the essential sauce for Korean BBQ and lettuce wraps. It combines Korean soybean paste (doenjang) and Korean chili paste (gochujang): salty-sweet, a little spicy and full of garlic.","groups":[{"name":"Base (golden ratio 2:1)","items":[["Korean soybean paste (doenjang)","2 Tbsp"],["Korean chili paste (gochujang)","1 Tbsp"]]},{"name":"Aromatics and seasoning","items":[["Minced garlic","1 Tbsp (about 2-3 cloves)"],["Chopped green onion","1 Tbsp (finely chopped)"],["Minced onion","1 Tbsp (very finely chopped, for natural sweetness and texture)"],["Honey or syrup","1 Tbsp (or 0.5 Tbsp white sugar)"],["Sesame oil","1 Tbsp"],["Toasted white sesame seeds","1 Tbsp (lightly crushed for more aroma)"]]}],"steps":["Prep the aromatics: chop the garlic, green onion and onion very finely.","Mix the base: stir 2 Tbsp doenjang and 1 Tbsp gochujang together in a bowl.","Add the aromatics and seasoning: garlic, green onion, onion, honey (or sugar), sesame oil and sesame seeds.","Mix well with a spoon until smooth with a little texture."],"tips":["Sweetness and heat: for a sweeter, milder sauce, use a 3:1 ratio of doenjang to gochujang and add a little more honey. For more heat, add some finely chopped Korean green chili.","Storage: keep in an airtight container in the fridge for about 1–2 weeks. Because it contains raw garlic and onion, it tastes best eaten soon.","Uses: besides lettuce wraps, it's great for dipping cucumber, carrot sticks or mild peppers, or mixed straight into rice."]},"g44":{"title":"Flavor-Packed Grilled Corn","intro":"To make grilled corn as good as a Taiwanese night market or American BBQ joint — charred outside, juicy inside and flavored all the way through — the keys are choosing the corn, pre-cooking, timing the glaze and controlling the heat. Here are the full steps and tips:","groups":[{"name":"Sauce A: Taiwanese night-market style (savory and rich)","items":[["Shacha sauce (Taiwanese BBQ sauce)","2 Tbsp"],["Thick soy sauce (soy paste)","1.5 Tbsp"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar or honey","1 Tbsp (key: helps it caramelize)"],["Peanut butter or sesame paste","1 tsp (the secret! adds richness and helps it cling)"],["Grated garlic","1 tsp"],["Five-spice / chili powder","a pinch (to taste)"]]},{"name":"Sauce B: American garlic butter (sweet and rich)","items":[["Unsalted butter","30 g (melted)"],["Minced garlic","2 cloves"],["Soy sauce","0.5 Tbsp"],["Black pepper","a pinch"],["Grated Parmesan or cracked black pepper","to taste (sprinkle on after grilling)"]]}],"steps":["Choosing corn: waxy corn (yellow/white/purple) is chewy and soaks up sauce — best for the Taiwanese glaze. Sweet corn (bicolor) is juicy and very sweet — best for garlic butter or cheese.","Score the surface: once cooked and cooled, lightly scratch or poke the kernels with a skewer or fork (or score shallow lines with a knife) so the sauce soaks into the cob — twice the flavor."],"tips":["Don't grill it raw! Grilling raw corn takes too long and dries it out, making it tough.","Method: leave one or two thin layers of husk on, then steam or boil for 8-10 minutes until about 80% cooked. This locks in moisture, so the grilling only needs to caramelize the glaze.","Home oven: preheat to 200°C-210°C, about 12-15 minutes total, turning and brushing with sauce twice.","Air fryer: 180°C for 6 minutes, brush with sauce, then 200°C for 3-5 minutes until glossy and charred.","Charcoal / outdoor grill: use the medium-low zone so the thick soy sauce doesn't burn.","Remember the three keys — cook first, score the kernels, brush thin layers several times — and you can make perfect, fully flavored grilled corn at home!"]},"g49":{"title":"Kimchi Tofu Stew with Beef (for one)","intro":"A single-serving Korean kimchi tofu stew (soondubu jjigae style) — simple, very quick, piping hot and full of flavor:","groups":[{"name":"[Main ingredients]","items":[["Thinly sliced beef","about 80–100 g (fatty brisket or marbled beef is best — richer broth)"],["Silken / soft tofu","half to one block (cut into pieces or scooped with a spoon)"],["Kimchi","about 80 g (well-fermented, slightly sour kimchi is best; keep 2 Tbsp of the kimchi juice)"],["Onion","1/4 (sliced)"],["Green onion","1 stalk (cut into lengths; finely chop the white part)"],["Egg","1"],["Stock or water","about 300 ml (kombu-bonito dashi is best; water or stock powder also works)"]]},{"name":"[Aromatics and seasoning]","items":[["Korean chili flakes (gochugaru)","1 Tbsp (adjust to taste)"],["Korean chili paste (gochujang)","1/2 tsp (optional, for extra depth)"],["Minced garlic","1 tsp"],["Soy sauce / thick soy sauce","1 tsp"],["Sesame oil","1 Tbsp"]]},{"name":"Add the broth and seasoning","items":[["Pour in","300 ml stock (or water) and 2 Tbsp kimchi juice"],["Add soy sauce","1 tsp (stir in the gochujang now too, if using)"],["Bring to a boil on high, then simmer on medium-low","about 3–5 minutes, so the beef and kimchi flavor the broth"]]}],"steps":["Stir-fry the beef and kimchi: on medium heat, add the beef and kimchi and stir-fry until the beef changes color and the kimchi smells fragrant.","Add the tofu: slide the soft tofu into the broth gently (try to keep it in pieces). Simmer on low for about 2–3 minutes so it absorbs the flavor.","Egg and serve: taste before serving; if bland, add a little salt or soy sauce. Sprinkle with the green part of the onion and crack 1 raw egg into the middle. Turn off the heat and let the residual heat cook the egg until just set."],"tips":["In a small pot (clay pot or small saucepan), heat 1 Tbsp sesame oil over medium heat.","Add the garlic, chopped white onion and sliced onion; fry until fragrant.","Turn to low, add 1 Tbsp Korean chili flakes and stir-fry quickly for about 10–15 seconds to make a red chili oil (careful not to burn it).","Richer: serve with a bowl of rice and dunk it in the stew, or lay a slice of cheese on top just before serving for a creamier finish."]},"g81":{"title":"Braised Beef Over Rice (Shredded Beef)","intro":"Shredded meat works perfectly! Using shredded beef (or pork) for braised beef over rice cuts the cooking time dramatically, and the thin strips get evenly coated in the rich sauce.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients (serves 2)","items":[["Shredded beef","200 g (or shredded pork if you don't eat beef)"],["Onion","1/2 (sliced)"],["Carrot","1/3 (thin strips or slices so it cooks fast)"],["Bok choy / water spinach","1 bunch (cut into lengths)"],["Minced garlic and ginger","1 tsp each"],["Green onion","1 stalk (cut into lengths)"]]},{"name":"Braising sauce","items":[["Spicy chili bean paste (doubanjiang)","1 Tbsp (use non-spicy bean paste or thick soy sauce if you don't like heat)"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Oyster sauce","1 tsp"],["Sugar","1 tsp"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Water or stock","300–350 ml"],["Five-spice (or star anise) powder","a pinch (for braised flavor)"]]},{"name":"Thickening and finish","items":[["Starch slurry","1.5 Tbsp potato starch + 3 Tbsp water"],["Sesame oil","a little"]]},{"name":"Marinating the beef (velveting)","items":[["Put the shredded beef in a bowl",""],["Add soy sauce",""],["Rice wine",""],["","5 Tbsp water"],["Massage with your hand until the meat absorbs all the liquid",""],["Add","1 tsp starch and mix well, then stir in 1 tsp vegetable oil (marinate about 10–15 minutes)"]]},{"name":"Thicken and combine with the beef","items":[["Add the greens and toss briefly",""],["Turn to low, re-stir the starch slurry and drizzle it in a circle in a few additions, stirring constantly until the sauce is thick and glossy",""],["Return the cooked beef and toss quickly on high heat","15–20 seconds so it's coated in sauce; drizzle with a little sesame oil and turn off the heat"]]}],"steps":["Quick-fry the beef: heat 1.5 Tbsp oil in a pan and quickly stir-fry the beef over medium-high heat, separating the strips. When about 70–80% cooked (browned outside, slightly pink inside), remove it right away so it doesn't get tough.","Aromatics and braising base: add a little more oil to the same pan and fry the garlic, ginger and green onion until fragrant. Add the onion and carrot and cook until the onion is translucent. Add the chili bean paste and fry on low until the oil turns red and fragrant. Pour in the rice wine, soy sauce, oyster sauce, sugar and five-spice, then 300–350 ml water/stock. Boil on high, then cook on medium for 3–5 minutes so the vegetables sweeten the sauce.","Serve: spoon over hot white (or multigrain) rice and enjoy!"],"tips":["Soy sauce: 1 Tbsp","Rice wine: 1 tsp","Water: 1.5 Tbsp (absorbed by the meat)","Potato starch / cornstarch: 1 tsp","Vegetable oil: 1 tsp (added last to seal in moisture)","Cutting: if you shred the beef yourself, cut across the grain so it stays tender.","Cook separately: the thin strips should never boil in the sauce or they'll shrink and toughen. Fry them first, remove, and toss them in the sauce at the very end — silky, restaurant-style results!"]},"g126":{"title":"Sugarcane-Smoked Chicken","intro":"Traditional sugarcane chicken can be smoked with either sugarcane bagasse or sugar, and the two are often combined. Many old shops mix bagasse and sugar in the bottom of the wok: the bagasse gives a unique fruitwood-like smoke and the sugar gives color. If you only have one, it works perfectly on its own too.","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients and tools","items":[["Main","1 whole or half chicken, already cooked (boiled or steamed) with the skin air-dried"],["Dried sugarcane bagasse","1~2 handfuls (lightly broken up)","smoking mix"],["Rice","1 Tbsp (helps make smoke and absorbs moisture)","smoking mix"],["Tea leaves","1 Tbsp (optional, adds depth)","smoking mix"],["Tools","heavy iron wok or old wok, steaming rack, aluminum foil"]]}],"steps":["Prepare the wok: line the bottom with 2 layers of foil (prevents burning and sticky residue).","Add the smoking mix: spread the bagasse in the middle of the foil and scatter the rice and tea leaves evenly over it.","Set up the chicken: place the rack in the wok and put the fully cooked, dried chicken on it, skin side up (it shouldn't touch the smoking mix).","Make smoke on high: cover and heat on medium-high until thick white smoke escapes around the lid (about 3~5 minutes).","Smoke on low: turn to low and smoke for about 8~12 minutes (depending on the size of the chicken and how smoky you like it).","Rest: turn off the heat and keep the lid closed for another 5~8 minutes so the smoky aroma settles in.","Brush with oil: take it out and brush the skin with a thin layer of sesame oil or chicken fat to lock in moisture and aroma.","[Method 2: sugar smoking — easy home caramel color] If you can't find bagasse, brown sugar is the easiest substitute, with a strong caramel aroma and a beautiful color.","[1. Ingredients and tools] Main: 1 whole or half chicken, already cooked (boiled or steamed) with the skin air-dried","Smoking mix: 3 Tbsp cane sugar (light or dark brown), 2 Tbsp white rice, 1 Tbsp tea leaves (black or oolong)","Tools: heavy iron wok or old wok, steaming rack, aluminum foil","Line the wok: lay two layers of heavy foil on the bottom to protect it.","Mix the smoking mix: combine the sugar, rice and tea and spread it over the foil. (The rice keeps the sugar from melting too fast and turning bitter; the tea balances the sweetness.)","Set up the chicken: put in the rack and the dried cooked chicken.","Heat and color: cover tightly and heat on medium. When the sugar starts to caramelize and gives off thick yellow-white smoke, turn to low and smoke for 5~8 minutes.","Watch the color: sugar colors very quickly, so don't go too long — turn off the heat once the skin is a lovely reddish-brown or golden.","Rest and oil: rest with the lid on for 3 minutes, then open and brush the skin with sesame oil while hot.","The skin must be dry: after cooking the chicken, air-dry it somewhere ventilated or pat it completely dry with paper towels. Any moisture makes the smoke taste bitter and stops the color from taking.","Choice of pan: use a heavy iron, stainless steel or old wok — not non-stick, as long dry heating can damage the coating.","Combine both (best method): if you can, smoke with 1 handful bagasse + 2 Tbsp sugar + 1 Tbsp rice for the best balance of color and aroma."],"tips":["Sugarcane bagasse: gives a very strong woody smoke aroma and lots of thick smoke.","Cane sugar (brown sugar/rock sugar): melts and caramelizes at high heat, giving the skin a glossy golden caramel color and a light sweet aroma."]},"g173":{"title":"Hand-Torn Chicken (Brined and Roasted)","intro":"Being only 2 cm from the heating element is really close (the top is the hottest zone and burns easily), but with a foil shield and staged temperatures you can avoid burning and get a beautiful amber, crispy skin! Here's the full process and seasoning from the end of brining until it goes into the air fryer:","groups":[{"name":"Stuff the cavity (for aroma)","items":[["Stuff the cavity with","half an onion (smashed)"],["2 lengths of green onion",""],["2 garlic cloves — they steam the aroma from the inside",""],["and help hold the chicken's shape",""]]}],"steps":["Make the Taiwanese old-style glaze: 1 Tbsp soy sauce + 1 tsp honey (or maltose) + 1 tsp oil + a little garlic powder or five-spice. Mix and set aside (note: don't brush it on at the start!).","Because the top is so close to the element, it's strongly recommended to start back side up and cover with foil:","[Stage 1: back up + foil (low-temperature cooking & protecting the top)] Position: put the chicken in the air fryer back side up, breast down.","Burn protection: loosely cover the top of the chicken with a sheet of foil, shiny side out (not tight — just enough to block direct heat from the element).","Setting: air-fry at 150°C for 25 minutes.","Purpose: the gentle hot air cooks the inside first, while the breast soaks up the fat dripping down and stays tender.","[Stage 2: flip (breast up) + foil again] Position: open the air fryer and turn the chicken over (breast and legs up).","Burn protection: keep the foil over the breast / highest point.","Setting: air-fry at 160°C for 15 minutes.","Check doneness: poke a chopstick into the thickest part of the thigh. If the juices run clear (no blood), it's cooked through.","[Stage 3: glaze + remove foil (final color)] Remove the foil.","Brush a thin, even layer of the Taiwanese glaze over the skin (especially the breast and legs).","Setting: raise to 180°C and air-fry for 5~8 minutes.","Note: stay next to the air fryer for these last 5~8 minutes! With the element only 2 cm away, soy sauce and honey color very fast — as soon as the skin is golden amber and lightly bubbling, turn it off and take it out.","[Step 3: rest and dipping powder] Rest 10 minutes (the key to juicy meat): after air-frying, put the chicken on a plate or rack and leave it for 10 minutes before cutting or tearing. This lets the juices pushed out by the heat soak back into the meat, so it bursts with juice when you tear it.","Make the dipping powder while it rests: 2 tsp white pepper + 1 tsp salt + a pinch of five-spice. Put on gloves and tear in!"],"tips":["Lift the chicken out of the brine — no need to rinse.","Pat the skin and the cavity completely dry with paper towels (the drier the skin, the crispier it gets).","Then leave it at room temperature for 15～20 minutes to take the chill off (cooking it straight from cold can leave it burnt outside and raw inside)."]},"g211":{"title":"Steamed Egg with Scallop Powder (Dairy-Free)","intro":"Using pure ground dried-scallop powder with a little salt instead of bonito powder and soy sauce is an excellent choice — and perfect for when the stomach is recovering!","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Eggs","2"],["Warm water (or cooled fat-free clear chicken stock)","about 220–250 ml"],["Homemade / natural scallop powder","1/2 tsp"],["Salt","1/4 tsp (adjust very slightly to taste)"]]}],"steps":["Dissolve the umami: stir the scallop powder and salt into 220–250 ml warm water (or cooled stock) so the scallop flavor fully infuses the water.","Beat and mix: beat 2 eggs in a bowl, then slowly pour in the scallop water from step 1 and stir gently.","Strain and skim: strain the egg mixture through a fine sieve 1–2 times and spoon off the bubbles on top (this makes it silky like pudding).","Cover and steam: cover the bowl with a small heatproof plate or lid (so condensation doesn't drip in and make holes). Once the steamer water boils, put it in and steam on low for 12–15 minutes until fully set.","The result is gentle and delicious — it satisfies the craving for something tasty while being kind to the stomach and safe with the medication!"],"tips":["Natural umami that's gentle on the stomach: store-bought bonito powder or soy sauce often contains a lot of sodium, MSG, preservatives or strong saltiness and acidity, which can irritate a stomach lining that's just recovering. Natural scallop powder is rich in natural glutamate and gives a clean umami with no burden.","High protein and very easy to absorb: scallops are high in protein and very low in fat. Ground fine and steamed into egg, the texture is very gentle and puts no strain on a recovering digestive system.","No antibiotic conflict: scallops are seafood with quality protein and have no interactions with Cipro (ciprofloxacin) or other medicines she was taking."]},"g212":{"title":"Get-Well Meal: Chicken Soup + Steamed Egg + Boiled Vegetables","intro":"Your meal plan is a great idea and fits the “low fat, easy to digest, lots of fluid” principle very well. But to make sure my wife's stomach isn't upset again by small irritants, 3 details need adjusting:","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Chicken","half a chicken (or about 400-500g thigh/breast)"],["Onion","1/2 (cut into big chunks)"],["Ginger","2 slices"],["Garlic","1 clove (smashed)"],["Water","about 1200ml"],["Salt","to taste"]]},{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Eggs","2"],["Warm water (or cooled fat-free chicken stock)","about 220-250ml (egg to water about 1 : 2)"],["Bonito powder","1/4 tsp"],["Soy sauce","1/2 tsp"],["Salt","a pinch"]]},{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Medium potato","1"],["Green zucchini (kusa)","1"],["Carrot","1/2"],["Salt","a little"],["A few drops of good olive oil (optional)",""]]}],"steps":["Chicken soup (adjusting garlic and ginger): onion becomes sweet and easy to digest when cooked soft, but lots of raw ginger and garlic contain strong pungent compounds and FODMAPs that can trigger stomach acid or bloating. Use just 1-2 smashed garlic cloves for flavor (or skip for now) and only 2 slices of ginger. The key is skimming off all the fat after simmering.","Steamed egg (bonito powder and soy sauce): bonito powder usually contains a lot of sodium and artificial flavoring, and soy sauce is salty and slightly acidic. Since she was taking Cipro (ciprofloxacin), use only a few drops of soy sauce for color and flavor and just a little bonito powder, so it's not too salty.","Boiled vegetables (potato/zucchini/carrot): a perfect combination! Potato gives good starch, and peeled zucchini and carrot cooked soft are very easy to absorb and gentle on the gut.","Blanch from cold: put the chicken in a pot with plenty of cold water, boil on high for 2-3 minutes to release the scum, then lift it out and rinse in warm water.","Simmer: fill a clean pot with about 1200ml water, add the chicken, onion, ginger and garlic. Bring to a boil, then cover and simmer on low for 30–40 minutes until the chicken is very tender.","Skim and season: turn off the heat and carefully skim every bit of fat from the surface (or use a fat-skimming sieve). Season with a little salt and serve.","Note: for the patient, focus on the clear broth and the soft chicken and onion.","[2. Japanese-style steamed egg] Beat and season: beat 2 eggs in a bowl with the bonito powder, soy sauce and a little salt.","Add the liquid: pour in the warm water or warm stock and stir gently.","Strain (key step): strain through a fine sieve 1-2 times and skim off the small bubbles (for a smooth surface with no holes).","Steam: cover the bowl with a heatproof lid or plate (so condensation doesn't drip in). In a Tatung rice cooker, put 1 cup of water in the outer pot; in a regular steamer, add it once the water boils and steam on low for 12-15 minutes until set.","[3. Soft, sweet boiled vegetables] Prep: peel the potato and carrot and cut into small chunks / thick slices; always peel the zucchini, then cut into thick slices.","Boil in stages: bring water to a boil with a little salt. Add the potato and carrot first and cook on medium-low for about 10-12 minutes until completely soft (a chopstick slides through easily). Add the peeled zucchini last and cook another 3-5 minutes until soft.","Serve: drain the vegetables, then toss gently with a few drops of olive oil and a little salt."],"tips":["Medication timing: for 2 hours before and after a 500mg Cipro dose, avoid milk, cheese and other dairy (this dinner has no dairy at all, so it's safe).","Taking pills: take the medicine with plain warm water, not chicken soup.","Small, frequent meals: if appetite hasn't fully returned, start with half a bowl of clear soup and a few bites of egg and soft potato — no need to finish everything at once."]},"g261":{"title":"Plain Rice Congee in a Tatung Cooker (from raw rice)","intro":"For a smooth, easy-to-digest plain congee for someone who's sick, made in a Tatung electric cooker, use a 1:8 ratio of rice to water.","groups":[],"steps":["Rinse and soak: rinse 0.5 cup white rice. If you have time, soak it in water for 15～30 minutes so the grains absorb water and the congee turns out softer and silkier.","Inner pot: drain the soaking water and put the 0.5 cup rinsed rice and 4 cups water in the inner pot (using the same measuring cup).","Outer pot: add 1 cup water to the outer pot.","Start cooking: put the lid on and press the switch."],"tips":["Prevent boil-overs: lay a heatproof bamboo skewer or chopstick between the inner and outer pot (or under the inner lid) to leave a small gap so the congee doesn't bubble over.","Rest after it clicks off (key): when the switch pops up, don't open it right away. Leave it for another 15～20 minutes so the rice fully breaks down and releases its starch.","Check: open the lid and stir evenly for 1 minute. If the grains have fully burst open and the liquid is thick and milky white, it's done.","It's extremely hot: let the bowl cool to warm (about 40°C～50°C) before giving it to the patient, to avoid burning the throat.","For an upset stomach: at first, give just the rice water on top or plain congee; hold off on fatty or salty toppings like meat floss to go easy on the stomach."]},"g264":{"title":"Orange Electrolyte Drink","intro":"It can't be exactly the same, but orange juice + lemon juice gets you 80-90% of the flavor of an isotonic sports drink like Pocari Sweat. The core of that flavor is slightly sour, slightly salty, lightly sweet, with a signature lemon-citrus aroma. Pure orange juice is too sweet, not sour enough and lacks the freshness of citric acid; add a little lemon juice and a pinch of salt and you get that cool, easy-drinking taste.","groups":[{"name":"Sports-drink-style natural electrolyte water (about 500 mL)","items":[["Warm / cool boiled water","400 mL"],["Fresh orange juice","80 mL (natural fruit sugar and potassium)"],["Fresh lemon juice","20 mL (about 1～2 tsp — the key sourness)"],["Salt","1.2 g (about 1/4 tsp / a pinch, for sodium)"],["Sugar or honey","1 Tbsp (about 10～15 g, adjust to taste)"]]}],"steps":["Lemon juice is the soul: the citrus note of these drinks leans toward grapefruit/lemon. Orange water alone tastes like diluted juice — the lemon sourness makes it right.","Measure the salt carefully: just a pinch (about 1/4 tsp). Too much tastes like salt water; a little brings out the fruitiness and sweetness.","Strain the pulp: strain the squeezed orange juice to remove the pulp so it's closer to the clean feel of a bottled sports drink."],"tips":["To mix: dissolve the salt and sugar/honey completely in a little warm water first, then add the orange juice, lemon juice and the rest of the water and stir well."]},"g296":{"title":"Shacha Lamb Thick Soup (2 liters)","intro":"Shacha lamb thick soup (makes 2 liters)","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients and seasoning","items":[["Sliced lamb","400g","main"],["Napa cabbage (or cabbage)","400g","main"],["Shredded wood ear mushroom","60g","main"],["Shredded carrot","60g","main"],["Shredded bamboo shoots","100g","main"],["Rice wine","2 Tbsp","lamb marinade"],["Soy sauce","2 tsp","lamb marinade"],["Potato starch","2 tsp","lamb marinade"],["Shacha sauce (Taiwanese BBQ sauce)","2 tsp","lamb marinade"],["Stock (or water)","2000ml","soup base"],["Shacha sauce","5 Tbsp","soup base"],["Shacha oil","2 Tbsp","soup base"],["Minced garlic","2 Tbsp","soup base"],["Thai basil — lots (about a handful)","","soup base"],["Soy sauce","3 Tbsp","soup base"],["Chinese black vinegar","4 Tbsp","soup base"],["Sugar","2 tsp","soup base"],["White pepper","1 tsp","soup base"],["Thickener","starch slurry (4 Tbsp potato starch + 6 Tbsp water, mixed)"]]}],"steps":["Marinate the lamb: mix the lamb with the rice wine, soy sauce, shacha sauce and starch; marinate for 10~15 minutes.","Fry the base: heat the shacha oil, fry the garlic until fragrant, then add 5 Tbsp shacha sauce and fry slowly on low until aromatic.","Make the soup: pour in 2000ml stock and add the cabbage, wood ear, carrot and bamboo shoots. Boil on high, then cook on medium for about 8~10 minutes until the vegetables are soft and flavorful.","Season and thicken: add the soy sauce, sugar and white pepper. Turn to low and drizzle in the starch slurry in several additions, stirring, until it's as thick as you like.","Cook the lamb: keep it at a gentle simmer and add the marinated lamb slice by slice; cook until it changes color and is done (about 1~1.5 minutes — don't overcook).","Finish: just before turning off the heat, pour the black vinegar around the edge of the pot and add lots of basil leaves."],"tips":["Evaporation: 2000ml stock plus water from the vegetables and the slurry makes a little over 2 liters. In a shallow pot more water evaporates while boiling, so top up with hot water as needed.","Thickening: with 2 liters of soup, always add the slurry in stages and watch the thickness as it bubbles. Stop when you like the texture — you don't have to use it all."]},"g344":{"title":"Scallion Beef Stir-Fry (320 g)","intro":"The secret to tender, never-tough beef is slicing it thinly across the grain and letting it fully absorb water in the marinade. Here is the golden-ratio recipe tailored to 320 g of beef:","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients and seasoning","items":[["Main","sliced beef (sirloin or chuck)","320 g"],["Marinade","soy sauce","1 Tbsp (about 15 ml)"],["Stir-fry seasoning","oyster sauce","1 Tbsp"]]}],"steps":["Prep and marinate: slice the beef thinly across the grain. Add soy sauce, rice wine and water and massage until the beef absorbs all the liquid, mix in the starch, then add 1 tsp vegetable oil to seal in moisture. Marinate for 15 minutes.","Heat the oil: heat 2 Tbsp oil in a wok on high to about 160-170°C.","Flash-fry the beef: add the beef, separate the slices quickly and stir-fry on high until about 80% cooked (about 20-30 seconds, just changed color). Remove right away.","Aromatics: leave a little oil in the wok and fry the ginger slices, garlic slices and white parts of the green onion until fragrant.","Combine on high heat: add the oyster sauce, soy sauce, white pepper and rice wine and toss quickly, then return the beef with the green parts of the onion and stir-fry on high for 10-15 seconds.","Finish: once the green onion is just softened and the sauce coats the beef, drizzle sesame oil around the edge of the wok, turn off the heat and serve."],"tips":[]},"g387":{"title":"Shacha Lamb Fried Rice with Garlic Chives (450 g)","intro":"This is a chive and shacha lamb fried rice recipe rebalanced for 450 g of lamb (3 times the original). With this much meat, cooking in batches is the key to keeping the rice dry and the wok hot.","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Sliced lamb","450g"],["Cold cooked rice","4~4.5 bowls (about 3 servings)"],["Garlic chives","120~150g (chopped 0.5~1 cm; wash and dry thoroughly)"],["Eggs","3"],["Garlic","8~9 cloves (chopped)"],["Chili","2~3 (sliced, to taste)"]]},{"name":"Lamb marinade","items":[["Shacha sauce (Taiwanese BBQ sauce)","1 Tbsp (15ml)"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp (15ml)"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp (15ml)"],["Potato starch / cornstarch","1.5 tsp"],["Vegetable oil","1.5 tsp (added last to seal in moisture)"]]},{"name":"Fried rice seasoning","items":[["Shacha sauce","4 Tbsp (60ml)"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp (15ml, splashed around the wok edge just before serving)"],["White pepper","1/2 tsp"],["Salt","1/2 tsp (taste and adjust after tossing)"]]}],"steps":["Marinate the lamb: mix the lamb with the rice wine, soy sauce, 1 Tbsp shacha sauce and starch, then mix in the oil to seal in moisture. Marinate for 15 minutes.","Flash-fry the lamb in batches (key step): 450g is a lot, so do 2 batches. Heat 1.5 Tbsp oil and quickly separate the lamb on high heat; cook until about 70% done (just turned pale) and remove immediately, keeping the juices on the plate.","Aromatics and egg: add 1 Tbsp oil to the same wok, fry the garlic and chili until fragrant, then crack in the 3 eggs and scramble quickly until half set.","Rice and seasoning: add the cold rice, turn to high and break it up with the spatula. Once the grains separate, add 4 Tbsp shacha sauce, white pepper and salt and keep stir-frying on high until every grain is evenly colored.","Add the lamb: return all the lamb (with its juices) and toss on high for about 30 seconds to combine.","Chives and finish: scatter in the chopped chives and splash 1 Tbsp soy sauce around the edge of the wok; toss on high for 15 seconds until the chives are fragrant, then turn off the heat and serve.","Keep the heat up: a lot of lamb releases a lot of liquid, so always fry it fast in batches and remove it quickly — otherwise it stews and loses its tenderness.","Tossing the rice: 4 bowls of rice fills a normal home pan or wok, so use a chopping and pushing motion so the heat gets evenly into every grain.","Is your wok big enough? If you're not sure, you can fry the rice and the meat separately and combine them at the end!"],"tips":[]},"g445":{"title":"Beef with Garlic Chives and Pressed Tofu","intro":"A classic, quick and appetizing home-style stir-fry! The keys are tender beef, pressed tofu fried until fragrant, and chives added last so they stay bright green.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Sliced / shredded beef","150g","main"],["Garlic chives","1 small bunch, cut into lengths","main"],["Pressed (dried) tofu","4-5 pieces, cut into strips","main"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp","marinade"],["Rice wine","1 tsp","marinade"],["White pepper","a pinch","marinade"],["Potato / sweet-potato starch","1 tsp","marinade"],["Vegetable oil","1 tsp, added last to seal in moisture","marinade"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Minced garlic","2 cloves"],["Soy sauce / thick soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Oyster sauce","1 tsp"],["Sugar","a pinch"],["Rice wine (splashed around the wok edge)","1 tsp"]]}],"steps":["Marinate the beef: cut the beef into strips or slices, mix with the soy sauce, rice wine and white pepper, then the starch for tenderness, and finally 1 tsp oil to seal in moisture. Marinate 10-15 minutes.","Fry the tofu: heat oil in a wok and fry the tofu strips on medium, turning, until lightly golden, slightly toasty and wrinkled. Remove and set aside.","Flash-fry the beef: using the oil left in the wok, stir-fry the beef on high until about 70% done (just turned pale) and remove quickly.","Aromatics and seasoning: fry the garlic in the same wok, add the fried tofu, then the thick soy sauce, oyster sauce and a little sugar and toss well.","Combine on high heat: add the white (tougher) parts of the chives first and stir-fry 30 seconds, then add the beef, chive leaves and 1 tsp rice wine and toss on high for 5-10 seconds. As soon as the chives soften slightly, turn off the heat and serve."],"tips":["Fry the tofu first: it absorbs the sauce better and gets a chewier texture.","Add the chives in stages: the stems and leaves cook at different speeds, so adding them separately keeps the leaves from turning dark and limp.","High heat all the way: beef and chives cook very fast — never cook them long. Locking in the juices and freshness is what makes it delicious."]},"g453":{"title":"Halva Milk Crumble Spread","intro":"The box in the photo is halva (halawa), common in the Middle East — made mainly from tahini, sugar and pistachios. It's slightly crumbly with a rich sesame aroma and natural oils. Because it's sweet and nutty, it can replace part of the sugar and fat to make a unique Middle Eastern pistachio-sesame version of the Taiwanese milk crumble spread (naisu).","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Halva","50 g (crumble it with a spoon first)"],["Unsalted butter","50 g (softened at room temperature)"],["Full-fat milk powder","40 g"],["Powdered sugar","5–10 g (halva is already sweet, so you can skip it and adjust at the end)"],["Salt","a pinch (about 0.5 g, to balance the sweetness)"]]}],"steps":["Soften the butter: leave the unsalted butter at room temperature until your finger leaves an easy dent.","Crumble the halva: crush it finely with a spoon or fork.","Mix: combine the softened butter and halva and stir with a spatula until smooth.","Add the milk powder: sift in the milk powder and salt and mix until no dry powder remains."],"tips":["Thick toast: spread it thickly on bread and bake in a preheated oven at 180°C for about 6–8 minutes until lightly golden and bubbling. Baking brings out even more sesame and butter aroma.","Bun filling: use it as a filling or spread for homemade buns; pair with unsweetened black tea or black coffee to balance the sweetness.","Small batch for 1–2 slices (2026-10-01): about 20g unsalted butter, 15–20g full-fat milk powder, 3–5g powdered sugar, a pinch of salt, and halva in the original ratio (about the same weight as the butter). Spread thickly on bread and bake at 180°C for 3–5 minutes until bubbling and golden. Add a little more butter if it's dry, a little more salt if it's too sweet."]},"g463":{"title":"Sliced Beef Noodle Soup (Beef-Bone Broth in a Tatung Cooker)","intro":"For a sweet, rich tomato-braised beef noodle soup, the Tatung electric cooker is the easiest tool. Based on what you have, here's the best timeline and broth recipe. Start around noon or early afternoon (4~6 hours before dinner):","groups":[{"name":"Ingredient prep","items":[["Beef rib fingers + beef sirloin","cut into big chunks (they shrink, so big is fine). Rib fingers give fat and aroma; sirloin gives meaty texture"],["1 onion","cut into chunks or slices"],["2~3 tomatoes","cut into chunks (score an X on the bottom and blanch to peel for a smoother broth; unpeeled is fine)"],["1 carrot","roll-cut into chunks"],["Ginger","3-4 slices","aromatics (if you have them)"],["Garlic","4-5 cloves","aromatics (if you have them)"],["Green onion","2 stalks (cut into lengths)","aromatics (if you have them)"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Soy sauce","4-5 Tbsp"],["Thick soy sauce or rock sugar","1 Tbsp"],["Spicy chili bean paste (doubanjiang)","1-2 Tbsp (optional)"],["Rice wine","2 Tbsp"],["Water",""]]}],"steps":["Let the flavors soak in and skim the fat: after cooking, leave the beef in the broth for 2~3 hours — it absorbs much more flavor and gets more tender.","Easy fat removal: once cooled, you can easily skim off the excess beef fat on top (rib fingers are quite fatty), so the broth is rich but not greasy.","Blanch the meat (key for removing gamy smell): put the beef in cold water with a few slices of ginger and a little rice wine, heat slowly until boiling and scum rises, then lift it out and rinse.","Fry the base (for aroma): in a wok with a little oil, fry the garlic, ginger and onion until lightly golden; add the tomatoes and cook until soft and juicy, then add the chili bean paste and soy sauce and fry until fragrant. (To do it all in the cooker, just put the aromatics and beef straight into the inner pot.)","Stew in the cooker: put the fried base, blanched beef and carrot in the Tatung inner pot. Add the rice wine and rock sugar, and water just to cover everything. Put 2 cups of water in the outer pot and press the switch (about 40-50 minutes).","Rest and soak: when the switch pops up, don't open it — let it sit for 30 minutes. Then check the beef; for even softer meat, add 1 more cup of water to the outer pot and cook again.","[Slicing and serving] Take out the stewed sirloin and rib fingers, let them cool slightly and slice thinly along the grain (cooled meat slices without falling apart).","Noodles and assembly: boil the noodles and greens in a separate pot. Put the noodles in bowls, top with the beef slices, soft carrot and tomato, ladle in the hot tomato beef-bone broth and sprinkle with chopped green onion."],"tips":[]},"g483":{"title":"Japanese Cabbage Salad Dressings","intro":"Japanese shredded-cabbage salad usually comes with one of two dressings: a rich, smooth sesame dressing, or a sweet-sour Japanese wafu dressing (yuzu/onion style). Both bring out the crunch of the cabbage and cut through fried food.","groups":[{"name":"Golden ratios for the two classic dressings","items":[["White sesame seeds","2 Tbsp (toast and grind until half powder, half seeds)"],["Japanese mayonnaise (or regular)","3 Tbsp"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Mirin (or sugar)","1 tsp"],["White vinegar (or rice vinegar)","1 Tbsp"],["Sesame oil","0.5 tsp"],["Grated onion","1 Tbsp (grated very finely, for natural sweetness)"],["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["White vinegar / lemon juice","2 Tbsp"],["Mirin","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1 tsp"],["Vegetable oil / sesame oil","1 Tbsp"],["Toasted white sesame seeds","1 tsp"]]}],"steps":["Cutting: remove the hard core, roll up the leaves and slice along the grain into very fine shreds.","Ice bath: soak the shreds in ice water for 5～10 minutes (not longer, or they lose flavor) — they soak up water and become incredibly crisp.","Drain: spin completely dry in a salad spinner or drying net. If they're wet, the dressing gets diluted and the cabbage loses its crunch."],"tips":["1. Rich: Japanese sesame dressing (goma dressing) — the first 6 ingredients","2. Light: Japanese onion wafu dressing — the last 7 ingredients","Want to learn how to cut restaurant-fine cabbage shreds with just a knife, no special tools?"]},"g484":{"title":"Japanese Tonkatsu Sauce","intro":"The heart of tonkatsu sauce is a perfect balance of sour, sweet, salty and rich spice. If you don't have a bottle at home, you can mix one quickly from common seasonings.","groups":[{"name":"Just stir these together in a bowl","items":[["Chinese black vinegar / Worcestershire sauce: 2 Tbsp",""],["Ketchup","2 Tbsp"],["Oyster sauce (or soy sauce)","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar (or honey)","1 tsp"],["White sesame seeds (toasted)","1 tsp (grind half-way for more aroma)"]]}],"steps":["Freshly ground sesame: the classic tonkatsu-shop way is to grind white sesame in a small bowl until half powder, half seeds, then pour in the sauce. The sesame oil softens the acidity and instantly lifts the flavor.","Adjusting: for tangier and lighter, add 0.5 tsp more ketchup or a little Japanese mustard (karashi). For richer and sweeter, use honey instead of sugar and add a little garlic or onion powder."],"tips":["This sauce is fruity, tangy and richly salty-sweet — great with lean steaks with little marbling, adding flavor and moisture!"]},"g486":{"title":"Grated Yam over Barley Rice (Tororo Mugi-meshi)","intro":"Tororo mugi-meshi is a classic, nourishing Japanese dish: silky, sticky grated yam poured over fluffy, fiber-rich barley rice. Smooth and very healthy. The keys are the right barley-to-rice ratio and seasoning the yam.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients (about 2～3 servings)","items":[["White rice","1 cup"],["Barley (pressed or pearl)","0.5 cup"],["Water","the usual amount for 1.5 cups of rice, plus about 30～50 ml extra (barley absorbs more)"],["Japanese nagaimo yam","about 200～250 g"],["Japanese dashi stock","3～4 Tbsp (cooled)"],["Light soy sauce (or regular)","1 tsp"],["Mirin","1 tsp"],["Salt","a pinch"]]},{"name":"Toppings (as you like)","items":[["Egg yolk (or raw egg)","1"],["Toasted sesame seeds, shredded nori, chopped green onion","to taste"]]},{"name":"Seasoning the yam","items":[["Add the cooled dashi",""],["soy sauce",""],["mirin and","a pinch of salt to the grated yam"],["Whisk quickly in one direction with chopsticks or a small whisk so the dashi blends in and air is whipped in — the yam becomes lighter and silkier",""]]}],"steps":["Rinse and soak: rinse the rice; just rinse the barley lightly (no need to scrub). Combine and add the right amount of water.","Soaking time: soak for 30 minutes so the barley absorbs water and cooks up soft and springy.","Cook and rest: cook on the normal rice setting. When it's done, let it rest 15 minutes, then open and fluff the barley rice to release extra steam.","Prepare the yam: peel it (if your skin reacts to the yam's slime, wear gloves or dip it in vinegar water before peeling). Grate it into a fine, sticky purée.","Fill a bowl with hot barley rice.","Pour the seasoned grated yam generously over the rice.","Make a small well in the middle for the egg yolk, then sprinkle with nori, green onion and sesame seeds."],"tips":["Grating tool: a traditional ceramic grating bowl or fine grater is best — hand-grated yam has far better stickiness and texture than blended.","The dashi must be cool: use cold or room-temperature dashi. Hot dashi sets the enzymes in the yam and ruins the silky texture.","Itchy hands: if your hands itch after peeling yam, wash them with diluted white vinegar or rinse with warm water.","Want quick alternatives to dashi (like store-bought noodle soup base) or tips on choosing barley?"]},"g488":{"title":"Japanese Beef Katsu (Gyukatsu)","intro":"The key to gyukatsu is a very hot, fast fry: a golden, crunchy crust with a tender, juicy rare-to-medium-rare inside. Served tororo-style with fresh grated yam, it perfectly balances the rich marbling of wagyu or ribeye.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Ribeye steak","1 (2～3 cm thick)","main"],["Nagaimo yam","100g","main"],["Panko breadcrumbs","1 bowl","main"],["Cake (low-protein) flour","2 Tbsp","coating"],["Beaten egg","1","coating"],["Salt","","seasoning"],["and black pepper","a little each","seasoning"],["Soy sauce","1 tsp","seasoning"],["Wasabi","to taste","seasoning"]]}],"steps":["Bring the steak to room temperature: take it out 30 minutes ahead and pat it completely dry. Just before frying, season lightly with salt and pepper (don't salt too early or it draws out moisture).","Grate the yam: peel the yam and grate it on a fine grater into a smooth, sticky purée; mix in 1 tsp soy sauce.","Three-step coating: coat the steak in flour (tap off the excess) → beaten egg → panko. Press the panko on firmly so it's fully covered, and let it rest 3 minutes so the coating sets.","Fry hot and fast: heat plenty of oil to 190°C–200°C (a pinch of panko should float and sizzle immediately). Fry the steak about 45 to 60 seconds per side until golden and crisp, then lift it out quickly.","Rest and slice: rest on a wire rack for 2 minutes (lets the heat carry through and keeps the juices in), then cut into thick slices with a sharp knife in single strokes."],"tips":["The highlight: the cut side should be pink, medium-rare. Ribeye is well marbled, and the quick fry locks in its broth-like juices.","How to eat: dip a hot slice in wasabi soy sauce, top with a spoonful of savory, sticky grated yam and eat with rice — silky and not heavy.","Hot enough oil is the key to crisp outside and tender inside. If you have a heavy iron griddle, you can also sear the slices briefly on both sides for extra aroma. Want suggestions for dipping sauces or rice pairings?"]},"g505":{"title":"Eggless Chocolate Cupcakes","intro":"Eggless (vegan) cakes usually get their lift and moisture from a vinegar–baking soda reaction, fruit purée or yogurt.","groups":[{"name":"What the egg substitutes do","items":[["Unflavored applesauce / mashed banana","replaces the moisture and binding of eggs; good for dense cakes (chocolate, banana)"],["White vinegar / apple cider vinegar + baking soda","the acid-base reaction makes CO₂ bubbles for an excellent fluffy texture"],["Plain yogurt / plant yogurt","adds moisture and a slight tang; texture very close to a regular cake"]]},{"name":"Dry ingredients","items":[["All-purpose flour","100g"],["Caster sugar","50g"],["Unsweetened cocoa powder","15g"],["Baking soda","1/2 tsp"],["Salt","1/8 tsp"]]},{"name":"Wet ingredients","items":[["Water (or plant milk)","120ml"],["Vegetable oil (sunflower or corn)","30g"],["White vinegar (or apple cider vinegar)","1/2 Tbsp"],["Vanilla extract","1/2 tsp"]]}],"steps":["Preheat the oven to 180°C and line the cupcake pan with paper cases.","Mix the dry ingredients: sift the flour, cocoa and baking soda into a large bowl, then stir in the sugar and salt.","Add the wet ingredients: water, oil, vinegar and vanilla.","Mix quickly: stir gently with a spatula or whisk just until no dry flour remains (don't overmix, or gluten develops and the cake gets tough).","Fill and bake: divide the batter between 6 cupcake cases (about 70% full) and bake at 180°C for 18–20 minutes.","Test: a skewer inserted in the center should come out without wet batter.","Want me to adapt the recipe to another flavor (matcha, banana or Earl Grey)?"],"tips":["Classic eggless chocolate cupcakes (makes 6)"]},"g558":{"title":"Cumin Lamb Fried Noodles (Spaghetti as Yellow Noodles)","intro":"To turn spaghetti into Taiwanese yellow (alkaline) noodles, the key is baking soda. It changes the pH so the durum wheat gets the springy bite and yellow color of alkaline noodles.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Spaghetti","200g"],["Water","2000 ml"],["Baking soda","2 Tbsp (about 20g)"],["Cooking oil","1 Tbsp"]]},{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Homemade yellow noodles","2 portions","main"],["Sliced lamb","250g","main"],["Water spinach (or garlic shoots)","150g","vegetables"],["Onion","half (sliced)","vegetables"],["Garlic","3 cloves (chopped)","vegetables"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp","lamb marinade"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp","lamb marinade"],["Potato starch","1 tsp","lamb marinade"],["Ground cumin","1 tsp","lamb marinade"],["Vegetable oil","1 tsp","lamb marinade"],["Ground cumin","1 Tbsp","noodle sauce"],["Cumin seeds","1 tsp (for texture; optional)","noodle sauce"],["Soy sauce","1.5 Tbsp","noodle sauce"],["Oyster sauce","1 Tbsp","noodle sauce"],["Sugar","1 tsp","noodle sauce"],["Water (or stock)","50 ml","noodle sauce"],["Coarse chili flakes (to taste)","","noodle sauce"]]}],"steps":["Bring the water to a boil and add the baking soda (it will foam — use a deep pot).","Add the spaghetti and cook 2 minutes longer than the package says, until soft but springy.","Drain and rinse immediately under cold water to wash off the alkaline taste and starchy slime.","Toss with 1 Tbsp oil to loosen — your homemade yellow noodles are ready.","[2. Cumin lamb fried noodles (serves 2)] Marinate: mix the lamb with the marinade and leave for 10 minutes.","Flash-fry the lamb: heat 1 Tbsp oil and stir-fry the lamb on high until about 80% cooked; remove.","Aromatics: add a little oil to the same pan and fry the onion and garlic until the onion is translucent.","Sauce and noodles: pour in the noodle sauce and bring to a boil, then add the noodles and toss on high so they absorb the sauce (about 1 minute).","Combine on high heat: add the water spinach and the lamb and toss quickly on high until the greens wilt and the sauce is absorbed. Serve.","Does the lamb need extra treatment for gaminess, and do you have cumin seeds at home?"],"tips":[]},"g679":{"title":"Scallion Pork Stir-Fry (serves 5)","intro":"Scallion pork stir-fry scaled to 5 servings, keeping the classic savory, rice-friendly flavor and tender texture.","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Sliced pork shoulder (or pork belly)","750 g (about 150 g per person)"],["Green onions","7–8 stalks (cut diagonally; keep whites and greens separate)"],["Garlic","6 cloves (sliced or smashed)"],["Mature ginger","1 small knob (julienned)"],["Large red chili","1–2 (cut diagonally, for color)"]]},{"name":"Pork marinade","items":[["Soy sauce","2.5 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","2.5 Tbsp"],["Potato starch (or cornstarch)","1.5 Tbsp"],["Vegetable oil","1 Tbsp (mixed in last to seal in moisture)"]]},{"name":"Sauce (mix ahead)","items":[["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["Oyster sauce","1.5 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1 tsp"],["Chinese black vinegar","0.5–1 tsp (splashed around the wok edge at the end)"],["White pepper","to taste"],["Water","2 Tbsp"]]}],"steps":["Marinate the pork: mix the pork with the soy sauce and rice wine until absorbed, mix in the starch, then the oil to seal. Marinate for 15 minutes.","Pre-cook the pork: heat a bit more oil than usual and stir-fry the pork on high, separating the slices, until about 80% done (just turned pale). Remove.","Aromatics: leave a little oil in the wok and fry the ginger, garlic and green onion whites on medium until the whites are lightly golden.","Stir-fry on high: turn up the heat and add the pork, green onion greens, red chili and the premixed sauce.","Finish: toss on high for 15 seconds until evenly coated, splash the black vinegar around the edge of the wok, toss twice and serve."],"tips":[]},"g722":{"title":"Taiwanese Lunchbox Braised Chicken Legs (with Spice Packet)","intro":"A complete recipe for Taiwanese lunchbox-style braised chicken legs using a ready-made braising spice packet. The packet saves mixing lots of spices, and gives a more layered flavor with a light herbal aroma.","groups":[{"name":"Overview","items":[["Prep time","10 minutes"],["Cook time","40 minutes (including soaking after the heat is off)"],["Serves","4"]]},{"name":"Full ingredient list","items":[["Main","bone-in chicken legs","4; drumsticks or boneless thighs also work (score the thick parts)"],["Spices","braising spice packet for chicken","1 packet; contains many herbal aromatics, so no need for star anise or five-spice"],["Aromatics","ginger slices","5–6 slices; removes gaminess"],["Sauce","soy sauce","100 ml; the main source of saltiness"]]}],"steps":["[1. Blanch the chicken] Rinse the legs and make 1–2 cuts in the thick meat on the back so the sauce soaks in.","Put the legs in a pot of cold water and heat slowly on low until just simmering and the scum rises (about 5–8 minutes).","Lift out the legs, rinse with clean water and drain.","[2. Aromatics and caramel] Heat 1 Tbsp oil in a pot, add rock sugar and melt it slowly on low until amber and lightly bubbling.","Add the ginger, garlic, green onion (and chili) and fry on medium until fragrant.","Pour in the soy sauce, thick soy sauce and rice wine and bring to a bubbling boil to bring out the soy aroma.","[3. Add the spice packet and braise] Put in the blanched legs skin side down and coat them in the sauce.","Add water (to about 80% of the height of the chicken) and the spice packet; bring to a boil on high.","Once boiling, turn to low, cover and braise for 20–25 minutes.","Turn off the heat and take out the spice packet first (soaking too long makes the herbs bitter).","Put the lid back on and let the legs soak in the residual heat for 20–30 minutes so they absorb the sauce and aroma. Serve."],"tips":[]},"g730":{"title":"Sun-Dried Tomato Beef Pasta (serves 10)","intro":"A sun-dried tomato and beef pasta designed for cooking for 10. The usual problems with big batches are tough beef, mushy pasta or unevenly mixed sauce. This recipe cooks the meat, sauce and pasta separately and combines them at the end, so every bite of beef stays tender and the sauce coats the pasta perfectly.","groups":[{"name":"1. Pasta and beef","items":[["Pasta (penne or spaghetti)","900g–1000g (tube shapes like penne hold rich sauce better and don't break when tossing big batches)"],["Beef (sirloin, flat iron or short rib slices)","1.2 kg–1.5 kg (thick slices or bite-size pieces)"],["Salt","1.5 tsp","beef seasoning"],["Black pepper","to taste","beef seasoning"],["Olive oil","2 Tbsp","beef seasoning"],["Garlic powder","1 tsp (optional)","beef seasoning"]]},{"name":"2. Sauce and aromatic base","items":[["Sun-dried tomatoes","250g–300g (cut into strips; if oil-packed, keep the oil)"],["Canned diced tomatoes","2 cans (about 400g each, for moisture and a sweet-sour base)"],["Tomato paste","4 Tbsp (for richness and color)"],["Garlic","15–20 cloves (sliced or roughly chopped)"],["Onions","2 (finely diced)"],["Chili flakes","1–2 tsp (to taste)"]]},{"name":"3. Seasoning and finishing herbs","items":[["Extra-virgin olive oil","about 8–10 Tbsp"],["Stock (beef or vegetable)","300 ml–400 ml"],["Italian seasoning","2 Tbsp"],["Fresh basil","about 2 handfuls (roughly chopped or torn)"],["Parmesan","100g (grated)"],["Salt and black pepper","to taste"]]}],"steps":["Marinate the beef: cut into slices about 0.5–1 cm thick and mix with the salt, pepper and 2 Tbsp olive oil. Leave 15 minutes.","Sear hot: heat a large deep pot or skillet until very hot. In 2–3 batches, add a little oil and spread the beef out. Sear on high for 1–2 minutes until golden and browned (about medium-rare), then remove immediately. Note: keep the juices that collect on the plate and add them back at the end.","Aromatics: in the same pot (to keep the beef flavor), add 4–5 Tbsp olive oil (or sun-dried tomato oil). Cook the onion on medium until soft and translucent (about 4–5 minutes), then add the garlic and chili flakes and fry for 1 minute.","Tomato paste and sun-dried tomatoes: add 4 Tbsp tomato paste and the sun-dried tomato strips; stir on medium-low for 2 minutes so the concentrated tomato blends with the oil.","Simmer the sauce: add the canned tomatoes, stock and Italian seasoning. Boil on high, then simmer uncovered on low for about 10–15 minutes until thickened. Season with salt and pepper a little stronger than usual (the pasta will be added).","Cook the pasta: bring a big pot of water (at least 6–8 liters) to a boil, add 2 Tbsp salt and the pasta. Cook 2 minutes less than the package says (clearly firm in the center). Save 300 ml of pasta water before draining.","Bring it together (emulsify): tip the drained pasta straight into the sauce, turn the heat to medium-high, add about 150–200 ml pasta water and toss vigorously for 1–2 minutes so the pasta absorbs the sauce and the starch turns it glossy.","Return the beef: turn off the heat (or to very low), add the seared beef with its juices and the fresh basil and toss quickly; the residual heat brings the beef to about medium-well.","[Step 4: Serve] Transfer to large platters, sprinkle evenly with Parmesan and a few torn basil leaves, and serve!","Always sear the beef in batches: 10 servings is a lot of beef. Adding it all at once boils it instead of browning (no Maillard crust). Searing hot in 2–3 batches keeps it juicy.","Pasta choice: for big batches, penne, fusilli or rigatoni coat more evenly than spaghetti and don't clump or turn mushy as quickly.","Use two pots: if you don't have a very large pot, divide the sauce into two pots at step 3 and toss each with half the pasta and beef — much easier!"],"tips":[]},"g757":{"title":"Thai Milk Tea (serves 10)","intro":"For Thai milk tea for ten, the key is to brew a strong tea concentrate first, then balance the milk and sweetness. Thai tea tastes less sweet once iced, so a strong concentrate diluted over lots of ice gives the most authentic taste.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients for 10","items":[["Thai ChaTraMue (Number One brand) tea leaves","100–120 g","about 1.5–2 bowls; the source of the rich tea flavor"],["Water","2,000 ml","for brewing"],["Sweetened condensed milk","250–300 ml","the core of the sweetness and creaminess (about one can)"],["Caster sugar","3–5 Tbsp","adjust to taste"],["Evaporated milk","350 ml","stirred into the tea (about one can)"],["Evaporated milk for topping","100 ml","poured over the ice at the end for layers"],["Ice","lots (about 2–3 big bags)","fill each glass to the top"]]}],"steps":["Brew the strong tea: bring 2,000 ml water to a boil, turn off the heat, add 100–120 g tea leaves, cover and steep for 6–8 minutes. Strain thoroughly through a tea strainer or tea sock to get a deep orange-red tea.","Mix the milk tea: while the tea is very hot, add the condensed milk, sugar and 350 ml evaporated milk and stir with a long spoon until everything is dissolved.","Cool: let it cool to room temperature, then chill in the fridge if you can (the flavors blend better when chilled).","Serve: fill 10 large glasses to the top with ice and pour in the milk tea to about 80% full.","Top: drizzle about 10 ml evaporated milk over the ice in each glass for the classic Thai layered look, and enjoy."],"tips":[]},"g844":{"title":"Japanese Curry with Curry Powder Only","intro":"Absolutely possible! What sets Japanese curry apart from Thai or Indian curry is its thick texture and gentle sweetness. A curry roux block is basically curry powder + fat + flour (for thickness) + seasonings and stock. With the curry powder you have, a simple homemade roux and something sweet, you can make the same rich Japanese flavor as the blocks.","groups":[{"name":"Before or while cooking, make the roux first","items":[["Ratio","2 Tbsp butter (or oil) : 2 Tbsp cake / all-purpose flour : 1~1.5 Tbsp curry powder"]]},{"name":"Thicken and season","items":[["Turn the heat to very low or off and stir the curry roux into the liquid until fully dissolved",""],["Add the grated apple",""],["honey",""],["ketchup and soy sauce",""],["Reduce","simmer on low for 3~5 more minutes so the roux thickens the curry into a smooth sauce"]]}],"steps":["Make the roux: melt the butter on low, add the flour and cook very gently for 3~5 minutes until it turns light brown and smells slightly nutty (keep the heat low the whole time — don't burn it).","Add the curry powder: stir it in quickly for about 30 seconds until very fragrant, then turn off the heat (you can season it with a little soy sauce or sugar now).","Brown: brown the meat and onions (cook the onions as dark as you can), then add the carrots and potatoes and stir-fry.","Simmer: add water or stock, boil on high, then cover and simmer on low for about 15~20 minutes until everything is tender."],"tips":["Japanese curry is all about sweetness and depth. Without the MSG and fruit purée in curry blocks, you can make up for it with:","Caramelized onions (essential): slice the onions and cook on medium-low until deep brown — this is the main base of Japanese curry's sweetness and richness.","Natural sweetener (pick one): grated apple (half an apple) or honey (1~2 tsp)","Richness and depth (optional): ketchup (1 Tbsp, for tang and umami), soy sauce (1 Tbsp, for savory depth), a small piece of dark chocolate or a little unsweetened cocoa (instantly deepens the color and adds bittersweet depth), milk or cream (a little at the end for a smoother texture)","Curry made this way tastes more natural than store-bought blocks, and you can adjust the sweetness and heat however you like!"]},"g876":{"title":"Taiwanese Popcorn Chicken Marinade (2 kg)","intro":"For 2 kg (2000 g) of Taiwanese popcorn chicken (yansuji), the marinade ratio is everything! Use boneless thigh (tender and juicy) or breast (firmer — cut the pieces a little bigger).","groups":[{"name":"1. Sauces and liquids","items":[["Light soy sauce","6 Tbsp (about 90 ml)"],["Rice wine","4 Tbsp (about 60 ml)"],["Thick soy sauce (or oyster sauce)","2 Tbsp (about 30 ml, for sweetness and cling)"],["Sesame oil","1.5 Tbsp (about 22 ml)"]]},{"name":"2. Aromatics and spices","items":[["Garlic","10～12 cloves (crushed or very finely minced, about 40～50g — needed for a strong garlic flavor)"],["Grated ginger","1 tsp (about 5g)"],["White pepper","1.5 Tbsp (about 10g)"],["Five-spice powder","1 Tbsp (about 6g — the core of the Taiwanese flavor)"],["Caster sugar","2 Tbsp (about 25g)"],["Licorice powder (optional)","1/2 tsp (an old southern-Taiwan secret that adds a sweet aftertaste)"]]},{"name":"3. For tenderness and juiciness","items":[["Egg yolks","2 (or 1 whole egg — makes the meat silkier and helps the coating stick)"],["Potato starch / cornstarch","2 Tbsp (locks in juices and helps the marinade cling)"]]}],"steps":["Cut the 2 kg chicken into bite-size pieces about 3 cm square. After rinsing, pat completely dry with paper towels so the marinade can soak in.","Mix all the liquids, aromatics, white pepper, five-spice and sugar together.","Pour over the chicken and massage with your hands for about 3～5 minutes until the chicken absorbs the marinade.","Add the egg yolks and 2 Tbsp starch and mix again to seal in the juices.","Cover with cling film and marinate in the fridge for at least 4 hours (overnight, 12～24 hours, is best).","Recommended coating: coarse sweet-potato starch (or coarse tapioca starch).","Coat the marinated chicken in batches with the coarse starch, pressing gently so it sticks.","Let it rest 5～10 minutes until the coating looks damp (white turns darker) — this stops it falling off in the oil and makes it crispy.","First fry (to cook): heat the oil to 160°C～170°C and fry the chicken in batches for about 3～4 minutes until set and cooked (about 80-90% done). Remove.","Second fry (to crisp): raise the oil to 190°C～200°C and fry all the chicken again on high for 30～45 seconds until golden and crisp; drain immediately.","The finishing touch: 10 seconds before the second fry ends, toss in a handful of washed and thoroughly dried Thai basil — the aroma is amazing!"],"tips":[]},"g1077":{"title":"Earl Grey Milk Tea","intro":"For a fragrant, not-watery Earl Grey milk tea at home, the keys are a high tea-to-water ratio and brewing a strong tea first, then adding warm milk. This extracts the bergamot aroma and tea flavor fully without the milk drowning it.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Earl Grey tea leaves / bags","5–6g (or 2 tea bags)"],["Hot water","150 ml (about 90–95°C)"],["Whole fresh milk","200 ml"],["Sugar (optional)","5–10g (raw cane sugar, brown sugar or honey add depth)"]]}],"steps":["Warm the mug and brew strong tea: rinse the mug with hot water and pour it out. Add 150 ml hot water and 5–6g Earl Grey leaves or 2 tea bags, cover and steep 4–5 minutes. Tip: milk tea needs a stronger base, so steep 1–2 minutes longer than for plain tea — but don't squeeze the bags, or it releases too much bitterness.","Warm the milk: microwave 200 ml whole milk for 30–40 seconds (or warm in a small pan on low) to about 60°C (just warm to the touch — don't boil, or it forms a skin and tastes scorched).","Combine and sweeten: remove the tea bags, stir in the sugar until dissolved, then slowly pour in the warm milk and stir.","[Flavor upgrades] Stovetop method (advanced): if you have time, boil 150 ml water, add the tea and simmer on low for 2 minutes, then pour in 200 ml cold milk and heat on low until tiny bubbles appear (about 60°C). Turn off the heat, cover for 3 minutes, then strain. The milk and tea blend even better this way.","Milk foam: if you have a small milk frother, froth some of the warm milk and spoon it on top for an easy English-style milk tea.","Do you usually prefer it unsweetened, or a little sweet to bring out the citrus aroma?"],"tips":[]},"g1097":{"title":"Taiwanese Grilled Corn Glaze","intro":"The rich, savory soul of Taiwanese night-market grilled corn is a thick sauce of shacha sauce, sesame paste (or peanut butter) and thick soy sauce. As it grills, it clings to the corn and forms an irresistibly fragrant glaze.","groups":[{"name":"Glaze ingredients","items":[["Shacha sauce (Taiwanese BBQ sauce)","2 Tbsp","savory, seafood-like base"],["Unsweetened sesame paste (or smooth peanut butter)","1 Tbsp","rich nutty aroma and thickness so it clings (the key ingredient)"],["Thick soy sauce (or vegetarian oyster sauce)","2 Tbsp","sweet-salty flavor and body"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp","more saltiness and soy aroma"],["Grated garlic","1 Tbsp","flavor (adjust to taste; omit for vegetarian)"],["Sugar / maltose","1 Tbsp","sweetness and a beautiful caramel shine once grilled"],["Five-spice powder","1/4 tsp","the classic night-market spice"],["White pepper","1/4 tsp","a gentle peppery kick"],["Water","1～2 Tbsp","to adjust thickness (keep it thick enough to brush on heavily)"],["Sesame oil or lard","1 tsp","shine and aroma"]]}],"steps":["Make the glaze: stir everything together in a small bowl. If the sesame paste or peanut butter is stiff, loosen it with a little warm water first, then add the rest.","Pre-cook the corn: steam (in the husk) or boil the corn (waxy or white corn is best) until about 80% cooked, then pat dry.","Score the kernels (the secret): scratch or poke the kernels with a fork or skewer so the glaze soaks in and sticks.","Glaze and grill in stages: Stage 1: brush with a thin layer of butter or sesame oil and grill until the surface is slightly dry. Stage 2: brush evenly with the glaze and grill over charcoal, in an air fryer (180°C) or oven (200°C) for about 3～5 minutes. Stage 3: turn, brush on another thick layer and grill until the glaze bubbles, chars slightly and smells amazing."],"tips":[]},"g1100":{"title":"Corn and Chicken Soup (Tatung Cooker)","intro":"Making corn chicken soup in a Tatung electric cooker is very easy, and because the steam heats it gently and evenly, the chicken comes out especially tender and the broth very clear!","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Bone-in chicken (legs or chopped pieces)","about 500 g"],["Sweet corn","1–2 cobs (cut into sections)"],["Carrot","half (roll-cut; optional, for sweetness and color)"],["Mature ginger","3–4 slices"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Water","about 1200–1500 ml (just to cover)"],["Salt","to taste"]]}],"steps":["Blanch the chicken (key step): put the chicken in cold water, heat on low until the scum rises, then lift out and rinse clean. Drain.","Fill the inner pot: put the blanched chicken, corn, carrot and ginger in the inner pot, add water to cover (about 80–90% full) and 1 Tbsp rice wine.","Water in the outer pot: add 1.5 cups water to the outer pot (about 30–40 minutes) and press the switch.","Rest and season: when the switch pops up, don't open it — let it sit for 15–20 minutes so the chicken gets more tender and flavorful. Then open, season with salt and serve."],"tips":["Don't salt while cooking: add the salt only after the switch pops up and it has rested, so the chicken doesn't tighten and dry out.","For a richer broth: if you want more corn flavor, add half a cup of water to the outer pot and steam a second time — the broth gets sweeter."]},"g1109":{"title":"Red Bean Soup (Pressure Cooker / Tatung Cooker)","intro":"Pressure cookers and Tatung cookers each have their strengths for red bean soup — it depends on the texture you like and how you cook:","groups":[{"name":"Which cooker?","items":[["Pressure cooker","for soft, sandy beans and a thick soup. High heat and pressure break down the bean skins fast — no long soaking, and 20～25 minutes gives a very creamy texture"],["Tatung cooker","for whole, separate beans and a lighter soup. Gentle steaming keeps the beans intact but soft inside, with no watching and no burning"]]},{"name":"Classic red bean soup ratio","items":[["Adzuki (red) beans","300g (about 2 rice cups)","choose plump, bright red beans"],["Water","1500 ~ 1800 ml","bean to water about 1:5 (rich) to 1:6 (light)"],["Dark brown sugar","80g","cane aroma and deep flavor"],["Raw cane sugar or rock sugar","70g","clean sweetness"],["Salt","1/8 tsp (a pinch)","the key — reduces beany taste and brings out the sweetness"]]}],"steps":["Rinse: wash the beans, pick out bad ones and debris, and drain (no soaking needed).","Into the pot: put the beans and 1500ml water in the pressure cooker.","Pressure: lock the lid and heat on high until it reaches pressure (whistle or pin up), then turn to low and cook for 20 minutes.","Release and sweeten: turn off the heat and let the pressure release naturally. Open and check the beans have split and are soft, add the brown sugar, cane sugar and salt, and simmer uncovered on low for 3～5 minutes until the sugar dissolves.","[Tatung method] Prep (freezer trick): rinse and drain the beans, then freeze them for at least 2 hours (ice crystals break down the structure, so they soften fast without soaking).","Water: inner pot — beans and 1500ml water. Outer pot — 2 rice cups of water.","First steaming: press the switch; when it pops up, don't open the lid — let it rest 20 minutes.","Second steaming: add 1 more rice cup of water to the outer pot and cook again; when it pops up, rest another 15～20 minutes.","Season: once a bean crushes easily between your fingers, gently stir in the brown sugar, cane sugar and salt, add 0.5 rice cup of water to the outer pot and steam once more so the sugar soaks in."],"tips":[]},"g1150":{"title":"Pumpkin Congee (serves 7, Tatung Cooker)","intro":"For 7 servings of pumpkin congee, the keys are the capacity of your cooker's inner pot and the rice-to-water ratio. Tatung cookers usually come in 10-person (or 11-person) sizes; if yours is 10-person or larger, 7 servings in one go is no problem!","groups":[{"name":"🥣 Ingredients for 7","items":[["White rice","2.5～3 rice cups (2.5 for thinner, 3 for thicker)"],["Pumpkin","about 600～800g (most of a medium pumpkin, cut into chunks)"],["With 2.5 cups rice ➔ add 15～17 rice cups of water (about 1","6～1:7 ratio)"],["With","3 cups rice ➔ add 18～20 rice cups of water"],["Water in the outer pot","1.5～2 rice cups"]]}],"steps":["Rinse the rice and cut the pumpkin: rinse and drain the rice. Seed the pumpkin and cut into chunks (for a big batch, about 2 cm pieces so they cook through evenly).","Assemble: put the rice and pumpkin in the inner pot and add the matching amount of water. Prevent boil-overs (very important): a big batch of congee foams up easily, so add 3～5 drops of cooking oil to the inner pot — it really stops the starchy water from bubbling over!","Cook: pour 1.5～2 cups of water into the outer pot (more water outside makes sure the rice cooks through when the batch is big). Put in the inner pot, cover and press the switch.","Stir and serve: open the lid, mash the pumpkin with a large ladle and stir well from the bottom up so the pumpkin's sweetness blends into the congee."],"tips":["When the switch pops up, do NOT open it right away!","Keep it plugged in and covered for another 20～25 minutes. The rice absorbs the water and thickens naturally, and the pumpkin softens.","About capacity: if yours is a small 6-person cooker, the inner pot may not hold 7 servings of congee (too much water will boil over badly). Cook it in two batches, or use a large deep pot on the stove.","Extra flavor: if you like a hint of salt to bring out sweetness, add half a teaspoon of salt at the end — the pumpkin's natural sweetness will shine!"]},"g1110":{"title":"Taiwanese Street-Stall Meatball Soup (White Pepper and Napa Cabbage)","intro":"This recreates that familiar bowl from Taiwanese food stalls. The trick is frying simple aromatics to bring out fried-shallot and white-pepper flavor — light and sweet but with a kick.","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Beef meatballs","8 to 10"],["Napa cabbage","2 to 3 leaves (washed, cut into large pieces)"],["Cilantro","1 small handful (chopped)"]]},{"name":"Aromatics and seasoning","items":[["Garlic","2 cloves (chopped)"],["Shallots","2 (sliced; if you don't have any, use a little store-bought fried shallot)"],["White pepper","1 tsp (the core flavor — freshly ground is best)"],["Salt","to taste"],["Sesame oil","a few drops"],["Water","about 700 to 800 ml"]]}],"steps":["Fry the aromatics: heat a little oil and gently fry the garlic and shallots (or fried shallots) on low until lightly golden with that classic food-stall fried-shallot smell.","Add water and cabbage: pour in the water and add the cabbage. Bring to a boil on high, then simmer on low for about 3 to 5 minutes until the cabbage is soft and sweet.","Add the meatballs: most beef meatballs are already cooked, so about 3 minutes is enough — until they all float and are hot in the center.","Season and the all-important pepper: season with salt. Just before turning off the heat, sprinkle in the white pepper and a few drops of sesame oil and stir gently.","Serve: ladle into bowls and top generously with chopped fresh cilantro."],"tips":[]},"g1166":{"title":"Old-Style Garlic Chicken Thick Soup with Bonito (400 g)","intro":"So it's the old-style version with a strong bonito aroma! This is a classic in northern Taiwan and old streets like Shilin Night Market and Dadaocheng. The soul of a traditional bonito thick soup is the smoky sweetness of bonito flakes, a hint of shacha and a punch of raw garlic — wonderfully layered.","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Chicken breast","400g (cut into strips, marinated as before)"],["Black wood ear mushroom","1–2 (shredded)"],["Shredded bamboo shoots","2/3 bowl (key to the old-style crunch)"],["Raw grated garlic — the soul","2.5–3 Tbsp (added at the end — double the garlic flavor!)"],["Eggs","2 (beaten, for fine egg ribbons)"],["Bonito flakes for serving","a little (sprinkled on top)"]]},{"name":"Soup seasoning","items":[["Soy sauce (or thick soy sauce)","2 Tbsp"],["Chinese black vinegar","3 Tbsp"],["Rock sugar","1.5 tsp"],["White pepper","1 tsp (bonito soup needs plenty of pepper)"],["Salt","to taste"]]},{"name":"Thickening and garnish","items":[["Starch slurry","3 Tbsp potato starch + 4 Tbsp water"],["Chopped cilantro / celery","a little"],["Sesame oil","a few drops"]]}],"steps":["[1. Bonito stock (the essential first step)] Bring 1500 ml water and the mushroom-soaking water to a boil.","Turn off the heat and steep a big handful of bonito flakes for about 3–5 minutes (don't boil too long or it turns bitter).","Strain out the flakes and keep the smoky, rich bonito stock.","[2. Old-style aromatic base] Heat oil, fry the chopped shallots until lightly golden, then add the shredded mushrooms and garlic and fry until fragrant.","Add 1 Tbsp shacha sauce and stir gently on low until fragrant (shacha gives the bonito broth more body).","[3. Soup and thickening] Pour in the bonito stock and add the bamboo shoots and wood ear.","Season with the soy sauce, rock sugar and white pepper; boil on high, then simmer on low for 5 minutes so the flavors combine.","Turn to high and add the 400g marinated chicken, separating the pieces; cook about 2 minutes until just white and cooked through.","Turn to low and drizzle in the starch slurry in stages, stirring gently, until the soup is smooth and thick.","[4. Egg ribbons and double garlic] Turn off the heat, pour the 2 beaten eggs over a spoon from a height in a circle, wait 5 seconds, then gently push to make fine egg ribbons.","Stir in the raw grated garlic and 3 Tbsp black vinegar.","[5. Serve] Ladle into bowls, sprinkle with cilantro, add a few drops of sesame oil, and finally top with a pinch of fresh bonito flakes!"],"tips":["Bonito flakes: a big handful (about 15–20g, for the stock)","Mushroom-soaking water + water/stock: 1500 ml total","Dried shiitake mushrooms: 5–6 (soaked, shredded; keep the soaking water)","Chopped shallots (or fried shallots): 2 Tbsp","Minced garlic: 4 cloves (for frying)","Shacha sauce: 1 Tbsp (the hidden secret of bonito thick soup — it ties the bonito and garlic together perfectly)","Bonito two ways: steeping draws out the clean, sweet bonito flavor for the stock, and a handful of dry flakes on top at the table will “dance” in the steam and add a lovely bonito bite!","Shacha as the finishing touch: a bonito stock with only soy sauce can taste thin; frying 1 Tbsp shacha sauce makes the broth as deep as an old-time food stall's."]},"g1175":{"title":"Taiwanese Garlic Chicken Thick Soup","intro":"This garlic chicken thick soup is packed with garlic flavor, with a savory broth and silky chicken — satisfying with rice or on its own! It's actually very easy to make at home. Here's the golden-ratio recipe:","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Chicken breast or thigh","300g (cut into small pieces or strips)"],["Black wood ear mushroom","1 (shredded)"],["Shredded bamboo shoots","half a bowl (optional, for crunch)"],["Shiitake mushrooms","3–4 (soaked, shredded)"],["Garlic powder or minced garlic","3 cloves for frying; 2 Tbsp grated garlic (the soul of the dish — added last!)"],["Egg","1 (beaten, for egg ribbons)"]]},{"name":"Chicken marinade","items":[["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["White pepper","1/2 tsp"],["Rice wine","1 tsp"],["Potato starch (or sweet-potato starch)","1 Tbsp (key to locking in juices)"]]},{"name":"Soup seasoning","items":[["Stock (or water)","1200 ml"],["Soy sauce","1.5 Tbsp"],["Chinese black vinegar","2 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1 tsp"],["White pepper","1/2 tsp"],["Salt","to taste"]]},{"name":"Thickening and finishing aromatics","items":[["Starch slurry","2 Tbsp potato starch + 3 Tbsp water (mixed)"],["Chopped cilantro / celery","a little"],["Sesame oil","a few drops"]]}],"steps":["[1. Prep and marinate the chicken] Cut the chicken into bite-size strips or pieces.","Mix with the soy sauce, white pepper and rice wine, then the starch, and marinate about 15 minutes.","[2. Aromatics and broth] Heat oil and fry the minced garlic and shiitake until fragrant.","Pour in the stock (or water), add the wood ear and bamboo shoots, boil on high, then simmer on medium-low for about 5 minutes so the ingredients release their sweetness.","Season with the soy sauce, sugar and white pepper.","[3. Chicken and thickening] Turn to high and drop in the marinated chicken piece by piece (so they don't stick together); cook about 2–3 minutes until done.","Turn to low and pour in the starch slurry in stages, stirring, until as thick as you like.","[4. Egg ribbons and the “soul” garlic] Turn off the heat (or very low), pour the beaten egg over a spoon from a height in a circle, wait 5 seconds, then gently stir for beautiful silky egg ribbons.","The key finish: stir in the fresh grated garlic and black vinegar gently, and it's done!","[5. Serve] Ladle into bowls, sprinkle with cilantro (or celery) and add a few drops of fragrant sesame oil!"],"tips":["The soul is the garlic: always keep some raw grated garlic to add when the heat is off! Garlic cooked in the broth turns sweet; raw garlic at the end gives that sharp, clear garlic flavor typical of Taiwanese thick soups.","Silky chicken: don't skip the starch marinade; make sure the soup is hot and work fast — cook the chicken, then thicken right away, and it won't toughen.","Adjusting sourness: black vinegar loses its aroma at high heat, so add it at the end. If you like it more sour, add a few drops to your bowl."]},"g1177":{"title":"Taiwanese-Style Buffalo Wings (Ninja Air Fryer, °F)","intro":"Ninja air fryers have strong airflow and heat up very fast, so the cooking time is shorter than in an oven and the results are crispier! Here's a recipe adjusted for a Ninja 1750W air fryer in Fahrenheit:","groups":[{"name":"1. Chicken and seasoning","items":[["Chicken wings, cut (flats + drumettes)","about 600 g (about 10–12 pieces)"],["Salt","1/2 tsp"],["Black pepper","1/2 tsp"],["Garlic powder","1/2 tsp"],["Aluminum-free baking powder","1 tsp (the secret to crispness! don't substitute baking soda)"]]},{"name":"2. Classic Buffalo sauce","items":[["American hot sauce (Frank's RedHot)","60 ml"],["Unsalted butter","40 g (melted)"],["Honey or maple syrup","1 tsp"],["Lemon juice or white vinegar","1 tsp (optional)"],["Grated garlic","1/2 tsp"]]}],"steps":["Dry them: pat the wings completely dry with paper towels.","Season and coat: mix the salt, pepper, garlic powder and baking powder and sprinkle evenly over the wings, tossing to coat.","Stage 1 (render the fat): preheat the air fryer to 360°F. Lay the wings skin side up in the basket (try not to overlap). Air-fry at 360°F for 12 minutes.","Stage 2 (crisp at high heat): take them out and flip every wing (or shake the basket). Raise to 390°F–400°F and air-fry another 6–8 minutes until golden and very crisp.","Melt the butter in a small pan on very low heat, turn off the heat and quickly whisk in the hot sauce, honey, garlic and lemon juice.","Put the hot, crispy wings in a large bowl, pour over the sauce and toss quickly so every wing is coated in the glossy red, tangy-spicy sauce!","[💡 Ninja air fryer tips] Little or no preheating: Ninja air fryers heat up very fast, so 1–2 minutes of preheating is enough.","Cook in batches: for a bigger batch, don't pile them up — air needs to get through. Two batches will be much crispier than one packed basket!"],"tips":[]},"g1228":{"title":"Bitter Melon Beef Stir-Fry (350 g)","intro":"Scaled to 350 g of beef (1.75 times the original 200 g recipe), the ingredients and seasonings are adjusted as follows:","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Beef","350g"],["Bitter melon","1.5 ~ 2 (use 2 if you love bitter melon)"],["Light soy sauce","1.75 Tbsp (about 1 Tbsp + 2 tsp)"],["Cooking wine","1.75 Tbsp"],["Oyster sauce","1.75 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1 tsp"],["Starch","1.75 tsp (nearly 2 tsp)"],["Cooking oil","1.5 ~ 2 Tbsp (to seal in moisture)"],["Garlic","5~6 cloves (sliced or smashed)"],["Fermented black beans","1.5 ~ 2 Tbsp"],["Light soy sauce for the stir-fry","1.5 Tbsp"]]}],"steps":["Fry the beef in batches or on very high heat: with more beef, the pan cools quickly and the meat releases water. Use a little more oil and a very hot pan, or flash-fry in two batches and remove, so the beef stays tender and juicy.","Salt for removing bitterness: with 2 bitter melons, use 1 tsp salt to draw out the bitterness. After it releases water, always rinse with clean water and squeeze dry, so the dish doesn't end up too salty."],"tips":[]},"g1238":{"title":"Tainan Mackerel Thick Soup + Fried Fish (Jordan Ingredients Version)","intro":"The soul of Tainan Spanish mackerel thick soup is the sweet-and-slightly-sour broth and the crispy, strongly seasoned fried fish. The Tainan way uses dried flatfish and napa cabbage for natural sweetness, finished with black vinegar.","groups":[{"name":"[Ingredients]","items":[["Fish","400 g Spanish mackerel fillet (or kanaad / Nile perch fillet)"]]},{"name":"Marinade","items":[["Grated garlic","2 Tbsp (Tainan style needs plenty of garlic)"],["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["Rice wine (or white vinegar / lemon juice, to remove fishiness)","1 Tbsp"],["Five-spice powder","1/2 tsp"],["White pepper","1/2 tsp"],["Sugar","1 Tbsp"],["Sesame oil","1 tsp"],["Coating","coarse sweet-potato starch, as needed"]]},{"name":"[Ingredients]","items":[["Dried flatfish","4～5 pieces (the essence of Tainan flavor; if unavailable, use 1 Tbsp dried shrimp)"],["Dried shiitake mushrooms","3～4 (soaked, shredded)"],["Shallots","3 (sliced)"]]},{"name":"Soup vegetables","items":[["Napa cabbage","half a head (large pieces — Tainan thick soup cooks it very soft for maximum sweetness)"],["Minced garlic","1 Tbsp"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Water or stock","1500 ml"],["Keep the mushroom-soaking water",""],["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["Sugar","2.5～3 Tbsp (Tainan flavor is on the sweet side; adjust to taste)"],["Salt","1/2 tsp"],["White pepper","1 tsp"]]},{"name":"Thickening and finishing","items":[["Starch slurry (potato starch","3 Tbsp + 3 Tbsp water)"],["Kong Yen black vinegar (or other black vinegar)","2～3 Tbsp"],["Cilantro",""],["Sesame oil","a little"]]},{"name":"Season and thicken","items":[["Add the soy sauce",""],["sugar",""],["salt",""],["and white pepper",""],["Keep it at a gentle simmer and drizzle in the starch slurry in stages, stirring, until smooth and thick",""]]}],"steps":["Prep and marinate: rinse the fish, pat it completely dry and cut into large thick pieces about 3×4 cm. Mix all the marinade ingredients, toss with the fish, cover and marinate in the fridge for at least 2 hours (overnight is even better).","Coat and rest: coat the fish evenly in coarse sweet-potato starch, press lightly and leave 5 minutes until the coating looks slightly damp, so it doesn't fall off when frying.","First fry: heat oil to about 160°C (small bubbles form around a wooden chopstick), fry the fish in batches on medium for about 3～4 minutes until cooked and set. Drain.","Second fry: raise the oil to 180°C and fry the fish again on high for 30 seconds until golden and crisp. Remove.","[2. Tainan-style thick soup] Flatfish and aromatics: heat 2 Tbsp oil, fry the dried flatfish on low until golden and fragrant (don't burn it), then remove and crush. In the same oil, fry the shallots, shiitake and garlic until fragrant.","Soften the cabbage: add the napa cabbage and cook until soft, then add the crushed flatfish and stir until fragrant.","Simmer: add the water (or stock) and mushroom water, boil on high, then cover and simmer on low for about 15～20 minutes until the cabbage is completely soft and sweet.","Just before turning off the heat, drizzle black vinegar around the edge of the pot.","Ladle the soup into bowls, top with the freshly fried fish, then sprinkle with cilantro and a little white pepper. Enjoy!"],"tips":[]},"g1255":{"title":"Delicious Salmon, Four Ways","intro":"There are only two keys to great salmon: crispy skin and juicy flesh that doesn't dry out. Here are 4 classic, nearly foolproof methods — from pan-seared to baked, all easy and top-notch:","groups":[{"name":"The sweet-salty sauce balances salmon's rich oils perfectly.","items":[["Golden sauce ratio","soy sauce : mirin : sake (or rice wine) : sugar = 2 : 2 : 2 : 1"]]}],"steps":["[1. Pan-seared salmon] The key: the skin must be completely dry.","After thawing, pat the salmon completely dry with paper towels (if you have time, salt it lightly and leave it uncovered in the fridge for half an hour — the skin gets even crispier).","Make two cuts in the skin so it doesn't curl, and season both sides with salt and pepper.","Heat a little oil and put the salmon in skin side down; cook on medium for about 3–4 minutes (press the fish gently with a spatula for 10 seconds so the skin stays flat against the pan).","Flip and cook on low for 1–2 minutes. Before turning off the heat, add a knob of butter, smashed garlic and rosemary, and keep spooning the melted butter over the salmon. Serve right away.","[2. Japanese teriyaki salmon (great with rice)] Season both sides with a little salt and pepper and dust lightly with flour (locks in juices and helps the sauce stick).","Pan-fry both sides until about 70-80% cooked.","Pour in the teriyaki sauce and reduce on medium-low, spooning the sauce over the fish until thick and glossy.","[3. French salmon en papillote (foolproof, keeps it juicy)] If you're not sure about heat control and worry about drying the fish out, baking in paper never fails.","Lay out a sheet of baking paper and put sliced onion, zucchini or cherry tomatoes on the bottom.","Put the salmon on top and add salt, pepper, minced garlic, a little butter and a few lemon slices.","Pour over a spoonful of white wine (or rice wine) and fold the paper tightly around the edges to seal.","Bake in a 200°C preheated oven for about 15 minutes. When you open it, the vegetables' sweetness and the salmon juices are all sealed in — incredibly tender.","[4. Miso saikyo-yaki (rich and elegant)] Marinade: 2 Tbsp white miso, 1 Tbsp mirin, 1 Tbsp sake, 0.5 Tbsp sugar.","Mix and spread over the salmon; marinate in the fridge for half a day to overnight.","Before cooking, gently wipe off the excess miso (otherwise it burns easily).","Bake in the oven or air fryer (about 180°C) for 10–12 minutes until beautifully golden and caramelized.","Chef's tip: the worst thing for salmon is overcooking. It's best when the center is still slightly pink; for sashimi-grade salmon, cook to 70-80% and let the residual heat finish it — the texture is amazing."],"tips":[]},"g1261":{"title":"Bitter Melon with Salted Egg (half a bitter melon)","intro":"Here's the recipe scaled precisely for half a bitter melon (one person or a small family meal):","groups":[{"name":"📋 Ingredients (½ bitter melon version)","items":[["White bitter melon","1/2 (about 150g–200g)"],["Salted duck egg","1 (yolk and white prepared separately)"],["Garlic","2 cloves (chopped)"],["Green onion","1/2 stalk (chopped)"],["Red chili","a little (sliced, for color; optional)"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Rice wine","1/2 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1/4 tsp"],["White pepper","a pinch"],["Cooking oil","about 1 Tbsp (for frying the yolk)"],["Sesame oil","a few drops (just before serving)"]]}],"steps":["Cut the bitter melon in half lengthwise and scrape out all the white pith with a metal spoon.","Slice about 0.3–0.5 cm thick.","Blanch to reduce bitterness: bring a small pot of water to a boil with a little salt and oil, blanch the slices for about 1 minute, then drain.","Separate the yolk and white of the salted egg.","Salted yolk: mash it into a fine paste with a spoon.","Salted white: chop finely (start with half the white so it's not too salty; add more to taste).","Heat about 1 Tbsp oil on low and add the mashed yolk.","Keep stirring the yolk until it foams into fine golden bubbles and smells fragrant (“golden sand”).","Still on low, add the garlic and white part of the green onion and stir into the golden sand.","Turn to medium, add the drained bitter melon and chopped egg white and toss quickly so the golden sand coats the melon.","Splash 1/2 Tbsp rice wine around the edge of the pan, add 1/4 tsp sugar and a pinch of white pepper and toss.","Just before serving, add the green onion and chili, drizzle a few drops of sesame oil, toss twice and plate!","💡 Reminder: with a half batch there's less food, so the pan heats fast — keep the heat at its lowest when frying the salted yolk so it doesn't burn!"],"tips":[]},"g1269":{"title":"Dipping Sauces for Taiwanese Lamb Hot Pot","intro":"Here are the 3 most classic dipping sauces for Taiwanese lamb hot pot: the authentic fermented-tofu bean sauce, a sweet garlic soy sauce, and a spicy sesame oil sauce. The first is the standard at famous lamb hot pot shops across Taiwan — the fermented aroma of the tofu perfectly sets off the sweetness of the lamb.","groups":[{"name":"1. Fermented tofu sauce","items":[["Spicy fermented tofu (or plain)","2 cubes"],["Spicy chili bean paste (doubanjiang)","1 tsp"],["Thick soy sauce","1 tsp"],["Sugar or powdered rock sugar","half a tsp"],["Broth from the hot pot","1～2 Tbsp (the key! dissolves the seasonings)"]]},{"name":"2. Garlic soy sauce","items":[["Garlic","3～4 cloves (crushed)"],["Fresh chili","half (chopped, to taste)"],["Thick soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["Black or white vinegar","half a tsp (cuts the richness)"],["Sesame oil","1 tsp"],["Chopped green onion","a little"]]},{"name":"3. Spicy sesame oil sauce","items":[["Spicy chili bean paste","1 Tbsp"],["Thick soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Toasted sesame oil","1 tsp"],["Toasted white sesame seeds","a little"],["Green onion / cilantro","to taste"]]}],"steps":["Fermented tofu sauce: mash the tofu in a small bowl with a spoon, add the other seasonings, then stir in 1～2 Tbsp hot broth from the hot pot until smooth.","Garlic soy sauce: just mix everything together.","Spicy sesame oil sauce: mix the sauce, then sprinkle with sesame seeds and green onion."],"tips":["If you're not used to fermented tofu, the garlicky sauce is excellent at cutting gaminess and richness."]},"g1288":{"title":"Easy Beef Fried Noodles (Soy Sauce + Mirin)","intro":"No problem! With just soy sauce and mirin you can make great Japanese-style fried noodles (like yakiniku / shoyu yakisoba). Mirin brings sweetness and a hint of wine, and with soy sauce it's the classic golden pairing — sweet, savory and delicious!","groups":[{"name":"🛒 Ingredients","items":[["Noodles","yellow noodles, cooked udon or cooked ramen / pasta, about 2 servings"],["Meat","the mildly marinated beef belly slices (about 150-200g)"],["Cabbage / water spinach / bok choy","1 handful"],["Onion","1/4 (sliced — highly recommended; its sweetness goes perfectly with soy and mirin!)"],["Green onion or garlic","a little (optional)"]]},{"name":"🥣 Sauce (mix before cooking)","items":[["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["Mirin","2 Tbsp"],["Water","3 Tbsp (about 45ml)"]]}],"steps":["Fry the beef: heat 1 Tbsp oil and stir-fry the marinated beef on high until 70-80% cooked (mostly changed color). Remove.","Vegetables: in the oil left from the beef, fry the onion until soft and fragrant, then add the cabbage / bok choy and toss a few times.","Add the sauce: pour in the soy sauce + mirin + water and bring to a boil on high.","Simmer the noodles: add the noodles and loosen them with chopsticks so they soak up the sauce evenly. Cover and cook on medium for about 1-2 minutes so the sweet soy flavor soaks in.","Return the beef: uncover, add the beef back and toss on high for 10-15 seconds until coated and the sauce has reduced slightly. Serve!"],"tips":["With only two seasonings, the ratio is simple (soy sauce : mirin : water = 2 : 2 : 3):","(If you have white pepper, a tiny bit in the sauce is nice; if not, just skip it!)","Onion is the key: because the seasoning is so simple, the natural sweetness of softened onion makes the sauce much better.","Use the beef belly fat: the meat was marinated with a little oil and soy sauce, and the fat and flavor left in the pan after frying it are the best natural stock!"]},"g1291":{"title":"Beef Belly Marinades for Fried Noodles (Two Versions)","intro":"","groups":[{"name":"1. Mild home-style (few aromatics, lets the beef and soy shine)","items":[["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp","base flavor and soy aroma for the noodles"],["Rice wine (or sake)","1 tsp","removes gaminess, adds aroma"],["White pepper","1/8 tsp (a small pinch)","subtly removes gaminess without overpowering"],["Sugar","1/2 tsp","softens the saltiness, helps browning"],["Water","1 Tbsp","adds moisture so the slices don't dry out"],["Potato starch (or cornstarch)","1 tsp","locks in juices for a silky texture"],["Vegetable oil","1/2 tsp","added last; seals in moisture and helps the slices separate in the pan"],["Grated garlic","1 tsp (about 2 cloves)","2. Stir-fry-shop style: the essential aroma"],["Shacha sauce (Taiwanese BBQ sauce)","1 tsp","classic Taiwanese base flavor"],["Soy sauce","1/2 Tbsp","soy aroma"],["Rice wine","1/2 Tbsp","removes gaminess, adds aroma"],["Chinese black vinegar","1/2 tsp","brightens and cuts greasiness"],["White pepper","1/4 tsp","more stir-fry heat"],["Potato starch","1 tsp","silky, locks in moisture"],["Sesame oil","1/2 tsp","aroma and coats the meat"]]}],"steps":["Beef belly is already rich in fat and the thin slices take on flavor easily, so the marinade's job is to remove gaminess, add flavor and keep it tender. No long marinating needed — mix well, rest 10-15 minutes and cook!","Version 1 cuts way back on strong aromatics like garlic and ginger, keeping just enough to remove gaminess, and uses soy sauce and egg white (or water) to keep the beef juicy — rich soy flavor, mild and smooth.","Version 2 is the soul of stir-fry-shop beef noodles! Garlic, shacha sauce and a little rice wine give the beef that strong, classic Taiwanese aroma when stir-fried.","Cook the beef first, then the noodles: beef belly is fatty and thin. In a hot pan with hot oil, stir-fry on high until 70-80% cooked (just changed color) and remove.","Cook the vegetables and noodles separately: use the beef fat left in the pan to fry onion, green onion or water spinach, add the noodles and noodle sauce and cook through, then return the beef and toss on high for 10 seconds — so the meat doesn't overcook!"],"tips":[]},"g1295":{"title":"Marinating Beef Slices for Taiwanese Thick Soup","intro":"For tender, old-style Taiwanese all-beef “lean meat thick soup” (beef geng), the marinade decides whether the meat is tender and flavorful. Because beef fibers are coarser, the marinade focuses on locking in moisture and softening the fibers.","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredient","items":[["Sliced beef","300 g (choose cuts with a little marbling, like beef belly, chuck or round; if the slices are thin, cut them a bit wider)"]]},{"name":"Marinade (tenderizing and flavor)","items":[["Soy sauce","1.5 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1 tsp"],["White pepper","1/2 tsp"],["Five-spice powder","1/4 tsp (the core old-style Taiwanese aroma)"],["Grated / minced garlic","1 Tbsp"],["Sesame oil","1 tsp"]]}],"steps":["Water: 2 Tbsp (massaged into the meat)","Beaten egg: half an egg (about 1.5 Tbsp)","Potato starch / cornstarch: 1.5 Tbsp","Fish paste (optional): about 50–100 g (if you want fish-paste-wrapped beef geng; skip for plain lean beef geng)","[1. Prep the beef] If the slices are very thin, stack 2–3 and cut into strips about 2 cm wide; thicker slices can simply be cut into strips.","[2. Marinate and add water (tenderizing)] Put the beef in a bowl with the soy sauce, rice wine, sugar, white pepper, five-spice and garlic.","Mix by hand in one direction so the meat absorbs the seasoning.","Add water: add the 2 Tbsp water in 2 additions, massaging until the beef absorbs it completely (this fills the beef with moisture so it isn't dry when cooked).","[3. Protective coating] Mix in the half egg so the slices get a protein coating.","Mix in the sesame oil for aroma and to seal in moisture.","Finally mix in the starch so every slice has a thin, even coating.","[4. Rest] Cover with cling film and marinate in the fridge for at least 30 minutes (overnight is even better).","[After marinating, poach to set] Bring a pot of water to just below a boil (small bubbles, not rolling — about 85°C–90°C) and turn to low.","Add the marinated beef in batches, stretching out each slice as you drop it in (so they don't clump).","Don't stir right away — let it sit about 10–15 seconds so the coating sets.","Gently separate with chopsticks and cook until the beef changes color and floats (about 1–2 minutes — don't overcook or it toughens).","Lift out with a slotted spoon and add to your finished thick soup (napa cabbage soup, radish soup, etc.) to serve!"],"tips":[]},"g1459":{"title":"Steamed Eggplant (Rice Cooker) with Garlic Soy Paste","intro":"The biggest worries when steaming eggplant are it turning dark and mushy. For beautifully colored eggplant with just the right texture, the keys are high heat to keep air out and precise timing.","groups":[{"name":"1. Using store-bought thick soy sauce (fastest)","items":[["Thick soy sauce (soy paste)","3 Tbsp"],["Garlic","4-5 cloves (crushed very finely or minced)"],["Sugar","0.5 tsp (to brighten)"],["Sesame oil","1 tsp"],["Warm water","1 Tbsp (to dissolve the sugar and adjust thickness)"],["(Optional) chopped green onion",""],["Chopped chili","a little"]]}],"steps":["[2. Homemade soy paste] In a small pan, combine 3 Tbsp regular soy sauce, 3 Tbsp water and 1 Tbsp sugar.","Bring to a boil on low, stir in a little starch slurry (1 tsp potato starch + 1 Tbsp water) and stir until thick like a paste. Turn off the heat.","Once cooled, stir in lots of grated garlic and the sesame oil.","Drain the steamed, slightly cooled eggplant, arrange neatly on a plate, pour over the garlic soy paste and sprinkle with green onion. It's delicious hot or cold — the soft eggplant soaks up the garlic and sweet soy. Perfect with rice!"],"tips":["If you can buy Taiwanese thick soy sauce, just mix it as above; if you're abroad and can't find it, simmer regular soy sauce with sugar and potato starch as a quick substitute."]},"g1511":{"title":"Homemade Chewy Udon","intro":"To make homemade udon as springy as store-bought (the Sanuki udon texture), the keys are the water ratio, salt, kneading and stomping pressure, and above all the resting time. Some store-bought udon adds modified starch (like potato or tapioca starch) for perfect bounce, but you can get there by hand with the right technique!","groups":[{"name":"🥣 Sanuki golden ratio","items":[["All-purpose flour","300g (moderate gluten and chew)"],["Water","135g (strictly 45% hydration)"],["Salt","15g (5% — the key to tightening the gluten for bounce; the salt goes into the cooking water, so it won't be too salty)"]]}],"steps":["[1. Mixing and hydration] Dissolve the salt completely in the water (the salt water must be even).","Pour the salt water into the flour a little at a time, using chopsticks or your hands to work it into tiny crumbs (like oatmeal) so every bit of flour gets wet.","Gather the crumbs and press firmly into a very rough, stiff dough (it will feel hard to knead — that's normal).","[2. Traditional technique: resting and stomping] Put the rough dough in a clean, thick, large plastic bag and press out the air.","Lay a towel on the floor and put the bag on it. In clean socks, stomp with your heels from the center outward until the dough is flat.","Open the bag, fold the flat dough in three (or roll it back into a ball), return it to the bag and stomp flat again.","Repeat stomp → fold → stomp 3 to 4 times until the dough surface is smooth like a baby's skin.","[3. Two rests] Shape the smooth dough into a ball, seam side down, and put it back in the bag.","First rest: 30 minutes at room temperature.","Second rest: flatten it a little and refrigerate for 2 to 3 hours (overnight is best if you have time — the gluten develops perfectly).","[4. Rolling and cutting] Take the dough out of the fridge and let it come to room temperature (about 20 minutes; otherwise it's too stiff to roll).","Dust the table generously with potato starch (or cornstarch) and roll the dough into a large rectangle about 3mm to 4mm thick.","Dust the sheet heavily with starch and fold it like a fan.","Cut into noodles about 3mm wide with a sharp knife. Shake them apart right away and shake off the excess starch.","Bring a very large pot of plenty of water to a full boil.","Add the noodles and loosen with chopsticks. Keep at a rolling boil for 10 to 12 minutes (adjust for thickness; bite one — it's done when there's no white center).","The most important step: drain and immediately plunge into ice water and rinse vigorously! Rub the noodles with your hands like washing clothes to remove the surface starch.","The ice bath makes the noodles contract sharply — the ultimate secret to store-bought udon's slippery outside and chewy, springy inside!"],"tips":["Too much water or too little salt: udon has one of the lowest hydrations of any noodle (usually about 45%). Too much water makes the dough soft and it loses its chew; salt tightens the gluten, so without enough salt the noodles turn mushy.","Not enough kneading pressure: udon's bounce comes from a strong gluten network. Hand kneading isn't enough — traditionally the dough is stomped to apply enough pressure to align the gluten.","Not enough resting: freshly kneaded or stomped dough is very tight. Without enough time to relax, it springs back when rolled and the cooked center is hard rather than springy.","This is the most reliable golden ratio (adjust water by season: about 2g less in summer, 2g more in winter):","For even more bounce: replace 30g of the flour with 30g tapioca (or sweet-potato) starch or Japanese potato starch. This gives the slightly translucent, instantly springy chew of frozen Sanuki udon.","How to eat: fresh handmade udon is best as cold zaru udon or kamaage udon with a simple bonito-soy dip, grated radish, green onion and an onsen egg — it really shows off the perfect chew you stomped into it!"]},"g1516":{"title":"Shacha Beef and Green Pepper Fried Noodles (Taiwanese Style)","intro":"Shacha beef and green pepper fried noodles is a timeless classic of Taiwanese stir-fry restaurants and night markets! The essence of the Taiwanese flavor is wok hei, the combination of shacha and garlic, and thick yellow noodles that soak up the sauce — slightly sweet, rich but not heavy, and silky.","groups":[{"name":"1. Main ingredients","items":[["Sliced beef","about 150g (sirloin or beef belly with some marbling, thinly sliced across the grain)"],["Green bell pepper","1 (julienned or sliced)"],["Yellow (oil) noodles","about 300g (the soul of Taiwanese fried noodles — great at absorbing sauce)"],["Garlic","3-4 cloves (finely chopped; use plenty for aroma)"],["Chili","1 (sliced diagonally, to taste)"],["Green onion","1 stalk (cut into lengths)"]]},{"name":"2. Beef marinade","items":[["Soy sauce","1 tsp"],["Rice wine","1 tsp"],["Potato starch","1/2 tsp"],["Water","1 Tbsp"],["Vegetable oil","1 tsp (mixed in before cooking so the slices don't stick)"]]},{"name":"3. Shacha sauce (mix in a bowl first)","items":[["Shacha sauce (Taiwanese BBQ sauce)","2 Tbsp (Bull Head brand is the classic)"],["Soy sauce (or thick soy sauce)","1.5 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1/2 tsp (brightens and balances the salt)"],["Chinese black vinegar","1 tsp (added at the very end; optional but adds depth)"],["Water or stock","half a bowl (about 100ml — the noodles need enough liquid to simmer in)"]]}],"steps":["Mix the beef with the soy sauce, rice wine and water until absorbed, mix in the starch, then 1 tsp oil to seal in moisture. Marinate 10-15 minutes.","Heat a little more oil than usual on high, add the beef and separate quickly; cook until about medium-rare (surface changed color, still pink) and remove immediately. (This keeps the beef very tender.)","In the leftover oil (full of beef flavor), on medium heat, fry the garlic, green onion whites and chili until very fragrant.","Add the green pepper and stir-fry about 30 seconds so it sets its color and picks up the aroma.","Pour in the shacha sauce mixture (except the vinegar and green onion greens) and bring to a boil on high.","Add the noodles and toss quickly with chopsticks and a spatula so they're evenly coated.","Turn to medium, cover and simmer about 1 to 2 minutes. This simmering lets the yellow noodles absorb the shacha sauce so they're full of flavor.","When the liquid has reduced and thickened, add the beef and green onion greens.","Turn to high and toss quickly for about 20-30 seconds so the beef finishes cooking and combines with the noodles.","Just before serving, splash 1 tsp black vinegar around the edge of the wok (the classic Taiwanese stir-fry finish), toss twice on high and serve!","[💡 Tips from Taiwanese stir-fry chefs] Noodles: if you can't find Taiwanese yellow noodles, use alkaline noodles or thick home-style noodles or udon — but rinse them in cold water after cooking to keep them springy before frying.","Shacha oil: when you scoop the shacha sauce, include some of the oil on top — that's where the aroma is.","Moisture: Taiwanese fried noodles aren't dry-fried; there should always be a little rich shacha sauce at the bottom of the plate so the noodles are silky and juicy!"],"tips":[]},"g1649":{"title":"Garlic Pork Thick Soup (Low-Residue)","intro":"Garlic pork thick soup is known for its strong garlic aroma, silky coated pork and a broth thickened with potato starch. Following the earlier hospital-meal and low-residue diet needs, this dish is actually a great fit — the meat is coated in starch, so it's very tender and easy to swallow.","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients","items":[["Main","150 g thinly sliced pork belly (cut into small strips)"],["Soy sauce","1 tsp","meat marinade"],["Sake","1 tsp","meat marinade"],["Water","1 Tbsp","meat marinade"],["Potato starch","2 tsp (a little more starch gives the classic slippery texture)","meat marinade"]]},{"name":"Broth and seasoning","items":[["Water (or strained clear stock)","250 ml"],["Raw grated garlic","1.5 Tbsp (pressed extremely fine with no bits, or use garlic juice)"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Black vinegar (or white vinegar)","1 tsp (adds depth; optional)"],["Sugar","0.5 tsp"],["White pepper","a pinch"],["Soft egg","1 egg (beaten with 1 tsp water)"],["Thickening for the broth","1 Tbsp potato starch + 2 Tbsp water"]]}],"steps":["Garlic (no bits): traditional garlic thick soup keeps lots of chopped garlic (fried and raw). For low residue, use plenty of garlic juice or extremely finely pressed raw garlic, so the aroma goes into the broth without any garlic pieces.","Leave out the extras: the usual bamboo shoots, wood ear, shiitake and bonito flakes are high-fiber or hard to digest (“high residue”) and must be left out completely. The broth keeps only the coated pork and egg.","Keeping it tender: the meat can toughen when held in a thermal cooker for a long time. Use pork belly (more fat), or lean pork strips marinated with enough water and starch, for silky starch-coated pork."],"tips":["The thick broth protects the meat: the soup is thicker than a regular sukiyaki sauce, so it coats the pork even better. On the way to the hospital, the thermal cooker's heat finishes cooking the pork, and with its thick starch coating it tastes just like fresh night-market thick soup — wonderfully silky.","Garlic and digestion: after brief simmering and long, gentle heat in the thermal cooker, raw garlic loses its bite and turns sweet. But if the person's stomach is sensitive to garlic, reduce it to 1 tsp or use a little garlic powder instead."]},"g1653":{"title":"Sukiyaki-Style Pork with Soft Egg (Low-Residue, Thermal Cooker)","intro":"To take this to the hospital in a thermal cooker, the core of this sukiyaki pork belly and soft egg rice bowl is resisting the cooker's long carry-over heat. Thermal cookers hold heat extremely well: if you cook the egg to half-set and the pork to just done as usual, by the time you arrive the egg is hard, the pork is dry, and the rice has soaked up all the sauce and gone mushy.","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients","items":[["Main","150 g thinly sliced pork belly (bite-size, easy to eat in the hospital)"],["Soft egg","2 eggs (well beaten with 1 Tbsp water or cream — keeps the egg tender so it doesn't harden in the thermal cooker)"],["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp","low-residue broth"],["Mirin","2 Tbsp","low-residue broth"],["Sake","1 Tbsp","low-residue broth"],["Sugar","1 tsp","low-residue broth"],["Water (or strained bonito stock)","80 ml (note: a bit more liquid on purpose, since some gets absorbed while it sits)","low-residue broth"],["Thickening (the key to keeping it tender)","1 tsp potato starch + 1 Tbsp water"]]}],"steps":["Starch thickening is the secret: a low-residue diet can't use onions or other vegetables to thicken the sauce, and long holding in a thermal cooker dries out the meat. A light starch glaze acts like a protective film that locks in the pork juices, and it makes the rice smooth and easy to swallow.","Why add water to the egg? Plain egg heated for a long time turns spongy and hard. Beating 1 Tbsp water or stock into the eggs raises their water content, so even after 1-2 hours in the thermal cooker they stay soft like an omelet."],"tips":["To keep the rice from going soggy and the meat from overcooking, either keep the rice separate or control the doneness precisely:","Best option (separate): put the rice in one insulated jar and the sukiyaki pork and egg in the thermal cooker. Pour over the rice when it's time to eat.","If they must go together (as a lunchbox): put the rice on the bottom and deliberately leave the egg just barely set or slightly underdone, because the heat in the thermal cooker keeps cooking the egg and pork."]},"g1681":{"title":"Old-Style Steamed Chicken Thighs with Soy Paste (900 g, Low-Residue)","intro":"900 g (1.5 Taiwanese catties) of skinless, sinew-free chicken thigh is about 3 times the original recipe (based on 300 g). To make prep easy and accurate, here are the seasonings already converted for 900 g. You can marinate it all at once; if it's too much for one meal, portion it and freeze or refrigerate, then steam when needed.","groups":[{"name":"Golden-ratio seasonings for 900 g chicken thigh","items":[["Skinless, sinew-free chicken thigh pieces","900 g (1.5 catties)"],["Plain soy sauce","3 Tbsp (about 45 ml)"],["Thick soy sauce (soy paste)","3 tsp (= 1 Tbsp, about 15 ml)"],["Sugar","1.5 tsp (about 7.5 g)"],["Water","3 Tbsp (about 45 ml — very important, the key to juicy meat!)"],["Potato starch (or cornstarch)","3 tsp (= 1 Tbsp, about 15 g)"]]}],"steps":["Because there's a lot of meat, marinating takes a little patience. Follow this order so it's evenly seasoned and silky:","Cut and trim in batches: cut the chicken into small pieces easy for Dad to eat, trimming off any visible white sinew, fat and skin as you go.","First marinade (absorb liquid): put all the chicken in a large bowl with the soy sauce, thick soy sauce, sugar and 3 Tbsp water. Massage and mix firmly for about 1-2 minutes until all the liquid at the bottom has been absorbed and the meat looks glossy and plump.","Second marinade (seal with starch): once the liquid is absorbed, sprinkle in the starch and mix again so every piece has a thin coating. This coating seals in the juices while steaming.","Steaming it all at once (large amount): 900 g is a thick layer, so spread it as flat as possible on the plate — if piled too thick the center may not cook. Put 1.5 cups water in the outer pot of the rice cooker (2 cups if it's piled thick). When it clicks off, always rest another 10-15 minutes so the thick pieces are fully cooked and tender.","Eating it over several meals (recommended): divide the marinated chicken into 2-3 bags by Dad's portion size. Refrigerate what you'll eat today and freeze the rest. Thaw the day before and steam one plate per meal (1 cup water in the outer pot) — fresh, tender steamed chicken every day, and more variety so he doesn't get tired of it!"],"tips":["(Note: 1 Tbsp = 3 tsp = 15 ml)"]},"g1911":{"title":"Sichuan-Style Spicy Cold Noodle Sauce (Taiwanese)","intro":"Taiwan's Sichuan-style spicy cold noodles are irresistible: the mellow richness of traditional Taiwanese sesame sauce (sesame paste and peanut butter) meets the numbing, spicy, savory aroma of Sichuan chili oil. Together they're rich but not heavy. Here's the sauce ratio for a classic bowl, in two parts: the core base sauce and the Sichuan numbing-spicy layer.","groups":[{"name":"2. Seasoning","items":[["Light soy sauce","2.5 Tbsp"],["Chinese black vinegar","1.5 Tbsp (Taiwanese cold noodles favor mellow black vinegar; for a brighter tang, mix in 0.5 Tbsp white vinegar)"],["Sugar","1 Tbsp (a little sweetness balances the salt and heat for a softer flavor)"],["Grated garlic","1 Tbsp (essential — the garlic is the soul of cold noodles)"],["MSG or chicken bouillon powder","a little (for umami; optional)"]]}],"steps":["When mixing, first loosen the sesame base, then add the other seasonings, and finally drizzle on the numbing-spicy part to taste.","[1. Base sauce] Pure sesame paste: 2 Tbsp","Unsweetened peanut butter: 1 Tbsp (the key to the rich aroma of Taiwanese cold noodles; use more sesame paste if you don't have it)","Warm or cold boiled water: 3 - 4 Tbsp (to loosen the sesame paste)","[3. Sichuan numbing-spicy layer] Sichuan red chili oil: 1 - 2 Tbsp (focus on the fragrance of the chili oil)","Sichuan pepper oil / ground Sichuan pepper: 0.5 - 1 tsp (the source of the numbing “ma” — the essence of Sichuan flavor)"],"tips":[]},"g1931":{"title":"Scallion-Braised Chicken over Noodles","intro":"Scallion-braised chicken over noodles is a classic! As bone-in chicken simmers, the goodness from the bones goes into the sauce and makes it twice as savory — irresistible over rice or noodles. Here's a scallion-braised bone-in chicken noodle recipe with plenty of rich, fragrant sauce for pouring over noodles:","groups":[{"name":"Chicken and marinade","items":[["Bone-in chicken pieces","about 500 g (bite-size thigh or whole-chicken pieces)"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["White pepper","a pinch"],["Potato starch","1 tsp (locks in juices and gives the sauce a slight body)"]]},{"name":"Main aromatics and seasoning","items":[["Green onions","5～6 stalks (the soul of the dish — be generous! cut into 5 cm lengths, whites and greens separate)"],["Mature ginger","5 slices"],["Garlic","4～5 cloves (lightly smashed)"],["Chili","1 (sliced diagonally; omit or seed if you don't like heat)"],["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["Oyster sauce","1 Tbsp (for richness and shine)"],["Rock sugar","1 tsp (for flavor and color)"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Water or stock","about 250～300 ml (keep plenty of sauce for the noodles)"],["Sesame oil","a little before serving"]]},{"name":"Seasoning and simmering (the key sauce)","items":[["Pour around the edge of the wok","1 Tbsp rice wine"],["2 Tbsp soy sauce and","1 Tbsp oyster sauce"],["Let the soy aroma sizzle in the hot wok",""],["Add the rock sugar and chili and toss so the chicken is evenly colored",""],["Pour in water or stock (to about","70～80% of the height of the chicken)"],["Bring to a boil on high",""],["Turn to low",""],["Cover and simmer","about 12～15 minutes"],["Until the bone-in chicken is cooked through and flavorful",""]]}],"steps":["Marinate the chicken: rinse and pat completely dry. Mix with the soy sauce, rice wine and white pepper, then the starch, and marinate 15～20 minutes.","Prep: cut the green onions, keeping whites and greens separate.","Fry the whites and ginger: heat 1.5 Tbsp oil and fry the ginger on medium-low until the edges curl and it smells fragrant. Add the garlic and green onion whites and fry until lightly browned (that's where the scallion flavor comes from).","Brown the chicken: add the marinated chicken, turn to medium-high and brown the surface until golden and slightly charred (it doesn't need to be cooked through — this seals in juices and renders the fat).","Reduce and add the greens: uncover, reduce on medium to the thickness you like (leave enough sauce for the noodles). Add all the green onion greens and toss about 30 seconds until soft and fragrant. Drizzle with a little sesame oil and serve.","While the chicken simmers, boil your favorite noodles in another pot (plain wheat noodles, ramen or thick home-style noodles soak up sauce best), drain and put in large bowls.","Pour the hot scallion chicken with plenty of rich sauce generously over the noodles.","Toss a little so every strand is coated in soy and scallion oil, and enjoy!","💡 Chef's tip: since it's going over noodles, don't reduce the sauce too much. If you'd like the sauce thicker so it clings to the noodles, add a tiny bit of starch slurry just before serving. Enjoy!"],"tips":[]},"g1959":{"title":"Taiwanese Herbal Lamb Hot Pot (with Herb Packet)","intro":"Having a ready-made Chinese herb packet is great — it's the heart of Taiwanese herbal lamb hot pot. For a rich broth and tender lamb with no gamey smell, the secrets are thoroughly blanching and rinsing the meat and frying the ginger in sesame oil.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Main","bone-in lamb pieces (lamb belly with skin, lamb chops or leg), about 1.2-1.8 kg"],["Lamb hot pot herb packet","1 packet","essentials"],["Mature ginger","1 large knob (thick slices)","essentials"],["Rice wine","1 bottle (up to 2, to taste)","essentials"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Black (toasted) sesame oil","3-4 Tbsp"],["Rock sugar","1 Tbsp"],["Salt","to taste"],["Hot pot add-ins of your choice","cabbage, napa cabbage, frozen tofu, tofu skin, enoki mushrooms, daikon radish (cook first)"]]}],"steps":["When to remove the herb packet? Usually after the broth has simmered gently for about 1 hour, the herbs have released most of their flavor. Taste before adding the hot pot ingredients, and once it's strong enough, take the packet out — soaking too long makes the broth bitter.","The essential dipping sauce: lamb hot pot needs its dipping sauce to cut the richness! Golden ratio: 2 cubes fermented tofu + 1 Tbsp spicy chili bean paste + 1 tsp sugar + 1 Tbsp broth, mixed well.","Salt goes in last: salting too early makes the meat tighten and toughen so it won't get tender — always season with salt just before serving."],"tips":["Enjoy a fragrant, warming and nourishing pot of lamb hot pot!"]},"g2103":{"title":"Shacha Stir-Fried Water Spinach","intro":"A classic full of Taiwanese stir-fry restaurant flavor. The rich aroma of shacha with crisp, tender water spinach — great as a side or over rice.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Water spinach","one large bunch (about 300g), washed and cut into lengths; keep stems and leaves separate"],["Garlic","3-4 cloves, smashed and minced"],["Chili","1 (to taste), sliced diagonally"],["Shredded meat (optional)","about 50g, for extra flavor and texture"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Shacha sauce (Taiwanese BBQ sauce)","1.5 - 2 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp (removes off-flavors, adds aroma)"],["Soy sauce","1 tsp (for color)"],["Sugar","a pinch (balances the shacha's saltiness)"],["Water","a little"]]}],"steps":["Marinate (if using meat): mix the meat with a little soy sauce, rice wine and a pinch of starch and marinate 5 minutes.","Aromatics: heat oil in a wok and fry the garlic and chili. If using meat, stir-fry it now until 80% done and remove.","Fry the shacha: turn to low and fry the shacha sauce until fragrant (watch the heat — burnt shacha turns bitter).","Stems first: turn to high, add the stems and toss quickly, then add the rice wine, soy sauce, sugar and a little water; keep the heat high so the stems soften.","Leaves and combine: add the leaves (and the meat) and toss on high for about 20-30 seconds.","Serve: as soon as the leaves change color and wilt, turn off the heat and plate — cooking too long makes the greens watery and dark."],"tips":["High heat, fast: the key to leafy greens is high heat and quick movement, so they stay bright green and crisp.","Prevent darkening: splash a little hot water or rice wine around the edge of the wok to make steam — it cooks faster and reduces oxidation.","Southeast Asian twist: for more layered flavor, replace some soy sauce with 1 tsp shrimp paste for a Southeast Asian umami note."]},"g2118":{"title":"Three-Cup Chicken","intro":"Boneless chicken pieces are great because they cook fast and there are no bones to deal with. This is the most rice-friendly option: with less fat in the chicken pieces, the sauce reduces faster — on the table in 15 minutes.","groups":[{"name":"My sauce ratio","items":[["Soy sauce","3 spoons"],["Mirin","3 spoons"],["Sugar","2 tsp"],["Rice wine","a splash"]]},{"name":"Other ingredients","items":[["Boneless chicken pieces","as needed"],["Ginger slices","as needed"],["Garlic cloves","as needed"],["Thai basil","a big handful"],["Black (toasted) sesame oil","as needed (even better if you have it)"],["Starch slurry","a little (to thicken)"]]}],"steps":["Heat oil in a wok (black sesame oil is best) and fry the ginger and garlic until fragrant.","Add the chicken and stir-fry on high until lightly charred.","Add the soy sauce, mirin, sugar and a splash of rice wine (my ratio: 3 spoons : 3 spoons : 2 tsp).","Reduce until thick, then thicken with the slurry.","Toss in a big handful of Thai basil, stir a few times and serve."],"tips":["If the chicken pieces are large, score them a few times with the tip of a knife so the heat gets in faster.","Before cooking, mix the chicken with a little rice wine and starch to lock in the juices so it doesn't dry out."],"mine":["My final sauce ratio: 3 spoons soy sauce, 3 spoons mirin, 2 tsp sugar, a tiny splash of rice wine; follow this recipe and thicken at the end. (I had Gemini save this as a note in Google Keep at the time.)"]},"g2287":{"title":"Marinated Beef Slices for Instant Noodles","intro":"To add tender, flavorful beef slices to instant noodles, the marinade is all about keeping moisture in and adding flavor. Instant noodle broth is usually strong, so a savory base marinade works best, plus a few physical tricks for tenderness.","groups":[],"steps":["[1. Golden marinade ratio (for 150g beef slices)] Base: 1 Tbsp soy sauce, 1 tsp rice wine","Flavor: a pinch of white pepper, a pinch of sugar (for umami)","For tenderness: 1 Tbsp water, 1 tsp potato starch (or cornstarch)","To seal: 1 tsp sesame or vegetable oil","[2. Method] Add water: put the beef in a bowl with the soy sauce, rice wine, sugar and white pepper, then 1 Tbsp water. Massage with your hand until the meat absorbs all the water and feels tacky.","Add starch: mix in the starch. It combines with the water to form a protective film so the meat doesn't lose moisture in the pot.","Seal with oil: finally mix in a little sesame oil. This stops the slices sticking together and locks in the marinade flavor.","Rest: at least 15–20 minutes.","[3. How to cook it perfectly] Beef slices are very thin and get tough if boiled with the noodles too long. Use one of these two methods:","Method A (most tender): cook the noodles first and put them in the bowl. Lay the slices flat in the still-boiling broth left in the pot, separate gently, and as soon as they change color (about 80% done) lift them onto the noodles.","Method B (richer broth): when the noodles are 80% cooked, add the beef and boil on high for 30 seconds; when the beef is pink, turn off the heat and let the residual heat finish it."],"tips":["If you like strong flavors: add a little shacha sauce or minced garlic to the marinade — it goes well with most braised-beef or seafood instant noodles.","If the meat is tough: add a few drops of white vinegar or a tiny pinch of baking soda (just a fingertip) when adding the water — it breaks down the fibers for a silkier texture.","Beef slices prepared this way are as silky as at a stir-fry shop or beef noodle restaurant, and they're delicious once they soak up the noodle broth!"]},"g2711":{"title":"Octopus with Garlic Shoots","intro":"Octopus stir-fried with green garlic (garlic shoots) is a classic home dish, especially popular at Taiwanese stir-fry restaurants and during Lunar New Year. The key is not to cook the octopus too long, or it shrinks and gets tough.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Octopus","about 300g (bite-size pieces; if raw, blanch for 20 seconds first)"],["Green garlic (garlic shoots)","2-3 stalks (sliced diagonally; keep the white and green parts separate)"],["Julienned ginger","a little"],["Chili","1 (sliced diagonally, to taste)"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar","a pinch"],["White pepper","to taste (for a stronger flavor, add 1 tsp shacha sauce)"]]}],"steps":["Texture: the springy, crunchy octopus contrasts with the soft, sweet cooked green garlic.","Aroma: heated green garlic releases a distinctive pungent aroma that masks any fishiness and brings out the sweetness.","Savory and great with rice: seasoned with shacha or soy sauce, it's a real “rice killer” and also great with drinks.","Aromatics: heat oil in a wok and fry the ginger, chili and the white parts of the green garlic.","Add the octopus: stir-fry on high. If it's already blanched, just toss until evenly mixed.","Season: splash the rice wine and soy sauce around the edge of the wok, add the sugar and pepper and toss quickly until fragrant.","Finish: add the green parts of the garlic and stir-fry about 10-20 seconds until just softened, then turn off the heat and serve."],"tips":["Octopus: if you bought cooked octopus legs from the supermarket, slice and stir-fry them briefly — don't cook them long.","Green garlic: the white part takes longer to cook and has more aroma, so always fry it first; the green part yellows easily, so add it last to keep it bright and crisp.","Removing fishiness: ginger and rice wine are the soul of this dish — don't skip them.","Want to try it with the octopus you have? If you have shacha sauce, a little makes it taste even more like a Taiwanese stir-fry restaurant!"]},"g2844":{"title":"Lemon Tart","intro":"A lemon tart has two parts: the pastry and the lemon curd filling. Here's the classic ingredient list for a standard French lemon tart (about 6-8 inches):","groups":[{"name":"1. Pastry — it should be cool and crisp.","items":[["Unsalted butter","100g - 120g (cold, diced)"],["Cake (low-protein) flour","200g - 250g"],["Powdered sugar","70g - 80g"],["Egg","1"],["Almond flour","30g - 40g (adds aroma and crispness; optional)"],["Salt","a pinch"]]},{"name":"2. Lemon curd filling","items":[["Lemon juice","80ml - 120ml (about 3-4 lemons, freshly squeezed)"],["Lemon zest","from 1-2 lemons (only the colored outer layer — the white pith is bitter)"],["Caster sugar","100g - 130g (to taste)"],["Whole eggs","3 (or use yolks for extra richness)"],["Unsalted butter","120g - 150g (softened; stirred in at the end for silkiness)"],["Gelatin","2g - 5g (optional; makes the filling firmer and easier to slice)"]]}],"steps":["Butter: keep the butter cold for the pastry, but slightly softened when stirring it into the lemon curd at the end.","Water bath: cook the curd over a water bath so the eggs don't heat too fast and scramble.","Strain: strain the finished curd once for an even silkier texture."],"tips":["The lemon curd is the soul of the tart — it's all about balancing sweet and sour.","For an easier version, try the American way: a base of crushed digestive biscuits mixed with melted butter, and a filling of condensed milk, egg yolks and lemon juice baked together."]},"g2847":{"title":"Strengthening a Sourdough Starter (Straight from the Fridge)","intro":"To bring a fridge-dormant sourdough starter back to explosive activity, the key is continuous, regular, warm feeding. For your current 18°C room temperature, I suggest a 2-day “boot camp”. This makes sure the starter is at peak activity before it goes into your cinnamon roll dough (high sugar, high fat).","groups":[{"name":"Final sweet levain","items":[["Ratio","30g active starter + 60g flour + 60g warm water + 10g sugar"],["This is the","100g starter used in your recipe (the extra is kept as your mother starter)"],["Float test","when this sweet levain peaks, drop a small piece in water. If it floats, it's now strong enough to lift a heavy cinnamon roll dough!"]]}],"steps":["Take it out and warm up: in the morning, take the starter out of the fridge and leave it at room temperature for about 1 hour.","First feeding (dilute the old acidity): ratio 1 (starter) : 1 (flour) : 1 (warm water) — e.g. 20g old starter + 20g flour + 20g warm water (about 30°C). White flour is fine here; whole wheat isn't necessary. Stir and put it in the warmest spot at home (e.g. inside the microwave next to a cup of hot water).","Watch: at 18°C it may take 8-12 hours to double.","Second feeding (morning): ratio 1 (starter) : 2 (flour) : 2 (water), with 10-20% whole wheat flour (e.g. 8g of the 40g flour is whole wheat). The minerals in whole wheat really excite the yeast.","Third feeding (evening/bedtime): if the starter doubled within 8 hours, repeat the 1:2:2 feeding. Goal: see whether it can rise to 2.5 or even 3 times its height.","Water temperature is key: use 30°C-35°C water at every feeding. This offsets the cold room and gives the culture a warm boost.","Keep less old starter: during strengthening, don't keep too much old starter. Too much makes it overly sour, and high acidity actually suppresses the yeast's rising power.","Use a “proofing box”: 18°C is really too slow. Put the starter in the oven (off) with just the oven light on. The light usually keeps it at 24-26°C — the yeast's favorite temperature."],"tips":["Following this plan, your starter will be very lively by the morning of day 3. Want me to add this “strong starter training plan” to your notes too?"]},"g2898":{"title":"Romanian Sour Soup (Ciorbă)","intro":"Ciorbă (Romanian sour soup) is the soul of Romanian cooking. Unlike a regular soup (supă), ciorbă is defined by its sourness, usually from fermented wheat bran liquid (borș), lemon, vinegar or sour cream. The most classic and popular is ciorbă de văcuță (Romanian beef sour soup). Here's the recipe:","groups":[{"name":"🛒 Ingredients","items":[["Meat","500g beef (small pieces; cuts with some connective tissue are best)"],["1 onion (chopped)",""],["2 carrots (diced)",""],["1 parsnip or parsley root (diced)",""],["1 small piece celery root (diced)",""],["2 potatoes (cubed)",""],["1 bowl green beans or peas",""]]},{"name":"Broth and seasoning","items":[["2 Tbsp tomato paste",""],["Sour element","200ml borș (if unavailable, juice of 1 lemon or 2 Tbsp white vinegar)"],["Fresh herbs","parsley and lovage (leuștean — the signature flavor of Romanian soups)"],["Salt and black pepper",""]]}],"steps":["Simmer the beef: put the beef in cold water and bring to a boil on high. Skim off the foam, then simmer on low for about 40-60 minutes until tender (use enough water to cover everything plus extra for the soup).","Add the root vegetables: onion, carrots, parsnip and celery root. Cook about 15 more minutes.","Add the other vegetables: potatoes, green beans and tomato paste. Simmer until the potatoes are cooked.","Season and sour: add the lemon juice, vinegar or heated borș, and season with salt and pepper. Bring back to a boil, then turn off the heat.","Herbs: add lots of chopped fresh parsley and lovage. Cover and leave 5 minutes so the aroma soaks in.","[💡 How Romanians eat it] Sour cream (smântână): Romanians always add a spoonful of rich sour cream to this soup.","Hot peppers (ardei iute): a bite of fresh or pickled green chili instantly lifts the soup.","Homemade bread: dipping thick country bread in the soup is the most authentic way to enjoy it."],"tips":[]},"g2906":{"title":"Hsin Tung Yang-Style Braised Pork Sauce (Lu Rou)","intro":"To recreate the glossy, rich, distinctive “canned” flavor of Hsin Tung Yang's canned braised pork sauce at home, the keys are frying the shallots and a long, gentle simmer. Its signature is finely minced meat (no big chunks of fat) with the fat and sauce fully blended. Here's the copycat recipe:","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients","items":[["Ground pork","600g (about 30:70 fat to lean; ask the butcher to grind it twice for a texture closer to the can)"],["Shallots","10-15 (chopped — the soul of the dish)"],["Minced garlic","3-5 cloves"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Soy sauce","100ml (a sweeter Taiwanese soy sauce is best)"],["Rock sugar","1.5 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","50ml"],["Five-spice powder","1/2 tsp (not more, or it overpowers)"],["White pepper","a pinch"],["Water","about 600ml (just to cover)"]]}],"steps":["Make shallot oil: pour in a bit more oil than for a normal stir-fry, add the chopped shallots to cold oil and fry slowly on low until golden and crisp, then remove. (Note: take them out as soon as they color, or they turn bitter.)","Fry the pork: in the remaining shallot oil, add the ground pork. Stir-fry on high, breaking it up, until it changes color and releases its fat.","Aroma: add the garlic, five-spice and white pepper and stir until you smell the meat and spices together.","Color and season: add the rock sugar and stir until melted (caramelized), then pour the soy sauce around the edge of the pan to bring out its aroma, and add the rice wine.","Slow simmer: add the water and the fried shallots. Bring to a boil on high, then cover and simmer on the lowest heat for about 40-60 minutes.","Rest: don't eat it right away — turn off the heat and let it sit 20 minutes. If you can leave it overnight, the fat and sauce blend into an even richer, can-like flavor."],"tips":["Fine is the key: the meat in the canned version is very fine. If your ground pork is coarse, chop it a bit more with a knife before cooking.","The fried shallots: the fine dark bits you see in the canned sauce are fried shallots cooked down. For a closer texture, crush the fried shallots before simmering.","Sealing with fat: the canned sauce is so fragrant because of the thick layer of lard on top. If your pork is lean, add an extra spoonful of lard while simmering for that silky texture over rice.","Would you like me to estimate the calories of this recipe or suggest side dishes?"]},"g2914":{"title":"Marinated Beef Slices (to Stir-Fry the Next Day)","intro":"For silky, never-dry beef like a restaurant chef's stir-fry, the keys are three steps: break the fibers, add water, seal with oil. Here's the golden marinade, made the day before so you only need a hot 2-minute stir-fry the next day:","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients (for 300g sliced beef)","items":[["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp","base seasoning"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp","base seasoning"],["White pepper","a pinch","base seasoning"],["Sugar","half a tsp, brightens flavor and softens the meat","base seasoning"],["Water","1-2 Tbsp","key to tenderness"],["Potato starch or water chestnut starch","1 tsp","key to tenderness"],["To seal and keep fresh","sesame or vegetable oil (1 Tbsp)"]]}],"steps":["Add water: put the beef in a bowl with the soy sauce, rice wine, sugar and pepper. The most important step is adding the 1-2 Tbsp water in stages, massaging in circles until the beef absorbs it all. Why: beef with more water in it won't turn dry and tough when stir-fried.","Add starch (locks in moisture): mix in the starch. It forms a protective film on the beef so the juices don't escape when heated.","Seal with oil (keeps air out): finally mix in 1 Tbsp oil. Bonus: it locks in the seasoning and stops the slices sticking together when you cook them the next day.","Storage: put the marinated beef in a container or zip bag, squeeze out as much air as possible and refrigerate.","Warm up: take it out of the fridge 10 minutes before cooking so it cooks more evenly.","Hot pan, then oil: heat the pan first, then add the oil, and add the beef once the oil is hot.","High heat: when the beef has changed color and is about 80% done, remove it. Cook the vegetables (gai lan, green pepper, onion), then return the beef and toss — that's how it stays most tender."],"tips":["For coarser cuts (like sirloin or round), add a tiny pinch of baking soda (the size of a fingernail) or half an egg white to the marinade. It breaks down the fibers for ultimate tenderness.","Would you like some quick stir-fry combos to go with this beef (like scallion or shacha)?"]},"g2915":{"title":"Fried Nian Gao (Sweet Rice Cake)","intro":"Fried nian gao is one of the most popular Lunar New Year snacks — crispy outside, soft and chewy inside, with a lucky meaning of “rising higher every year”. Here's the classic crispy batter method:","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients","items":[["Star ingredient","sweet nian gao (red bean or plain)"]]},{"name":"Batter ratio","items":[["Cake (low-protein) flour","100g"],["Rice flour or sweet-potato starch","30g (for extra crunch)"],["Egg","1"],["Water","about 80ml - 100ml (adjust for thickness)"],["Vegetable oil","1 tsp (secret: makes the batter crispier)"],["Baking powder","1/2 tsp (optional, for a puffier texture)"]]},{"name":"Making the batter","items":[["Combine the flour",""],["starch",""],["and egg",""],["Add the water gradually, stirring until thick (it should drip slowly off the whisk and leave a trail)",""],["Finally add","1 tsp oil and stir well"],["Coat","coat each slice of nian gao in a thick layer of batter"]]}],"steps":["Slice: cut the nian gao into rectangles or squares about 0.8cm - 1cm thick. Too thin and they melt; too thick and the center won't heat through.","Fry: heat oil to about 160°C (a drop of batter should float up immediately). Add the slices one at a time and fry on medium until golden on both sides. Turn to high for about 10 seconds before removing to drive out the oil, then drain."],"tips":["Stop them sticking: don't add too many at once, and don't move them for the first 30 seconds — separate them once the batter has set.","Variation (pickled greens / cilantro): many people sandwich a piece of pickled mustard greens or cilantro between two slices before battering — the sweet-salty contrast cuts the richness.","Wonton-wrapper version: no batter needed — roll each slice in a wonton wrapper, seal the edge with water and fry. Super crispy, like a spring roll!","Would you like me to scale this up for a larger amount (like a whole big nian gao)?"]},"g2918":{"title":"Marinated Fish Slices for Hot Pot (500 g)","intro":"Great! For 500 g of fish slices (about two large tilapia fillets or one big sea bass fillet from the supermarket), we can adjust the marinade precisely so the fish stays tender and springy in the pot.","groups":[{"name":"Rinse and pat the fish dry, then add these in order and mix in one direction","items":[["Rice wine (or cooking wine)","1.5 Tbsp (removes fishiness, adds aroma)"],["Salt","1 tsp (about 5g, seasons and firms the fish)"],["White pepper","1/2 tsp (key to removing fishiness; a little extra is fine)"],["Minced ginger","1 tsp (use ginger juice if you don't like biting into ginger)"]]},{"name":"When the fish feels a little tacky, add","items":[["Egg white","1 (for 500g fish, use a whole one for even coating)"],["Potato starch (or cornstarch)","1.5 to 2 Tbsp (this thin coat locks in juices so it doesn't dry out)"],["Sesame oil","1 tsp (added last so the slices don't stick together in the pot)"]]}],"steps":["Add in stages: first the seasoning and wine so the fish absorbs moisture, then the egg white for silkiness, and the starch last.","Rest: refrigerate 20 - 30 minutes. The cold helps the protein and starch bind so the coating doesn't fall off in the pot.","Heat control: 500g is a lot, so add it to the pot in two or three batches; dumping it all in at once drops the broth temperature and the fish cooks unevenly."],"tips":["More umami? Add a little chicken bouillon powder or fish sauce to the marinade.","Sichuan style? For a red chili oil hot pot, add 1 tsp ground Sichuan pepper to the marinade for more depth.","Are these 500 g of fish slices for tonight's hot pot? If you'd like an all-purpose hot pot dipping sauce to go with them, I can give you one right away!"]},"g2938":{"title":"Quinoa Salad Dressing (based on 12 spoons lemon juice)","intro":"If you've used 12 spoons of lemon juice, to keep the classic 2:1 oil-to-acid ratio (2 parts oil : 1 part acid), adjust the other dressing ingredients like this:","groups":[{"name":"🥗 Adjusted dressing ratio","items":[["Fresh lemon juice","12 spoons (fixed)"],["Extra-virgin olive oil","24 spoons (or about 1.5 cups / 360ml)"],["Honey or maple syrup","2 - 3 tsp (adjust to how much sourness you like)"],["Black pepper","to taste"],["Salt","to taste (keep in mind the saltiness of the feta)"]]}],"steps":["Quantity note: 12 spoons of lemon juice is about the juice of 3 - 4 medium lemons. This makes a lot of dressing (almost 400ml total) — enough for 10 - 12 cups of cooked quinoa or a party of 10+.","Emulsifying: for this much dressing, shake the oil and lemon juice hard in a clean glass jar or use a small stick blender so it emulsifies fully — it's smoother and coats the quinoa more evenly.","Leftovers: if you don't use it all, the emulsified dressing keeps in the fridge for about 3 - 5 days. Shake again before using."],"tips":["Want me to update the adjusted dressing ratio in the earlier memo too?"]},"g3021":{"title":"Preserved Radish Chicken Soup (Rice Cooker)","intro":"Looks like you want the simplest yet authentic preserved radish chicken soup! Stewing in an electric cooker keeps the chicken sweet, and the aged aroma of old preserved radish is fully released at a steady temperature. This soup is nicknamed “Taiwan's black truffle soup” for its deep, mellow, lingering flavor.","groups":[{"name":"🛒 Ingredients","items":[["Chicken","half a chicken (bone-in thighs are best — they stay juicy when stewed)"],["Aged preserved radish (lao cai pu)","about 40g (the black-gold kind — source of the mellow broth)"],["Young preserved radish","about 20g (the light-colored kind; adds crunch and saltiness; optional)"]]},{"name":"Other ingredients","items":[["Ginger slices","5 slices"],["Garlic cloves","6"],["Clams","10 (the secret to umami)"],["Rice wine","2 Tbsp","seasoning"],["Water","1500 ml (no need for salt — it's usually salty enough)","seasoning"]]}],"steps":["Rinse and cut: rinse the aged preserved radish quickly (don't soak — it loses its aroma) and cut into small pieces or slices.","Blanch: bring a pot of water to a boil, blanch the chicken for 2-3 minutes until scum appears, then lift out and rinse in cold water. (Don't skip this — it keeps the broth clear.)","Into the pot: put the chicken, both preserved radishes, ginger and garlic in the inner pot of the electric cooker.","Add water and cook: add 1500 ml water (or enough to cover), put 1.5 cups water in the outer pot and press the switch.","Finish with umami: when the switch pops up, add the clams and rice wine, add 0.2 cup water to the outer pot (or just leave it covered for 5 minutes) and let the heat open the clams."],"tips":["Add daikon: if you worry the aged radish is too salty, add a few chunks of fresh daikon radish — it adds a clean sweetness and balances the heaviness.","Don't peel the garlic: rinse whole unpeeled cloves and add them — the garlic flavor is mellower and the broth stays clear.","Time makes it delicious: after the switch pops up, always rest it at least 15 minutes. This lets the chicken soak up the salty-sweet radish flavor.","Want me to suggest a few light stir-fries to round out a meal with this soup?"]},"g3035":{"title":"Shiitake Pork Thick Soup Instant Noodles + Shredded Beef","intro":"A really clever combo! Shiitake pork thick soup instant noodles already have a bonito-scented, thickened broth; with fresh ingredients added, they easily upgrade into restaurant-style “da lu mian”. The keys are tender shredded beef and sweet vegetables.","groups":[{"name":"Beef marinade","items":[["Soy sauce","1 tsp (color and aroma)"],["Rice wine","1 tsp (removes off-flavors)"],["White pepper","a pinch"],["Sesame oil","half a tsp"],["Potato starch (or cornstarch)","1 tsp (key: forms a protective film)"],["Water","1 Tbsp (the meat absorbs it and becomes more tender)"]]}],"steps":["Prep: julienne the carrot (cooks faster and looks nicer) and tear the cabbage into bite-size pieces.","Fry the vegetables: heat a little oil and fry the carrot until slightly golden (beta-carotene is fat-soluble, so frying makes it more nutritious), then add the cabbage and toss briefly.","Broth: add water (a little more than the package says, since the vegetables absorb some), bring to a boil and add the seasoning packet.","Poach the beef: when it boils, add the marinated beef, separating the strands. As soon as it turns pink (about 70-80% done), lift it out.","Noodles: add the noodles and cook to your liking.","Combine: return the beef to the pot, add the black vinegar packet that comes with the noodles (the soul of thick soup!), turn off the heat, stir and serve."],"tips":["More depth: if you have dried chilies or shacha sauce, add a little when frying the carrot — it goes amazingly well with the thick soup broth.","Thickness: if the included thickener isn't thick enough, add a little starch slurry at the end for a more authentic thick-soup noodle feel.","Want me to generate an image of the finished dish to get you hungry?"]},"g3133":{"title":"French Onion and Mushroom Soup","intro":"Of course! Adding mushrooms to French onion soup brings a woodsy aroma and umami, and the tender mushrooms make the texture richer. Think of it as an upgraded classic onion soup.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Onions","3 (sliced)"],["Mixed mushrooms","250g (cremini/portobello, button or shiitake, thickly sliced)"],["Garlic","2 cloves (minced)"],["Butter","40g"],["Stock","1000ml (beef is richest; chicken or vegetable also work)"],["Red or white wine","120ml (adds depth)"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Salt",""],["Black pepper",""],["Dried thyme",""],["Bay leaf","1"],["To serve","thick slices of sourdough, Gruyère or mozzarella"]]}],"steps":["Dry-sear the mushrooms (key step): no oil yet — put the sliced mushrooms in a hot pan and dry-fry on medium-high until the moisture is gone and they shrink and turn golden brown. Then add a little butter and salt, toss and set aside. This keeps the mushrooms from going soggy and bland in the soup and releases the most aroma.","Caramelize the onions: add the rest of the butter to the same pan with the onions and a pinch of salt to draw out moisture. Cook slowly on low for about 30 minutes until the onions are deep chocolate brown.","Combine: return the mushrooms with the garlic, stir with the onions for 2 minutes and sprinkle in the thyme.","Deglaze and simmer: pour in the wine and scrape up the caramelized bits from the bottom. Once the alcohol has cooked off a little, add the stock and bay leaf, bring to a boil and simmer on low for 15-20 minutes.","Gratinée with sourdough: ladle the soup into ovenproof bowls, top with a slice of toasted sourdough and a thick layer of cheese. Bake at 220°C until the cheese bubbles and browns in spots.","[Gemini's tasting notes] Why sourdough? Mushrooms and onions are both sweet, and sourdough's gentle tang brightens the whole bowl like lemon juice, so the rich cheese doesn't feel heavy.","More body: for a heartier soup, stir in a tablespoon of flour at step 3 — the soup gets thicker, like a restaurant version.","Would you like tips on choosing a red wine for this soup, or on keeping the sourdough crisp in the soup?"],"tips":[]},"g3228":{"title":"Cantonese Beef and Egg Thick Soup","intro":"A classic Cantonese home dish that's all about tender beef, silky egg and a savory broth. To get that restaurant-style silkiness, the keys are marinating the beef and timing when it goes in.","groups":[{"name":"🛒 Ingredients","items":[["Beef tenderloin / sirloin","200g","thinly sliced across the grain"],["Eggs","2","lightly beaten — don't overbeat"],["Cilantro","as much as you like","chopped (the essential supporting role)"],["Ginger, julienned/minced","a little","removes off-flavors, adds aroma"],["Stock / water","800ml","chicken stock is the most flavorful"]]}],"steps":["[1. Slice the beef, then marinate 15 minutes with] Light soy sauce: 1 Tbsp","Rice wine: 1 tsp","White pepper: a pinch (key to removing off-flavors)","Starch: 1 tsp","Cooking oil: 1 tsp (locks in moisture)","[2. Starch slurry] In a small bowl, mix 3 Tbsp starch with 3 Tbsp water.","[3. Broth] Heat a little oil, fry the ginger until fragrant, then add the stock and bring to a boil.","Season with salt and white pepper.","Turn to low and slowly pour in the slurry, stirring as you pour, until the broth is smooth and thick.","[4. Beef and egg] Still on low, slide the marinated beef in slice by slice. Don't stir right away — wait 20 seconds for the surface to set, then gently push apart, so the beef is tender and the broth stays clear.","Once the beef changes color (about 80% done), turn off the heat.","While it's still hot, drizzle the egg over the surface in a circle. Wait 10 seconds, then gently push with a ladle to form pretty cloud-like ribbons.","[5. Serve] Sprinkle with lots of chopped cilantro and a few drops of sesame oil, and serve."],"tips":["Cut across the grain: look at the grain of the beef and slice across it to cut the fibers — it'll melt in your mouth.","Thicken first, then add the beef: this shortens the beef's cooking time. If you add the beef first and thicken slowly, it overcooks and toughens.","Add the egg off the heat: for silky egg, never stir hard while boiling — egg cooked by residual heat is the most tender.","This soup is fantastic with rice or noodles! Want some side-dish suggestions to go with it?"]},"g3250":{"title":"Cantonese Silky Egg with Shredded Beef","intro":"A classic Cantonese home dish all about silkiness — silky beef and tender egg. With a bowl of hot plain congee, it's the ultimate comfort food.","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients","items":[["Star ingredient","shredded beef (about 150g - 200g); tenderloin or sirloin, cut across the grain so it isn't chewy"],["Eggs","3","egg mixture"],["Chopped green onion","as much as you like","egg mixture"],["Water","2 spoons","key slurry"],["Salt","a pinch","key slurry"],["White pepper","a pinch","key slurry"],["Potato starch","1 tsp (mixed well)","key slurry"]]}],"steps":["[Step 1: Pre-cook the beef] Heat the wok and add oil (a bit more than usual helps the beef “slide”).","When the oil is medium-hot, add the beef and separate quickly.","Key point: as soon as the surface changes color (about 70-80% done), lift it out and drain. Don't cook it through, or it'll be tough when it goes back in.","[Step 2: Egg mixture] Beat the remaining eggs with the key slurry and green onion.","Put the pre-cooked beef straight into the eggs and stir (the egg coats the beef so it heats more evenly).","[Step 3: Silky egg] Leave a little oil in the wok and turn to medium.","Pour in the egg and beef mixture.","Don't stir wildly! Let the bottom set slightly, then gently push from the edge toward the center with the spatula.","When the egg is about 80% set and still glossy and slightly runny, turn off the heat immediately.","Push it a couple more times with the residual heat and serve."],"tips":["[Beef marinade] Soy sauce: 1 tsp","Rice wine: 1 tsp","Potato starch: 1 tsp (locks in moisture)","Vegetable oil: 1 Tbsp (helps the strands separate in the pan without clumping)","Egg white: 1 spoon (take a little from the 3 eggs — it gives the beef a silky texture)","Beef congee upgrade: for softer beef, keep half the shredded beef and drop it straight into the congee 2 minutes before it's done, and use the other half for the silky egg on top — more layers of texture.","Removing beefy smell: if you find beef strong-smelling, a little ginger juice in the marinade works very well.","Do you have beef on hand now? Want me to help you substitute marinade ingredients with what you have at home?"]},"g3279":{"title":"Fu Yi Xuan-Style Egg Rolls (Thick Butter Cookies)","intro":"To make egg rolls like Chiayi's famous Fu Yi Xuan — thick, crunchy and full of egg flavor — the keys are the egg-to-butter ratio and controlling the gluten. Fu Yi Xuan's are thicker than ordinary egg rolls and have real bite.","groups":[{"name":"🥚 Ingredients (makes about 15-20)","items":[["Unsalted butter","200g","softened at room temperature"],["Caster sugar","120g","adjust to taste; Fu Yi Xuan's are on the sweet side"],["Whole eggs","4","about 200g, at room temperature"],["Cake (low-protein) flour","130g","sifted"],["Salt","1g","adds depth to the sweetness"],["Black sesame seeds","as you like","key for aroma"]]},{"name":"Cooking and rolling: preheat the egg roll iron and spoon in about 1 Tbsp batter.","items":[["Close the iron and press firmly; cook one side about","30-40 seconds, then flip and cook 20 seconds"],["While hot, roll quickly around a metal rod or long chopstick. Work fast — once it cools it becomes brittle and cracks",""]]}],"steps":["Cream the butter: beat the softened butter with the sugar and salt until pale and creamy. This is the key to crunchiness.","Add the eggs gradually: beat the eggs and add in 4-5 additions, mixing thoroughly each time so it doesn't split (if it looks thin and curdled, you added them too fast).","Fold in the flour: sift in the flour and fold with a spatula just until no dry flour remains. Don't overmix, or gluten develops and the rolls turn hard instead of crisp.","Add the sesame: stir in the black sesame and rest the batter 15 minutes so it evens out."],"tips":["For that crunchy-firm texture, use unsalted butter instead of vegetable oil.","The secret to thickness: don't press the iron too hard or too thin. Fu Yi Xuan's thickness comes from a higher batter ratio and leaving a little gap when rolling.","Toasty flavor: roll once the sheet is a slightly deeper golden brown — that's when the toasty egg-butter flavor comes out.","Cool completely: they're soft right after rolling; they only become very crisp after cooling completely on a rack.","Do you already have an egg roll iron? If you like, I can adapt this into a reduced-sugar or all-vegetable-oil version!"]},"g3390":{"title":"Homemade Pocari Sweat-Style Electrolyte Drink","intro":"Making a Pocari Sweat-style electrolyte drink at home is easy — the key is balancing sugar, salt and vitamins (acidity) to replace fluids and electrolytes. Here are two recipes: a copycat version closest to the store-bought one, and a more natural fresh-fruit version.","groups":[{"name":"1. Copycat version","items":[["Water","1000ml (room temperature or cooled boiled water)"],["Salt","2-3g (about half a teaspoon, for sodium)"],["White sugar","40-60g (to taste; provides energy and helps absorb water)"],["Lemon / lime juice","30-50ml (about 1 lemon, for potassium and vitamin C)"],["Honey","1 Tbsp (adds flavor; optional)"]]}],"steps":["Dissolve the sugar and salt completely in a little hot water (about 100ml).","Add the rest of the room-temperature water.","Add freshly squeezed lemon juice (remove the seeds, or it turns bitter).","Chill in the fridge before drinking — tastes best cold.","[2. Natural “healthy” version (about 500ml)] For faster potassium after exercise, use coconut water as the base.","Coconut water: 250ml (natural electrolytes)","Purified water: 250ml","Sea salt / rock salt: a pinch (about 1g)","Lemon juice: half a lemon","Honey: 1-2 tsp","Put everything in a water bottle.","Shake vigorously until mixed."],"tips":["Temperature: if using honey, keep the water below 45°C to protect its nutrients.","Fizzy version: want a sparkling Pocari? Just replace the water with soda water.","Emergency recipe: for diarrhea or serious dehydration, the WHO oral rehydration ratio is roughly 1 liter water + 6 tsp sugar + half a tsp salt.","Are you making this to rehydrate after exercise, or because you've been feeling unwell lately?"]},"g3460":{"title":"Molten Peanut Tangyuan (Glutinous Rice Balls)","intro":"The secret to peanut tangyuan that burst with molten filling isn't technique — it's the fat ratio. The peanut filling must be solid at room temperature (so you can wrap it) but turn liquid when heated (so it oozes).","groups":[],"steps":["[1. Molten peanut filling] This ratio is the key to the runny center. Lard gives the most authentic aroma and flows best; if you don't eat pork, use unsalted butter.","Roasted peanut powder: 100g (a slightly coarse one has better texture)","Powdered sugar: 60g (must be powdered sugar for a smooth texture)","Lard or unsalted butter (melted): 60g - 70g. Note: fat at about 60%-70% of the peanut powder's weight is the golden ratio for oozing.","Mix the peanut powder and powdered sugar.","Add the melted fat and stir into a thick paste.","Key step: freeze the peanut paste for 20-30 minutes.","While it's firm, quickly roll into small balls of about 8g - 10g each and return them to the freezer (they must be frozen hard to wrap easily).","[2. Dough] For a chewy skin that doesn't go mushy or tear, always use a cooked dough starter (guo cui, like a tangzhong).","Glutinous rice flour: 200g","Warm water: about 150ml - 160ml","Mix 20g of the glutinous rice flour with a little water and knead into a small piece of dough.","Drop it into boiling water and cook until it floats — this cooked piece is the guo cui.","Add the guo cui to the rest of the flour and add the warm water gradually, kneading into a dough as soft as an earlobe and not sticky. A skin with guo cui is very elastic and doesn't crack when wrapping.","[3. Wrapping] Take about 15g of dough, roll into a ball, flatten and press a hollow in the middle.","Put in one frozen peanut ball.","Close it up between your thumb and index finger and gently roll round.","Cooking: add the tangyuan to rapidly boiling water, turn to medium and cook until they float, then 1 more minute so the filling fully melts.","Peanut tangyuan are very high in calories: peanuts and lard are both rich, so 2-3 at a time is enough to satisfy a craving.","Avoid burns: the molten filling is extremely hot. If you have a mouth sore, hot oil will make it worse. Always bite a small opening first and let it cool a little."],"tips":["Add a little peanut butter: a heaped spoonful of chunky peanut butter in the filling adds thickness and sweet-salty depth for an even richer molten center.","Prevent cracking: if you're not cooking them right away, cover with a damp cloth or keep in an airtight box so the skins don't dry and crack — otherwise the filling leaks when boiled.","What sweet soup will you serve them in? (Unsweetened red bean soup or ginger syrup balances the sweetness of peanut tangyuan nicely!)"]},"g3462":{"title":"Sichuan Braising Spice Packet (Chuan Lu)","intro":"The heart of a homemade Sichuan braising packet is the balance of numbing, spicy, savory and fragrant. The biggest difference from Taiwanese braising is the large amount of Sichuan pepper and dried chilies, plus other spices for depth.","groups":[{"name":"Step 2: Fry the aromatics","items":[["Heat oil in a pot",""],["Add lots of green onion lengths",""],["ginger slices",""],["and whole garlic cloves and fry until fragrant",""],["Add","2 big spoonfuls of Pixian chili bean paste and fry on low until the oil turns red (the soul of Sichuan flavor)"]]}],"steps":["[1. Sichuan braising packet recipe (for 2-3 liters of water)] Take this list to a Chinese herbal shop, or buy the spices and put it together yourself.","Numbing and spicy: Da Hong Pao red Sichuan pepper 20g (the main aroma); green Sichuan pepper 10g (a fresher numbing note); dried chilies 30g (adjust to taste; cut them open for more flavor)","Aromatic layers: star anise 3-4; cassia (cinnamon) 1 small piece; cloves 3-5 (strong — don't use more); fennel seeds 10g (key for removing gaminess and adding aroma); black cardamom (tsaoko) 2 (crack open to release flavor); bay leaves 3-5; dried tangerine peel 1 small piece (cuts richness); white cardamom 3-5 (fresh aroma); licorice root 2 slices (sweet aftertaste, balances heat)","[Step 3: Caramel color] Heat a little oil and rock sugar in a pot on low until the sugar melts into a deep amber with tiny bubbles.","Add a bowl of hot water (careful — it spatters) and stir. This is your natural braising color.","[Step 4: Braising liquid] Put the fried aromatics in a large pot with water (or stock), the caramel, soy sauce and rice wine.","Add the spice packet.","Bring to a boil on high, then simmer on low for 40 minutes to fully release the spices — your Sichuan braising liquid is ready."],"tips":["Crack the tsaoko: the seeds inside give the aroma, but remove some of them or it can taste bitter.","Don't leave the packet in: once the braise is done, take the packet out rather than leaving it in the liquid until it cools, or the herbal flavor overpowers the meat.","For a heavier flavor: Sichuan braises are usually salty. If it doesn't taste rich enough, simmer a small piece of pork fat with it — the fat absorbs the numbing-spicy flavor and makes everything silkier.","Will you use this packet for meat (beef, pork) or vegetarian items like pressed tofu and kelp? I can help you adjust the saltiness for each."]},"g3463":{"title":"Custom Multigrain Rice (Pressure Cooker)","intro":"Your grain ratio is very high (about 31% white rice : 69% mixed grains) — a challenging, high-fiber, heavy multigrain rice! At this ratio, the red rice, black rice and 14-grain mix dominate the texture. To avoid a waxy or gritty result, for your 1:2.2 ratio I suggest these settings:","groups":[],"steps":["White rice cooks fast and grains cook slowly, so the biggest risk with lots of grains is white rice turning to mush while the grains stay hard. Try these:","Rinse together, soak longer: you can rinse them together, but soak at least 1 hour before cooking (3 hours is better). Soaking lets the red rice and 14-grain mix absorb water first, narrowing the gap with the white rice.","Add a “softener”: add 1 tsp olive oil and 1 tsp rice wine to the pot. Rice wine helps heat penetrate the hard hulls and adds aroma; olive oil makes high-fiber rice smoother.","Fluff it (very important): once the pressure is released and you open the lid, immediately fluff the rice from the bottom up with a rice paddle. This releases excess steam at the bottom so the bottom isn't mushy and the top isn't dry.","[Your golden formula, summarized] Rice cooked this way has the chew of red rice, the aroma of black rice, and the 14-grain mix fully cooked.","After cooking, do you usually eat it hot right away, or portion and freeze it for healthy lunchboxes? (For lunchboxes, use a little more water, since microwaving dries it out.)"],"tips":["Pressure time: 22 - 25 minutes. Why: 14-grain mixes usually contain beans (black beans, soybeans), and red rice has thick bran. Under 20 minutes the grains are too hard while the white rice turns pasty from the extra water; a longer time evens out the texture.","Natural release: at least 15 minutes. This lets the grains steam through fully. If the float valve hasn't dropped after 15 minutes, you can help release the rest manually.","Because your grain proportion is so high, don't skimp on water — use 1.5 times the volume of rice.","Example: 1 cup white rice + 2.2 cups grains (3.2 cups total) → 4.8 cups water.","Advanced: for an even softer texture, you can go up to 1.6 times the water."]},"g3550":{"title":"Beef with Green Peppers","intro":"Beef with green peppers is a stir-fry classic. The beef should be silky, the peppers crisp — and not bitter.","groups":[{"name":"🛒 Ingredients","items":[["Sliced beef (sirloin or flat iron recommended)","about 250g","main"],["Green bell peppers","1-2","main"],["Minced garlic","1 Tbsp","aromatics"],["Julienned ginger","a little","aromatics"],["Chili (optional)","","aromatics"]]},{"name":"Key marinade (the secret to silky beef)","items":[["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","1 tsp"],["Potato starch (or cornstarch)","1 tsp"],["Oil","1 tsp (added last to lock in moisture)"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Oyster sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar","a pinch"],["Black pepper","to taste"]]}],"steps":["Marinate the beef (most important): slice the beef across the grain, mix with the marinade except the oil, then mix in 1 tsp oil (stops the slices sticking together in the pan). Marinate 15 minutes.","Prep the peppers: seed and cut into strips. If you find green pepper too strong, blanch for 10 seconds in boiling water, or dry-fry briefly in a pan before cooking.","Pre-cook the beef: heat a bit more oil than usual, add the beef once hot and separate quickly. Cook until about 80% done (slightly pink) and remove immediately.","Aromatics and peppers: in the remaining oil, fry the garlic, ginger and chili. Add the peppers and stir-fry on high for about 30 seconds.","Combine and season: return the beef, add the seasoning (oyster sauce, sugar, black pepper) and toss quickly on high until the sauce coats everything. Serve."],"tips":["Cut across the grain: check the direction of the fibers and cut perpendicular to them, so the beef isn't chewy.","High heat, fast: don't cook the beef long once it's in — high heat all the way keeps it tender and the peppers bright green.","Add shacha: for a more Taiwanese flavor, add 1 Tbsp shacha sauce with the seasoning for more depth.","Great as a lunchbox dish — still delicious reheated the next day!","Would you like tips on choosing beef cuts for stir-frying, or on thickening without potato starch?"]},"g3582":{"title":"(Not a recipe) Washing Your Face with Hot Water and Demodex Mites","intro":"Regularly washing your face with very hot water doesn't help against Demodex mites — it actually does serious harm. It's a common misconception that heat kills bacteria or parasites, but on facial skin it usually backfires. Here's why:","groups":[{"name":"The facts","items":[["Lethal temperature","research shows that killing Demodex mites usually takes temperatures of 54°C to 58°C"],["What skin can take","a comfortable face-washing temperature is about 30°C to 35°C. Washing with water near 60°C would give your skin a second-degree burn or serious damage first, while the mites deep in the follicles might be just fine"]]}],"steps":["Cool it down physically: when your face is hot and red, use a cool (not ice-cold) compress to constrict the blood vessels.","Reduce oil buildup: a rinse-off cleanser with salicylic acid helps dissolve oil clogging the pores — more effective than hot water and gentler on the skin.","Deal with mites in your environment: changing your pillowcase every day (or laying a clean towel on it and changing that daily) helps more than hot face washes."],"tips":["If you already have enough mites to cause symptoms (redness, pustules), hot water is the biggest aggravating factor:","Dilated blood vessels: hot water makes facial capillaries dilate quickly. Demodex causes skin inflammation, and dilated vessels add heat and inflammation, making your face redder, more swollen and itchier.","Nerve irritation: skin with mite problems usually has a fragile barrier, and hot water directly irritates damaged nerve endings, causing burning and stinging.","Rebound oiliness: hot water strips the oils from your skin. When skin feels overly dry and its barrier is damaged, it protects itself by producing even more oil.","More food for mites: Demodex feeds on oil (sebum). The hotter you wash and the oilier your skin, the bigger the buffet for the mites.","Face: use cool or room-temperature water (about 25°C-30°C). It calms blood vessels, reduces inflammation and protects the skin barrier.","Bedding: what really needs hot water is your bedding (pillowcases, sheets). Wash weekly at 60°C or above and tumble dry to cut off reinfection from your environment.","After washing your face now, does your skin feel tight and dry, or does it get oily again quickly? That tells you whether hot water has already damaged your skin barrier."]},"g3598":{"title":"Yakiniku Don (Japanese Grilled Beef Rice Bowl)","intro":"To turn grilled beef into a hot bowl of yakiniku don, besides cooking the meat well, the key is combining caramelized onions with the essential sauce.","groups":[{"name":"Mix these together","items":[["Soy sauce","3 Tbsp"],["Mirin","2 Tbsp"],["Rice wine or sake","1 Tbsp"],["Sugar","1 Tbsp"],["Water","2-3 Tbsp (add more if you like extra sauce over the rice)"],["Advanced","add a little grated garlic or ginger for a more Japanese-diner taste"]]}],"steps":["Fry the onion: heat a little oil and cook the sliced onion until translucent. For more flavor, cook until the edges caramelize.","Sear the beef: add the beef slices and cook until about 70% done.","Reduce the sauce: pour in the sauce and bring to a boil on high. It will thicken and coat the beef and onion evenly.","[4. Secrets for more depth] Torched flavor: if you have a kitchen torch, flame the surface of the meat after arranging it for a strong charcoal-grilled aroma.","Sides to cut the richness: add a few slices of pickled cucumber, red pickled ginger or kimchi on the side of the bowl to balance the rich beef.","The rice: sprinkle a little crumbled nori on the rice before adding the meat.","Want to know how to make a perfect Japanese onsen egg to go with it? I can share an easy no-stove method!"],"tips":["The difference between yakiniku don and just eating grilled beef is the saucy smoothness and the layers:","Sliced onion: 1/2, sliced along the grain the other way (softens easily and releases sweetness).","Essential toppings (pick one): onsen egg / raw yolk — adds silkiness and coats the rice when mixed; green onion and white sesame — more aroma; shichimi pepper — a little heat.","Lay the onion on the rice first, then pile the beef on top (the “meat mountain” effect).","Finally pour over the reduced sauce left in the pan."]},"g3752":{"title":"Italian Olive Oil Bread Dips","intro":"Here are some delicious, classic Italian dips — perfect with your freshly baked focaccia, toast or other bread!","groups":[{"name":"1. Classic balsamic dipping oil","items":[["Extra-virgin olive oil","1/4 cup (about 60 ml)"],["Balsamic vinegar","1 Tbsp (adjust to taste)"],["Garlic, finely minced","1/2 tsp"],["Dried oregano","1/4 tsp"],["Dried basil","1/4 tsp"],["Crushed red pepper flakes","a pinch (adjust the heat to taste)"],["Sea salt","a pinch"],["Black pepper","a pinch"]]},{"name":"2. Basil pesto","items":[["Fresh basil leaves","2 cups (about 50 g)","Genovese basil is best"],["Pine nuts","1/4 cup (lightly toasted)"],["Parmesan, grated","1/2 cup"],["Garlic","2 cloves"],["Extra-virgin olive oil","1/2 cup (about 120 ml)"],["Salt and black pepper","to taste"]]},{"name":"3. Roasted garlic dipping oil","items":[["Whole garlic heads","2-3"],["Extra-virgin olive oil","1/2 cup (about 120 ml)"],["Dried rosemary or thyme","1 tsp"],["Salt and black pepper","a pinch"]]},{"name":"Making the dip","items":[["Take the roasted garlic out and let it cool",""],["Squeeze the soft cloves out of their skins into a small bowl",""],["Add","1/2 cup olive oil"],["herbs",""],["salt and pepper",""],["Mash the cloves with a fork and mix well with the oil",""],["Serve with warm bread",""]]}],"steps":["Put all the ingredients in a small shallow dish.","Stir gently with a small spoon or fork to combine.","Let it sit about 5-10 minutes so the herbs release their flavor.","Serve with fresh bread.","[2. Basil pesto] Pesto is versatile and full of flavor — great for dipping bread or spreading on bread and toasting.","Blend the dry ingredients: put the basil, pine nuts, Parmesan and garlic in a food processor (or blender).","Blend until finely chopped and evenly mixed.","Add the oil slowly: with the machine running, pour the olive oil in through the feed tube in a slow, steady stream until the pesto is as smooth as you like.","Season with salt and black pepper.","Use the pesto right away, or store in an airtight container in the fridge with a layer of olive oil on top.","[3. Roasted garlic dipping oil] Roasted garlic becomes soft and sweet with no harsh bite — perfect with bread.","Roast the garlic: cut off the tops of the heads to expose the cloves. Drizzle with a little olive oil and sprinkle with salt. Wrap in foil and roast in a 200°C preheated oven or air fryer for about 30-40 minutes until the cloves are very soft and golden brown."],"tips":["The classic balsamic dip is the simplest and most common bread dip — the quality of the oil and vinegar is what matters.","Would you like tips on making sourdough sandwich bread, or are you interested in soup recipes?"]},"g3754":{"title":"No-Knead Focaccia","intro":"There are many focaccia recipes; here's a classic, no-knead and relatively easy one. The keys are a high-hydration dough and a long fermentation, which give beautiful large holes.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Bread flour","250 g"],["Warm water (about 30°C)","190 g","about 76% hydration"],["Instant dry yeast","2 g (about 1/2 tsp)"],["Salt","4 g (about 2/3 tsp)"],["Extra-virgin olive oil","20 g (for the dough)"],["Extra-virgin olive oil","as needed (for the pan and top)"],["Rosemary","a little","fresh or dried"],["Sea salt / flaky salt","a little","sprinkled on top"]]}],"steps":["Mix the dough: in a large bowl, gently mix the warm water and yeast and let stand 5-10 minutes to activate. Add the flour, salt and 20 g olive oil. Stir with a spatula or chopsticks until there's no dry flour. The dough will be very wet and sticky — that's normal. Cover with cling film.","First rise (folds): leave at room temperature for 30 minutes. Do the first stretch and fold: with wet hands, grab the dough from the bottom edge and fold it to the middle. Turn 90 degrees and repeat until all four sides are folded. Cover, and repeat every 30 minutes for 3 sets of folds in total (about 90 minutes).","Long cold ferment: put the dough in an oiled container, cover tightly and refrigerate for 12 to 24 hours.","Shape and warm up: line a baking pan with parchment (or use a flat pan) and coat evenly with olive oil. Take the dough out of the fridge and tip it gently into the pan. Let it come to room temperature for about 1-2 hours (until slightly puffy and soft). Gently press and stretch it with your fingers to fill the pan evenly.","Final rise and dimpling: when the dough has nearly doubled, it's ready to bake. Drizzle olive oil evenly over the top. With your fingertips (like playing the piano), press deep, even dimples all over the dough — that's where focaccia's signature holes come from. Sprinkle with rosemary and coarse sea salt.","Bake: preheat the oven to 220°C. Bake the focaccia for about 20-25 minutes until golden on top and colored underneath. Immediately take it out of the pan and cool on a rack."],"tips":["Sticky dough is normal: focaccia is a high-hydration dough; wet or oil your hands to stop sticking.","Overproofed sourdough: as you mentioned, if a dough overproofs and loses its gas, you can turn it into focaccia. Focaccia needs a wetter, softer dough anyway, so overproofed dough stretches into the pan more easily.","Baking time: adjust for your oven. If the top browns too fast, lower the temperature slightly."]},"g3770":{"title":"Braised Beef Brisket in a Rice Cooker","intro":"Sorry about that — I understand you want a recipe for stewing beef brisket in an electric (Tatung) cooker. Brisket (from the chest or belly, like brisket or flank) has lots of connective tissue, perfect for long stewing until tender. The cooker's steady heat suits this dish very well.","groups":[{"name":"🌟 Main ingredients","items":[["Beef brisket","500 g","cut into 3-4 cm chunks."],["Ginger slices","5-6"],["Green onion","1 stalk, cut into lengths"]]},{"name":"🍜 Spice packet (optional, for more flavor)","items":[["Star anise","2"],["Bay leaves","2"],["Dried chilies","3-5","for a little heat; optional."]]},{"name":"🧂 Broth and seasoning","items":[["Soy sauce","100 ml","about half a rice cup; for saltiness."],["Dark soy sauce / thick soy sauce","1 Tbsp","for color."],["Rock sugar","1 Tbsp","adds sweetness and helps color."],["Rice wine / cooking wine","30 ml","removes gaminess."],["Water","enough to cover the beef","about 800 - 1000 ml."]]}],"steps":["[1. Blanch] Put the brisket in cold water with a few slices of ginger and a little rice wine.","Bring to a boil on high and keep boiling about 5-8 minutes until lots of scum and blood rise.","Lift out the brisket and rinse off the scum with warm water.","[2. Into the pot] [Recommended] Brown first: if you have time, stir-fry the ginger, green onion and brisket in a wok with a little oil until the beef is lightly browned. Add the soy sauce and rock sugar and fry until fragrant, then transfer to the inner pot.","[Simplified] Straight in: put the prepared brisket, ginger, green onion, spices and all the seasonings directly into the inner pot.","Water: add enough water to fully cover the brisket.","[3. Cooking] Brisket has a lot of connective tissue and needs a long time to soften, so do two heating cycles:","First heating: put 2 cups of water (about 300 ml) in the outer pot. Cover the inner pot and the cooker and press the switch.","First rest: when the switch pops up, don't open the lid; let it sit in the residual heat for 45 minutes to 1 hour.","Second heating: open the outer lid, add 1.5 cups of water (about 200 ml) to the outer pot and press the switch again.","Second rest: when it pops up, rest another hour.","Total heating and resting is about 3 hours. Poke the brisket with a chopstick — if it goes through easily, it's very tender.","[4. Soak] To let the brisket fully absorb the braising liquid and firm up so it slices without falling apart, let it cool completely in the liquid — ideally soak overnight.","Reheated the next day, the flavor and tenderness are at their best. If you like a thicker sauce, reduce the braising liquid on the stove before pouring it over the brisket."],"tips":[]},"g3774":{"title":"Sichuan Spicy Braised Beef Shank","intro":"Here's a recipe for numbing-spicy, fragrant Sichuan-style braised beef shank. The key is lots of dried chilies, Sichuan pepper and spices for Sichuan cooking's signature numbing (ma) and spicy (la) flavors.","groups":[{"name":"🌟 Main ingredients","items":[["Beef shank","1-2 pieces (about 800g - 1000g)"],["Ginger slices","5-6"],["Green onion","3-4 lengths"]]},{"name":"🌶️ Sichuan spice packet","items":[["Dried chilies (facing-heaven / erjingtiao)","20-30","for heat."],["Sichuan peppercorns (red or green)","2-3 Tbsp","for the numbing sensation; adjust to taste."],["Star anise","3-4","classic aroma."],["Cassia bark","1 small piece","rich cinnamon aroma."],["Bay leaves","3-4","adds depth."],["Fennel seeds","1 tsp","adds spice."],["Black cardamom (tsaoko)","1 (cracked)","deep aroma."],["White cardamom","4-5","fresh aroma."]]},{"name":"🍜 Broth and seasoning","items":[["Water","enough to cover the beef","about 1500 - 2000 ml."],["Soy sauce","150 ml (about 1 rice cup)","main source of color and saltiness."],["Oyster sauce","2 Tbsp","adds body and umami."],["Rock sugar","1.5 - 2 Tbsp","brightens and gives a sweet aftertaste."],["Rice wine / cooking wine","50 ml","removes gaminess."],["Pixian chili bean paste (doubanjiang)","1-2 Tbsp","the soul of Sichuan flavor! adds savoriness and red color."]]}],"steps":["[1. Blanch] Put the beef shank in cold water with a few slices of ginger and a little rice wine.","Bring to a boil on high and keep boiling about 5-10 minutes until lots of scum and blood rise.","Lift out the shank and rinse off the scum with warm water.","[2. Braising liquid] Heat a little oil (about 2 Tbsp) in a pot.","Add the ginger, green onion, Sichuan peppercorns and dried chilies and fry slowly on low until the numbing pepper and chili aromas come out (don't burn them).","Add the chili bean paste and fry until the oil turns red and fragrant.","Add the soy sauce, oyster sauce, rice wine, rock sugar and water and bring to a boil.","Add the rest of the spices (star anise, cassia, etc.).","[3. Braise] Put the shank into the boiling liquid, making sure it's fully covered.","When it boils again, turn to the lowest heat (barely simmering).","Cover and braise about 1.5 - 2 hours, until a chopstick goes through easily (but it still holds its shape).","[4. Soak] Turn off the heat! This is the key to great braised shank. Let it cool completely in the liquid, ideally soaking overnight (at least 6-8 hours). Cooling and soaking let it absorb the flavor and firm up so it doesn't fall apart when sliced.","[5. Slice] Take out the fully cooled shank and drain.","Slice thinly across the grain (about 0.3 - 0.5 cm thick).","Drizzle with a little cooled braising liquid, or make a Sichuan dipping sauce (chili oil, Sichuan pepper oil, minced garlic, a little soy sauce, black vinegar).","Braised shank made this way is numbing, spicy and fragrant, tender but not falling apart — perfect as a cold appetizer or with drinks!"],"tips":["(Tip: put all the dried spices in a muslin bag to make a spice packet.)"]},"g3777":{"title":"Chicken with Pickled Cucumber (Rice Cooker)","intro":"Of course! Making soy-braised chicken with pickled cucumber (the rice-friendly version) in an electric cooker is the easiest, most time-saving way. The gentle steaming makes the chicken tender and flavorful, and the sauce concentrates.","groups":[{"name":"🌟 Ingredients","items":[["Chicken thighs","2 (about 600g)","boneless thighs are best — more tender; cut into bite-size pieces."],["Canned pickled cucumber","1/3 can or to taste","Taiwanese canned pickled cucumber (e.g. Da Mao brand) or similar soy-pickled cucumber, diced."],["Pickled cucumber liquid","3 - 4 Tbsp","the main source of saltiness and flavor."],["Ginger slices","5","for aroma."],["Garlic","3-5 cloves","smashed or minced."]]},{"name":"🧂 Seasoning","items":[["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp","adds color and depth."],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp","removes off-flavors, adds aroma."],["Rock sugar / sugar","1/2 tsp","brightens and rounds out the flavor."],["Water","3 Tbsp","helps it steam and gives room to reduce."]]}],"steps":["[1. Prep] If using bone-in chicken, blanch it first (bring to a boil from cold and skim) or rinse well with hot water to remove impurities for a cleaner result.","Boneless thighs can be used as is.","[2. Into the pot] Put the chicken pieces in the inner pot.","Add the ginger, garlic, diced pickled cucumber and its liquid, soy sauce, rice wine, rock sugar and water.","Stir so all the seasoning coats the chicken.","[3. Cook] Add about 1.5 ~ 2 cups of water (about 200-250 ml) to the outer pot.","Put the inner pot in the cooker, cover and press the switch.","When the switch pops up, don't open the lid right away; let it rest 10-15 minutes so the chicken gets more tender and flavorful.","[4. Finish] Open the cooker and take out the inner pot (careful, it's hot).","If you like a thicker sauce, pour the liquid into a pan and reduce on high for about 2-3 minutes so it gets rich and clings to the chicken.","Taste: if too mild, add a little more pickle liquid; if too salty, add a little water.","✨ Serve! This savory chicken is perfect with rice, and the sauce over rice is delicious.","👉 Tip: adjust the amount of pickled cucumber and liquid to taste — more sugar if you like it sweeter, more pickle liquid if you like it saltier!"],"tips":[]},"g3792":{"title":"Taiwanese Pepper Salt for Popcorn Chicken","intro":"You've hit on the essence of the dish! The pepper salt on Taiwanese popcorn chicken is so good because it isn't just salt and pepper — it's a blend of several spices that adds layers: salty, fragrant, sweet and numbing.","groups":[{"name":"Professional pepper salt usually includes Chinese spices such as","items":[["Cinnamon (or cassia)","a warm, distinctive sweet aroma — one reason popcorn chicken smells so good"],["Fennel / star anise / cloves / Sichuan pepper","the basic components of five-spice, giving a complex, rich spice base. Sichuan pepper also adds a light numbing feel"]]},{"name":"🍽️ Homemade popcorn chicken pepper salt","items":[["Salt","2 Tbsp","sea salt or fine salt"],["White pepper","2 Tbsp","the main aroma"],["Black pepper","1 Tbsp","more heat; add more if you like it spicy"],["Licorice powder","1 tsp","a lingering sweetness — essential"],["Cinnamon","1/2 tsp","the special Taiwanese aroma"],["Five-spice powder","1/2 tsp","a complex base"],["MSG (monosodium glutamate)","1/2 tsp","umami; adjust or omit to taste"],["Garlic powder (optional)","1/2 tsp","garlic aroma; omit if you prefer"]]}],"steps":["Measure all the ingredients (salt, pepper, spice powders) and put them in a small dry pan.","On the lowest heat, slowly stir and toast all the powders for about 1-2 minutes.","Toast until very fragrant (careful not to burn it).","Turn off the heat, let cool and store in an airtight jar.","This homemade pepper salt recreates the classic Taiwanese night-market flavor on popcorn chicken, fried tofu, fried sweet potato or grilled meat!","Ready to make the pepper salt, or would you like the marinade recipe for the popcorn chicken itself?"],"tips":["White pepper: the main aroma and gentle heat.","Black pepper: stronger heat and deeper flavor.","Salt: the basic saltiness.","This is the biggest difference between Taiwanese pepper salt and ordinary pepper salt — and why it's addictive:","Licorice powder: a unique sweetness and lingering aftertaste that makes the flavor softer and rounder.","MSG or other seasonings: strong umami — the chemical key to “delicious”.","Garlic powder or chili powder: adds kick and appetite.","Sweet-potato starch (or cornstarch): some recipes add starch to make the powder fluffier or stick better.","Try the recipe below and adjust the proportions to find your favorite. Toast all the powders together on low for about 1-2 minutes to release the spice aromas — even more fragrant!"]},"g3836":{"title":"Lamb Chop Marinades (Two Ways)","intro":"There are many ways to marinate lamb chops, depending on the flavor you like. Here are two common, delicious methods:","groups":[{"name":"1. Herb marinade — ingredients","items":[["Lamb chops","as needed"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Olive oil","about 3 Tbsp"],["Fresh rosemary","2-3 sprigs (or 1-2 tsp dried)"],["Fresh thyme","2-3 sprigs (or 1 tsp dried)"],["Minced garlic","3-4 cloves"],["Sea salt","to taste (depending on the thickness)"],["Black pepper","to taste"]]},{"name":"2. Cumin spice marinade — ingredients","items":[["Lamb chops","as needed"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Cumin seeds / ground cumin","2 Tbsp"],["Chili powder (optional)","1 Tbsp"],["Garlic powder / minced garlic","1 tsp"],["Ginger slices / grated ginger","a little"],["Onion","1/4, cut into strips"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Salt","to taste"],["White / black pepper","a pinch"],["A little oil (vegetable or olive)",""]]}],"steps":["[1. Herb marinade] This brings out the natural sweetness of the lamb with fresh herb flavor.","Prep the chops: pat them dry with paper towels.","Make the marinade: mix the olive oil, garlic, chopped rosemary and thyme, sea salt and black pepper in a bowl.","Coat: spread the marinade evenly on both sides and massage it in so everything is covered.","Marinate: put the chops in a zip bag or covered container in the fridge. Recommended time: at least 1 hour, up to 4 hours. Don't marinate too long or it affects the texture.","Before cooking: take them out of the fridge and leave at room temperature for about 15-20 minutes before cooking.","[2. Cumin spice marinade] Rich cumin and spice flavor — a common style for Chinese barbecue and Xinjiang-style lamb.","Prep the chops: pat dry.","Mix the marinade: combine all the seasonings (cumin, chili powder, garlic, ginger, onion, soy sauce, salt, pepper and oil) in a bowl.","Coat: spread evenly over the chops and massage so the onion juices and spices soak in.","Marinate: refrigerate for 1-2 hours.","Cook: before grilling, brush off some of the bits of marinade so they don't burn, then grill.","[📌 Tips] Pat dry: always dry the chops with paper towels before marinating so the marinade sticks and soaks in better.","Massage: gently massaging in the marinade helps tenderize the meat and carries the flavor deeper.","Marinating time: lamb chops are fairly tender, so usually don't marinate more than 4 hours — longer can make the meat dry.","Which marinade would you like to try, or shall I find more lamb chop recipes?"],"tips":[]},"g3845":{"title":"Napa Cabbage Three Ways (No Shrimp, No Pork)","intro":"No problem! Napa cabbage is naturally very sweet, so even without dried shrimp or pork, mushrooms, tofu products or butter and milk can make it delicious. Here are 3 shrimp-free, pork-free napa cabbage recipes, from rich to light:","groups":[{"name":"1. Braised napa cabbage with mushrooms and tofu skin — ingredients","items":[["Napa cabbage","half a head (large pieces)"],["Dried shiitake mushrooms","6-8 (the key ingredient; soak, then slice; keep the soaking water)"],["Fried tofu skin","3 sheets (wide strips)"],["Carrot","a little (sliced, for color)"],["Wood ear mushroom","a little (for crunch)"],["Ginger slices","3-4"],["Seasoning","thick soy sauce (or vegetarian oyster sauce), white pepper (the soul of this dish), sesame oil"]]},{"name":"2. Creamy napa cabbage — ingredients","items":[["Napa cabbage","half a head (small pieces)"],["Garlic","4-5 cloves (smashed; use mushrooms if you avoid garlic)"],["Butter","20g"],["Milk","200ml"],["Flour","1 Tbsp (for thickness)"],["Other add-ins (optional)","corn kernels, sliced mushrooms"]]},{"name":"3. Hot and sour napa cabbage — ingredients","items":[["Napa cabbage","1/3 head (use only the stems for this dish — save the leaves for soup; the stems stay crunchy)"],["Dried chilies","5-6 pieces"],["Sichuan peppercorns","a little (optional)"],["Sliced garlic",""],["Minced ginger",""],["Seasoning","black vinegar (or aged vinegar), sugar, salt, starch slurry"]]}],"steps":["[1. Braised with mushrooms and tofu skin] Aromatics: heat a bit more oil than usual and fry the ginger and shiitake on medium-low until golden and fragrant.","Stir-fry: add the carrot and wood ear, then the cabbage stems and cook until soft.","Braise: add the remaining leaves and tofu skin, then the mushroom water and enough water to come halfway up the vegetables.","Season: add 1 Tbsp thick soy sauce and salt to taste. Cover and simmer on low for 15 minutes until the cabbage is very soft.","Finish: sprinkle with plenty of white pepper (warming and flavorful) and a little sesame oil.","[2. Creamy napa cabbage (Western / Hong Kong style)] A “white sauce” dish loved by adults and kids — the creaminess perfectly sets off the cabbage's sweetness, and it's easier than you think.","Soften: heat regular oil, fry the garlic, add the cabbage and cook until soft and watery, then set aside (discard the liquid).","Make the roux: in the same pan on low, melt the butter, stir in the flour (it becomes a paste), then slowly add the milk a little at a time, stirring, until you have a smooth white sauce.","Combine: return the softened cabbage (and corn/mushrooms) to the sauce.","Simmer: cook on low for about 3-5 minutes so the cabbage is coated in the sauce.","Season: with salt and black pepper. (For a stretchy finish, top with shredded cheese and bake briefly.)","[3. Hot and sour napa cabbage (high-heat stir-fry)] If you want crunch, this is like vinegar-glazed cabbage, but napa cabbage stays juicier and more tender.","Cutting: slice the stems on the diagonal (cuts the fibers so it absorbs flavor better and has a better texture).","Sauce: in a bowl, mix 2 spoons black vinegar, 1 spoon sugar, 1 spoon soy sauce, a pinch of salt and a little starch.","Aromatics: put oil in a hot pan and fry the Sichuan peppercorns and dried chilies on low, remove the peppercorns so you don't bite into bitterness, then add the garlic and ginger.","Stir-fry: turn to maximum heat and toss the stems quickly for 1 minute (not longer, or they release water).","Sauce: pour in the sauce and toss quickly; once it coats the cabbage and turns glossy, serve.","Don't skimp on oil: napa cabbage soaks up fat, so without meat use a little more oil than usual, or add oily tofu products like fried tofu skin or gluten, so it doesn't taste dry.","Use “umami bombs”: without dried shrimp, dried shiitake and dried wood ear are the best flavor boosters. Always fry the dried shiitake until dry and toasty — the broth will be as rich as meat stock.","Bouillon / stock: if you don't mind chicken products, replace the water with chicken stock or add a chicken bouillon cube — instant upgrade."],"tips":[]},"g3847":{"title":"Kung Pao Chicken","intro":"Kung Pao chicken is a Sichuan classic. Its soul is the “scorched-chili lychee flavor” — first a gentle sweet-sour taste (like lychee), then the numbing Sichuan pepper and the heat of dried chilies. For tender chicken, crunchy peanuts and a rich clinging sauce, the key move is a premixed sauce (“bowl sauce”).","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Tip","always use chicken thigh for tenderness; breast dries out easily"],["Peanuts","half a bowl (skinned, roasted; ready-made plain or spicy peanuts are easiest)"],["Leek / large green onion","1-2 (cut into about 1.5cm lengths)"]]},{"name":"Aromatics","items":[["Dried chilies","1 handful (cut into pieces; remove the seeds if you're afraid of heat)"],["Sichuan peppercorns","1 tsp (Da Hong Pao is best, for the numbing)"],["Garlic","3 cloves (sliced)"],["Ginger","5-6 slices"]]},{"name":"Marinade (the key to tender chicken)","items":[["Soy sauce","1 tsp"],["Rice wine","1 tsp"],["Potato starch","1 tsp"],["Water","a little (so the meat absorbs moisture)"],["Oil","1 tsp (added last to seal in juices)"]]},{"name":"Golden-ratio “bowl sauce” (always mix ahead)","items":[["Sugar","2 Tbsp"],["Black vinegar (or aged vinegar)","2 Tbsp (1:1 sugar to vinegar is the basis of the lychee flavor)"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp"],["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Potato starch","1 tsp"],["Water","2 Tbsp"]]}],"steps":["[Step 1: Marinate the chicken] Put the diced chicken in a bowl with the soy sauce, rice wine, water and starch.","Mix by hand until the liquid is absorbed and the chicken feels tacky.","Mix in a little oil (stops sticking in the pan) and leave for 15 minutes.","[Step 2: Pre-cook the chicken] Heat a bit more oil than usual and add the chicken once hot.","Stir-fry on medium-high, separating the pieces, until the chicken changes color (about 70-80% done), then remove and drain. This keeps it tender — don't cook it through.","[Step 3: Make the scorched-chili aroma (control the heat)] Leave a little oil in the wok and turn to low (it must be low, or it turns bitter).","Add the Sichuan peppercorns and dried chilies and fry slowly until the chilies are deep reddish-brown and smell strongly pungent.","Add the ginger, garlic and white parts of the leek and fry until fragrant.","[Step 4: High heat and sauce] Turn to high and return the half-cooked chicken.","Toss a few times so the chicken absorbs the chili aroma.","Pour in the bowl sauce (stir it first — the starch settles).","Toss quickly — the sauce instantly thickens and coats the chicken.","[Step 5: Finish] Turn off the heat (or to the lowest).","Add the peanuts.","Toss twice in the residual heat and serve immediately. Key: always add the peanuts last so they stay crunchy — they go soft if they sit in the sauce.","Sugar to vinegar 1:1: Kung Pao chicken isn't just spicy — it's sweet-sour with heat. You need enough sugar and vinegar for the “lychee flavor”.","Don't skip the Sichuan pepper: the numbing kick is the essence of this dish. If you can't find peppercorns, a drizzle of Sichuan pepper oil before serving works.","Learn the bowl-sauce trick: this dish is a fast high-heat stir-fry. If you add sugar, vinegar and soy sauce one by one, the chicken overcooks. Mix the sauce in a bowl, pour it in at once, and it's done in 30 seconds."],"tips":["This dish with rice is unbeatable!","How much heat can you handle? If you don't like it too spicy, I can show you how to treat the dried chilies so they add aroma without much heat."]},"g3848":{"title":"Hot and Sour Vinegar Cabbage","intro":"A very appetizing, rice-friendly home dish that balances sour, spicy, sweet and crunchy. To make it like a restaurant — crisp, not soggy, with real wok hei — the keys are high heat and when you add the vinegar.","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredient","items":[["Tip","tear the cabbage by hand instead of cutting — the uneven edges hold more sauce and taste better"]]},{"name":"Aromatics","items":[["Dried chilies","5-6 (cut into pieces; seeded is nicer)"],["Garlic","3 cloves (sliced)"],["Sichuan peppercorns","1 tsp (the soul! optional, but it gives a restaurant-quality aroma)"]]},{"name":"Golden-ratio sauce (mix ahead)","items":[["Black vinegar (or aged vinegar)","2 Tbsp (main sourness and aroma)"],["White vinegar","1 tsp (brighter tang; optional)"],["Soy sauce","1 Tbsp (umami)"],["Sugar","1 Tbsp (the key — sweet and sour must balance)"],["Salt","a pinch"],["Starch slurry","a little (for a light glaze; skip for a lighter dish)"]]},{"name":"Step 1: Prep (key)","items":[["Tear the cabbage","wash the cabbage and tear it by hand into bite-size pieces"],["Drain well","very important! After washing, spin or pat it as dry as possible. Wet cabbage cools the wok and ends up boiled instead of crisp"]]}],"steps":["[Step 2: Pepper oil] Put oil in a cold wok with the Sichuan peppercorns and fry slowly on low until fragrant.","When the peppercorns darken and smell fragrant, scoop them out and discard (so you don't bite into them), leaving the fragrant oil. If you don't mind them, you can leave them in.","[Step 3: Aromatics] Add the dried chilies and garlic to the pepper oil.","Fry on low until the garlic is lightly golden and the chilies change color (careful — dried chilies burn easily).","[Step 4: Stir-fry] Turn to maximum heat and add the cabbage.","Toss quickly for about 1-2 minutes. When it shrinks slightly and turns glossy bright green, it's perfect. Don't cook it until completely limp.","Pour in the premixed sauce (around the edge of the wok so the vinegar sizzles and releases its aroma).","Keep the heat high and toss quickly (about 20 seconds).","If you like the sauce to cling, drizzle in a little starch slurry now (optional).","Turn off the heat and serve!","Why black vinegar? Vinegar-glazed dishes are about the mellow aroma of aged vinegar. White vinegar is sour but its aroma is thin and sharp. Use mostly black vinegar with a little white.","Don't skip the sugar: it's not just sour — you need enough sugar to soften the vinegar's sharpness and create a sweet-sour balance.","High heat all the way: once the cabbage is in, turn the heat as high as it goes. The longer it takes, the more water the cabbage releases and the chewier it gets instead of crisp."],"tips":["The hot-and-sour aroma when it comes out of the wok is irresistible!","Do you like the numbing Sichuan pepper aroma in this dish? If you don't have peppercorns, I can suggest other ways to add fragrance."]},"g3854":{"title":"Thai Basil Beef (Pad Kra Pao Neua)","intro":"Switching Thai basil pork to beef (pad kra pao neua) is easy — the seasoning logic is the same, but beef has different fat and texture, so a few tweaks in the cut and heat give an even richer, beefier rice-friendly dish.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Ground beef","300g (or flat iron / short rib cut into small dice)"],["Holy basil (kra pao)","1 big handful (Thai basil works if you can't find it, but the authentic flavor comes from holy basil)"],["Minced garlic","5 cloves","aromatics"],["Chilies","3-5 (minced)","aromatics"],["Shallots","2 (sliced, optional)","aromatics"],["Eggs","2 (for fried eggs)"]]},{"name":"Golden-ratio sauce (mix ahead)","items":[["Oyster sauce","1.5 Tbsp"],["Fish sauce","1 Tbsp (the saltiness — and the soul of Thai flavor)"],["Light soy sauce","1 tsp (for color)"],["Dark soy sauce","0.5 tsp (for deep color; optional)"],["Sugar","1 tsp (balances the saltiness)"],["Water","1 Tbsp"]]},{"name":"Step 4: Sauce and reduce","items":[["Pour in the golden-ratio sauce",""]]},{"name":"Toss quickly on high so the sauce coats every bit of beef. Beef shouldn't cook long — work fast and reduce the liquid just slightly.","items":[["Note","some like it saucy for mixing with rice — don't reduce too much; for a drier, toastier version, cook it down more"]]}],"steps":["Choosing the meat (fat ratio): pork is usually 30:70 fat to lean. Beef that's too lean is dry, so buy ground beef with about 20% fat (80/20). If you worry it's dry, use a bit more oil when cooking. Advanced: the beef version is great with hand-chopped beef instead of ground — chewier, and you taste the sweetness of the beef.","Heat control (most important): beef releases water fast. If the pan isn't hot enough, it boils instead of searing. Keep the heat high and work fast the whole time.","Flavor: beef has a stronger flavor than pork, so use a little more garlic and chili to balance it and bring out the meatiness.","[Step 1: Fried eggs] Heat plenty of oil and shallow-fry the eggs until the edges are crisp; set aside.","[Step 2: Aromatics] In the remaining oil (add more if needed), on medium heat, fry the garlic, chilies and shallots until strongly fragrant.","[Step 3: Sear the beef] Turn to maximum heat and add the ground beef.","Key technique: don't stir right away — spread it flat and sear 15-20 seconds to brown the bottom (Maillard reaction), so the beef doesn't keep releasing water.","Then stir-fry quickly, breaking it up, until it changes color (about 80% done).","[Step 5: Basil] Turn off the heat (or to the lowest).","Add a big handful of holy basil (or Thai basil).","Toss a few times in the residual heat until the leaves wilt and release their aroma, then serve. Don't cook the leaves long, or they blacken and lose their fragrance."],"tips":["Lime juice? Authentic pad kra pao has no lime (that's larb). But if you like a little tang to cut the richness, squeeze some on the side of the plate when eating — don't cook it in, or the flavor turns odd.","Texture upgrade: if you can find long beans, dice them and stir-fry with the beef — their crunch goes great with the tender beef!","Will you use supermarket ground beef this time, or do you have steak at home to chop yourself? If you're chopping, I can suggest how to cut it for the best texture."]},"g3855":{"title":"Chestnut Chicken Soup","intro":"A perfect soup for autumn and winter: the starchy chestnuts make the broth rich and sweet, and with the savory chicken it's very warming and comforting. For a clear broth, whole (not crumbled) chestnuts and tender chicken, there are a few key tricks.","groups":[{"name":"Main ingredients","items":[["Tip","use bone-in free-range chicken — it holds up to stewing and has fragrant chicken fat"],["If using raw chestnuts, peel them first; vacuum-packed peeled chestnuts from the supermarket are easiest",""]]},{"name":"Flavor add-ins","items":[["Shiitake mushrooms are the perfect partner for chestnuts and add depth to the soup",""],["Ginger slices","5-6 (to remove off-flavors)"],["Red dates (jujubes)","6-8 (natural sweetness)"],["Goji berries","a few (for color at the end)"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Rice wine","1 Tbsp"],["Salt","to taste"],["Water","about 1500ml - 2000ml"]]},{"name":"Step 1: Prep","items":[["Shiitake","rinse and soak in warm water until soft (about 15 minutes); keep the soaking water (makes the broth more fragrant). Rinse the red dates"],["Chestnuts","if using raw chestnuts in the shell, boil 2-3 minutes and peel off the shell and skin while hot. Vacuum-packed ones just need a rinse"]]},{"name":"Step 3: Simmer the broth","items":[["Take a soup pot",""],["add the prepared chicken",""],["ginger",""],["soaked shiitake and red dates",""],["Add water (and the strained mushroom water) to cover",""],["Add","1 Tbsp rice wine"],["Boil on high, then cover and simmer on low","about 30 minutes"]]},{"name":"Step 5: Final seasoning","items":[["Before serving","2 minutes, add the goji berries"],["Add","salt to taste (add gradually, tasting as you go)"],["Turn off the heat — done!",""]]}],"steps":["[Step 2: Blanch the chicken] Put the raw chicken in a pot of cold water.","Heat on medium-low until boiling — you'll see lots of murky foam (blood and impurities).","Lift out the chicken, rinse in cold water and drain. Why? Starting in cold water slowly draws the blood out of the bones, so the soup is clear with no off-smell.","[Step 4: Add the chestnuts] Uncover and add the chestnuts.","Simmer on low another 15-20 minutes. Note: don't add the chestnuts at the start — cooked too long they fall apart into the soup and make it cloudy. Added in the last 20 minutes, they're soft but intact.","About chestnut texture: if you like a slightly grainy, thick broth, crush a few chestnuts and simmer them in — the soup becomes rich and sweet like a creamy soup.","In an electric (Tatung) cooker: after blanching, put everything except the goji in the inner pot with water to cover. Add 2 cups of water to the outer pot; when it pops up, rest 10 minutes, then add the goji and salt. The broth comes out very clear this way.","Variations: for more texture, add Chinese yam or carrot — both go beautifully with chestnut chicken soup."],"tips":["No complicated techniques — just blanch the chicken from cold water and add the chestnuts late, and you'll have restaurant-quality soup.","Are you using fresh chestnuts in the shell or peeled supermarket chestnuts? If they're in the shell, I can show you a quick way to peel them without hurting your hands."]},"g3876":{"title":"Vietnamese Beef Pho (Phở Bò)","intro":"Hello! Authentic Vietnamese beef pho has three parts: the broth, the toppings and assembling. The broth is the soul and needs a long simmer. Here's a classic recipe with approximate proportions — adjust to your pot size and taste:","groups":[{"name":"1. Broth (stock) — the soul","items":[["Marrow beef bones","3 - 3.5 kg (the more, the richer)","the body of the broth — rich gelatin and flavor."],["Beef shank / brisket","500 g - 1 kg (sliced after cooking as a topping)","adds meaty flavor and is the main cooked-beef topping."],["Water","5 - 7.5 liters","the base for the stock."],["Yellow onions","2 - 3 (halved, charred with skin on)","sweetness and aroma."],["Mature ginger","about 80 g (sliced, charred)","removes gaminess, adds warm aroma."],["Fish sauce","3 - 5 Tbsp (about 45 - 75 ml)","the soul of Vietnamese seasoning — salty umami."],["Yellow rock sugar / brown sugar","50 g or 1 - 2 Tbsp","adds depth and a sweet finish."],["Salt","to taste (about 3 - 4 Tbsp)","basic seasoning."]]},{"name":"Spice bag (in a muslin bag)","items":[["Star anise","8 - 10","core aroma — essential."],["Cinnamon","1 - 2 sticks (about 8 cm long)","core aroma."],["Fennel seeds","1 Tbsp (15 ml)"],["Coriander seeds","1 Tbsp (15 ml)"],["Cloves","5 - 6","strong — don't overdo it."],["Black cardamom","1 (cracked)","adds a deep smoky aroma."],["Bay leaves","2 - 3"]]},{"name":"2. Toppings and noodles","items":[["Dried rice noodles","about 80 - 100 g (per person)","soak in warm water for half an hour, boil 4-7 minutes, then drain and rinse."],["Cooked beef slices","as you like (the shank/brisket cooked in the broth)","thinly sliced."],["Raw beef slices","as you like (optional, very thin high-quality beef)","placed in the bowl and cooked by the boiling broth."],["Bean sprouts","1 big handful","blanch briefly or eat raw."],["Thinly sliced onion","a little","as thin as possible; soak in ice water to remove the sharpness and crisp it."],["Thai basil","1 - 2 sprigs","adds a distinctive aroma."],["Cilantro / green onion","a little","garnish and aroma."],["Lemon / lime","1 wedge","squeeze in at the table."],["Chilies (e.g. bird's eye)","a few slices","to taste."],["Fish sauce, hoisin sauce, sriracha","to taste","for dipping or adding to the soup."]]}],"steps":["Blanch and rinse: blanch the bones and shank in boiling water for 5-10 minutes, then rinse thoroughly. This is the key to a clear broth.","Char and toast: char the onions and ginger directly over a flame or in a dry pan on low until browned and fragrant. Toast all the spices from the spice bag in a dry pan on low for 2 minutes until fragrant.","Simmer: put the cleaned bones, shank, charred onions and ginger, spice bag, water, fish sauce, rock sugar and salt in a large pot. Bring to a boil on high, then turn to the lowest heat, uncovered (or lid ajar), at a gentle simmer for 6 - 8 hours or more.","Remove the meat and skim: the shank can usually come out after 1.5 - 2 hours; cool and slice. Keep skimming foam and fat throughout so the broth stays clear.","[3. Assembly] Put the soaked, cooked noodles in a bowl (about a third of the bowl).","Top with cooked beef and sliced onion (if using raw beef, put the raw slices on first).","Pour the boiling broth evenly over everything until covered (cooking the raw beef to medium-rare with the hot broth is the classic way).","Finally add the bean sprouts, basil, cilantro/green onion and a lime wedge."],"tips":["Are you looking for a quicker, easier pho recipe, or do you have specific questions about the spice proportions for the broth?"]},"g3912":{"title":"Japanese Teriyaki Chicken","intro":"Great teriyaki chicken comes down to the perfect sauce ratio, choosing and preparing the chicken, and controlling the heat when reducing the glaze. Here's a classic, easy Japanese teriyaki chicken recipe with a few keys to success.","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients (serves 2)","items":[["Chicken","boneless, skin-on chicken thighs","2 (about 300g-400g); thighs are recommended — more tender."],["Basic seasoning","salt","a little; for seasoning."],["Teriyaki sauce","light soy sauce","3 Tbsp"],["Garnish / finish","white sesame seeds","as you like; sprinkled on at the end."]]}],"steps":["Trim: cut off excess fat from the thighs, then make a few cuts in the thick parts (without cutting through the skin) so it cooks and seasons evenly.","Season: sprinkle both sides with a little salt and white pepper, massage gently and rest about 10-15 minutes.","Prevent curling: to stop the skin shrinking and curling while cooking, pin the meat with bamboo skewers along both sides of the skin; remove them after cooking.","[2. Sauce] Pour the soy sauce, mirin, sake and sugar into a bowl and stir until the sugar dissolves. Tip: if using honey, add it at the end when reducing so the heat doesn't destroy its nutrients.","Heat the pan: add a little oil to a skillet (the skin releases fat, so not much is needed).","Sear the skin: put the thighs skin side down in a cold or warm pan and cook slowly on medium-low for about 5-8 minutes until the skin releases lots of fat and turns golden and crisp.","Cook the other side: flip and cook the meat side until it changes color, about 3-5 minutes.","Pour off the fat: drain the excess fat, leaving just a little so the sauce isn't greasy.","Add the sauce: turn to medium-low and pour in the teriyaki sauce.","Simmer covered: cover and cook 2-3 minutes so the chicken cooks through and absorbs the sauce.","Reduce on high: uncover, turn to medium-high and keep spooning the sauce over the skin until it boils down to a thick, glossy syrup.","Turn off the heat: when the sauce coats the chicken in a tempting caramel color, turn off the heat.","[3. Serve] Take out the chicken and rest 1-2 minutes (this keeps the juices in so they don't run out when you cut it).","Slice diagonally into strips and pour over the remaining thick sauce.","Sprinkle with sesame seeds and green onion and serve with rice."],"tips":["Skin side down in a cold pan, slowly: the golden rule for crispy skin.","Sauce ratio 1:1:1: soy sauce, mirin and sake (or rice wine) at 1:1:1 is the classic teriyaki base.","Use enough heat when reducing: the sauce must reduce at high heat to get that thick, shiny teriyaki glaze that clings to the chicken."]},"g3913":{"title":"Chinese-Style Roast Lamb Ribs","intro":"China is huge, and every region has its own idea of the “best” roast lamb ribs. The two most popular styles are the bold northwestern style (Xinjiang) and the numbing-spicy Sichuan-Chongqing style. Here's a “gold standard” home recipe that combines the best of both, focused on tender meat and well-seasoned spices.","groups":[{"name":"1. Ingredients (serves 4)","items":[["Lamb ribs","lamb rib rack or shoulder ribs","about 1.5 - 2 kg; bone-in with a mix of fat and lean."],["To remove gaminess","ginger slices","1 knob; smashed"],["Basic marinade","salt","1 - 2 tsp"],["The essential spice rub","cumin seeds / ground cumin","3 Tbsp"]]},{"name":"Prepare the final seasoning in the last 20 minutes of slow roasting","items":[["Mix the rub","combine the cumin, chili powder, remaining salt and mixed spice powder in a small bowl"],["Unwrap","carefully open the foil (watch the steam), take out the lamb and drain off the juices"],["Coat with spices","brush both sides with a thin layer of oil, then sprinkle generously and evenly with the spice rub. Pat gently so it sticks"],["High-heat crisping","raise the oven to 220°C (430°F). Return the lamb to the oven, unwrapped, for about 10-15 minutes until the surface is crisp, glossy and the spices smell toasty"]]}],"steps":["This method slow-roasts first, then crisps at high heat, so the ribs are tender and juicy inside and crisp outside.","[Prep] Rinse: wash the lamb ribs; if you worry about gaminess, soak them in water for 1-2 hours, changing the water once.","Blanch: put the ribs in a pot of cold water with the ginger, leek or green onion and 1 Tbsp cooking wine. Bring to a boil on high, skim, then boil about 5-8 more minutes. Take out, rinse in warm water and drain.","[Marinate] Rub both sides with chopped onion, salt, 1 Tbsp vegetable oil and a little black pepper.","Put the ribs in a zip bag or container and refrigerate at least 4 hours, ideally overnight.","[Slow roast] Preheat the oven to 150°C (300°F).","Wrap: wrap the marinated ribs tightly in foil like a sealed parcel.","Slow roast: roast on the middle rack for about 2 to 2.5 hours until very tender (it pulls apart easily with a fork)."],"tips":["Richer Sichuan flavor: when marinating, add 1 Tbsp chili bean paste and a little ground Sichuan pepper for a numbing-spicy base.","Keep the aroma: use cumin seeds rather than only ground cumin — seeds popping at high heat give a longer-lasting, more layered aroma.","Grill / air fryer: after slow roasting, cut the ribs into pieces, coat with the spice rub and cook on high heat for 5-8 minutes until crisp."]},"g19":{"title":"Honey-Oozing Roast Sweet Potatoes (Frozen, Air Fryer Timer)","intro":"Frozen sweet potatoes don't need thawing — roasting them straight in the air fryer is the easiest way to draw out the syrup and get that oozing, honeyed texture. For your “set it before I leave, ready when I'm back from the gym” plan, here's the key consideration and the easiest approach:","groups":[],"steps":["[Option A: delay start] Gym time: say leaving and finishing the gym takes 1.5 hours, and roasting takes 40～45 minutes in total.","Delay setting: set the delay time to start in 45 minutes～1 hour. Temperature and time: 200°C for 40～45 minutes (45～50 minutes for large potatoes).","Result: the air fryer starts roasting when the countdown ends, so when you get home from the gym the sweet potatoes have just finished — piping hot, and the whole place smells wonderful.","[Option B: start before you leave] Two-stage roasting: 160°C for 25 minutes (low heat to cook and develop sugars), then 200°C for 20 minutes (high heat to make the syrup ooze and caramelize).","Use keep-warm / residual heat: if your air fryer has a keep-warm function, turn it on (usually 60～80°C). If not, don't open it after roasting — let the potatoes sit in the closed air fryer. Resting makes the caramelized syrup ooze out more evenly, and even after 30～60 minutes they stay hot and soft.","No foil needed: put the frozen sweet potatoes straight in the basket (line the bottom with parchment to catch the dripping syrup). Roasting them bare lets the skin dry out quickly for a thin, crisp skin and an oozing inside.","Poke test: when you're back, poke a toothpick or chopstick into the thickest part. If it slides through easily, they're fully cooked and syrupy.","Choose the right size: medium potatoes (egg- to fist-sized) ooze best and are easiest to time. For extra-large ones, add 10～15 minutes."],"tips":["Most air fryers have a delay/preset start, but since the potatoes come straight from the freezer, putting them in 1～2 hours early means they thaw at room temperature and release moisture on the surface, which spoils the crisp-skin, honeyed result.","Depending on your air fryer and when you leave, choose one of the following methods:"]},"g4667":{"title":"Hot and Sour Sea Bass Soup (Two Ways)","intro":"Hot and sour sea bass soup is appetizing and delicious, combining the sweetness of sea bass with a tangy, spicy broth. Here are two common versions:","groups":[{"name":"1. Thai-style tom yum sea bass — ingredients","items":[["Sea bass","300g"],["Tomato","1"],["Onion","30g"],["Galangal","10g"],["Lemongrass","10g"],["Chilies","20g"],["Kaffir lime leaves","2"],["Shallots","2"],["Oyster mushrooms","30g"],["Stock","500ml"],["Lime juice","30ml"],["Chopped green onion","10g"],["Chopped cilantro","10g"],["Salt","10g"],["Sugar","10g"],["Fish sauce","20ml"]]},{"name":"2. Sichuan pickled-cabbage sea bass — ingredients","items":[["Sea bass","1 whole fish"],["Pickled mustard greens (suan cai)","150g"],["Ginger","1 small knob"],["Green onions","2"],["Dried chilies","as you like"],["Sichuan peppercorns","a few"],["Stock","1000ml"],["Salt","to taste"],["Rice wine","as needed"],["White pepper","a pinch"],["Sugar","a pinch"],["Sichuan pepper oil","a little"],["Cornstarch","a little"]]}],"steps":["[1. Thai style] Rinse the sea bass and cut into pieces; cut the tomato and onion into chunks; slice the galangal, lemongrass and chilies; halve the lime leaves and shallots; cut the mushrooms into small pieces.","Put the stock and 100ml water in a pot with the galangal and lemongrass and simmer about 5 minutes.","Add the sea bass and cook about 5 minutes until boiling.","Add the tomato, onion, chilies, lime leaves, shallots and mushrooms and cook about 5 minutes until boiling; season with salt, sugar and fish sauce.","Turn off the heat, add the lime juice, green onion and cilantro, and it's done.","[2. Sichuan pickled-cabbage sea bass soup] Slice the fish, marinate with salt and rice wine for 5 minutes, then coat with cornstarch.","Cut the green onion whites diagonally and the greens into thin shreds; cut the ginger into thick strips and the pickled greens into shreds.","Heat oil, fry the ginger, then add the green onion whites, dried chilies and pickled greens and fry until fragrant.","Add the stock and season (salt, white pepper, sugar, Sichuan pepper oil).","Add the fish and cook through, then sprinkle with the green onion greens and Sichuan peppercorns."],"tips":["Sea bass is delicate — don't cook it too long or it toughens.","The sourness of the pickled greens affects the broth; adjust the amount to taste.","For a heartier soup, add tofu, enoki mushrooms or other extras.","I hope these recipes help!"]},"g196":{"title":"Three-Cup Chicken (Pressure Cooker Version)","intro":"Yes, three-cup chicken works perfectly in a pressure cooker! Abroad and in home kitchens, making it in a pressure cooker (including multicookers / Instant Pot) is very common. But since three-cup chicken is all about springy skin, tender meat and a thick, clinging sauce, the order of steps is a bit different from the traditional clay pot or wok:","groups":[],"steps":["The pressure cooker's big advantage is making the meat tender and flavorful very quickly, but because it's fully sealed and no water evaporates, you can't add much liquid like a stew. The key is: sauté with the lid off ➔ pressure 5-8 minutes ➔ lid off and reduce on high.","[1. Sauté first (lid off / sauté mode)] Put sesame oil in the pressure cooker (plus a little vegetable oil to stop burning), fry the ginger until slightly curled, then add the garlic and red chili and fry until fragrant.","Add the chicken (bone-in thigh is best) and stir-fry until the skin changes color and releases some fat; add rock sugar and fry until caramelized.","[2. Simplified sauce and pressure] Pour in the soy sauce and rice wine.","Important: don't add any water to the pressure cooker! The chicken releases lots of juice and fat, and too much liquid dilutes the flavor.","Lock the lid and cook on high pressure for about 5～8 minutes (for a whole large leg cut into pieces, 5-6 minutes keeps it springy; longer and it falls apart).","After releasing the pressure, you'll find lots of delicious sauce in the pot.","Leave the lid off and boil on high (sauté mode) to reduce until the sauce is thick and sticky and coats the chicken.","Just before turning off the heat, add a handful of Thai basil and toss in the residual heat to release its aroma.","[Why make it in a pressure cooker] No splattering, clean kitchen: frying ginger and chicken skin in sesame oil usually splatters; the deep inner pot cuts smoke and grease a lot.","Flavor goes in faster: high pressure quickly drives the soy and sesame oil flavors deep into the meat and bones.","Very fast: from prep to table in about 15-20 minutes."],"tips":[]},"g3105":{"title":"Sourdough Discard Cinnamon Rolls","intro":"Absolutely! Using sourdough discard in cinnamon rolls is many home bakers' secret weapon — it adds layers of flavor without waiting a whole day like a true sourdough bread.","groups":[],"steps":["[If you want to start right now, follow this logic] Mix: combine your discard, warm milk, melted butter, egg, sugar, a little dry yeast and flour.","Knead: knead until smooth (cinnamon rolls don't need the perfect windowpane of sandwich bread).","Rise: in a warm spot for about 1.5 - 2 hours, until doubled.","Shape and bake: spread with cinnamon sugar, roll up, proof for another 45 minutes, then bake.","Want to try this “hybrid” method? I can work out the exact recipe proportions for you!"],"tips":["Because your discard's activity is already dropping, relying on it alone to raise a rich, sugary enriched dough like cinnamon rolls would take a painfully long time.","Recommended: use a hybrid of commercial yeast + discard.","Benefits: commercial yeast provides reliable rising power (soft, fluffy rolls), and the discard brings rich aroma and moistness.","If you have a regular cinnamon roll recipe (with dry yeast), substitute like this:","Calculation: if you add 100g discard (100% hydration), subtract 50g flour and 50g water (or milk) from the original recipe.","Yeast: keep the original amount of dry yeast, or reduce it only slightly.","Flavor balance: cinnamon rolls are very sweet, and the gentle sourness of the discard balances the sweet icing nicely.","Stays fresh longer: sourdough keeps the bread moist longer, so it's still soft when reheated the next day."]},"g329":{"title":"Middle Eastern Taiwanese Egg Yolk Pastry (with Ghee)","intro":"Absolutely — and it'll be wonderfully fragrant and flaky! In the Middle East (like Jordan), if you can't find the unsalted butter or lard used for Taiwanese egg yolk pastries, ghee (clarified butter) is a perfect substitute, and in some ways even better than regular butter.","groups":[{"name":"Why ghee works","items":[["Flakiness (excellent)","regular butter contains about 16%–20% water, while ghee is 100% pure butterfat. In the inner (oil) dough, with no water to develop gluten, the layers bake up very distinct — extremely flaky and melt-in-your-mouth"],["Aroma (very rich)","ghee is cooked down, giving it a strong toasty, buttery aroma, so the pastries have an intense nutty, milky fragrance"]]}],"steps":["Adjusting the fat: regular butter contains water, ghee is pure fat. If your recipe uses regular butter, use 5%–10% less ghee, or add a little more water to the outer dough, otherwise the oil dough may be a bit soft.","Ghee has a slightly higher melting point than butter but is still soft at room temperature. If the dough feels too oily or soft while working, chill it for 10 minutes to firm up before rolling — it'll be much easier to wrap.","In short, ghee is essentially ultra-pure butterfat, and traditional Chinese pastries rely on high-purity fats for flaky layers. It's not only fine for egg yolk pastries — the flakiness can rival lard!"],"tips":["Cow ghee: most recommended! Closest in taste to Taiwanese butter, with a gentle aroma.","Sheep / goat ghee (samneh): very common in the Middle East, but it has a stronger lamb-like flavor; if you're not used to it, it may taste odd in a dessert.","Vegetable ghee: it works, but the aroma is far below pure animal fat."]},"g385":{"title":"Peach Wine (with Wine Yeast)","intro":"Using a dedicated wine yeast for peach wine gives precise control over fermentation, producing a clean-tasting peach wine with the right alcohol level.","groups":[],"steps":["Temperature: the warmer the fermentation (e.g. 28°C), the faster the yeast works — primary fermentation can finish in 7 days. Cool fermentation (15–18°C) is very slow but keeps more of the peach's delicate aroma.","Yeast strain and amount: strong dedicated yeasts (like EC-1118) ferment very fast; relying on wild or ordinary yeast stretches the process out a lot.","When to remove the fruit: soaking the fruit pulp too long can create bitterness or off-flavors, so it's usually strained out after 5–7 days — but the strained wine still needs weeks of secondary fermentation and settling.","Clearing and stabilizing: alcoholic (primary) fermentation takes only 7–10 days, but for the suspended yeast and pectin to settle completely to commercial-grade clarity, it needs to rest 30–60 days."],"tips":["The difference between these timeframes comes down to which stage of fermentation is meant and the method used:","Comparison | Done in 7 days (fast primary fermentation / infused wine) | 40+ days (full winemaking / aging and clearing)\nWhat the time covers | only the vigorous primary fermentation, or the alcohol-infusion method | primary + secondary fermentation + natural settling and clearing\nHow it works | with the right nutrients and temperature, yeast eats 80-90% of the sugar and turns it into alcohol within 7 days | after primary fermentation, slow fermentation, acid and ester reactions (flavor maturing) take weeks\nQuality and taste | cloudy, yeasty, harsh; may have residual sugar or keep fizzing in the bottle | clear, smooth and rounded, stable and keeps well long-term\nCommon types | quick commercial brewing, warm fast home fermentation, or infused “liquor + sugar + peaches” (not truly fermented) | traditional pure fermented fruit wine"]},"g158":{"title":"Date Yeast Water","intro":"Making yeast using dates relies on capturing the naturally occurring wild yeasts on the surface of dried fruit. This creates yeast water — a natural, low-acid alternative to sourdough starter that gives bread a light, sweet rise without sour notes.","groups":[],"steps":["[Day 1: Set up the culture] Clean the glass jar thoroughly with hot water.","Add the dates, water, and sugar/honey to the jar.","Seal the lid, shake vigorously to dissolve the sugar, then loosen the lid slightly (gas needs space to escape so pressure doesn't build up).","Place the jar in a warm spot out of direct sunlight (ideally 24°C–28°C or 75°F–82°F).","[Days 2–5: Fermentation and aeration] Twice daily: tighten the lid, shake the jar well for a few seconds to aerate the water, then loosen the lid again.","What to look for: Days 2–3: tiny bubbles begin forming around the dates, and the dates start to swell and float to the top. Days 4–5: the liquid turns cloudy and fizzy and smells pleasantly fruity and fermented (similar to cider).","[Days 5–6: Ready to use] When you open the lid and hear a distinct fizz, and the surface has a light layer of micro-foam, your yeast water is active and ready.","Strain out the dates and discard them.","[How to bake with date yeast water] You can use the active yeast water directly or build a levain (preferment) for bread baking:","Build a levain: mix equal weights of strained date yeast water and bread flour (e.g. 100 g yeast water + 100 g flour). Cover and ferment at room temperature for 8–12 hours until foamy and doubled.","Substitute in recipes: use this levain in place of commercial yeast or sourdough starter in your favorite bread recipes. (Wild yeast proofing times are usually longer than instant yeast, so give your dough plenty of time to rise.)","[Storage and maintenance] Refrigeration: store unused yeast water in the fridge with a loose lid. It stays active for 1–2 months.","Refreshing: to revive or maintain it, keep about 100–200 ml of old yeast water, add 1 fresh date, 1 tsp sugar and 300 ml fresh water, then leave it in a warm room for 1–2 days until fizzy again."],"tips":["2–3 unsulfured, organic dried dates (pitted or whole; unsulfured is critical because sulfur dioxide kills wild yeast)","500 ml (approx. 2 cups) unchlorinated water (spring, well, or boiled and cooled water — chlorine inhibits wild yeast)","1 tablespoon sugar or honey (gives the wild yeast extra fuel to thrive)","1 clean glass jar with a lid"]},"g156":{"title":"Building a Levain with Date Yeast Water","intro":"Building a levain (preferment) with date yeast water is straightforward. Because wild yeast water lacks the natural acidity of a flour sourdough starter, it produces a pure, sweet ferment that lets the natural flavor of your flour shine through. For most bread recipes, a 100% hydration levain works well.","groups":[],"steps":["Bring your date yeast water to room temperature (22°C–26°C).","Shake the bottle well so the wild yeast cells suspended in the liquid are evenly distributed.","Strain out any fruit pieces or sediment.","In a clean glass jar or container, combine 100g date yeast water and 100g flour.","Stir thoroughly with a fork or spatula until no dry flour remains.","Scrape down the sides of the jar and mark the starting level on the outside with a rubber band or dry-erase marker.","Cover loosely with a lid or plastic wrap to let gas escape.","Place the jar in a warm spot (24°C–28°C).","Let it ferment for 8 to 12 hours (wild fruit yeast is slightly slower than commercial yeast, so be patient).","[How to tell when it's ready] Your levain is at peak strength and ready to mix into dough when:","Volume: it has doubled or tripled in size.","Surface: the top looks domed, foamy and covered with tiny gas bubbles.","Texture: scooping a bit reveals a webbed, spongy interior full of trapped air.","Aroma: it smells sweet, yeasty and slightly fruity, with no sharp vinegary sourness.","[How to use it in your recipe] Substitution: replace the levain/starter in your recipe with this preferment at a 1:1 ratio.","Baker's percentage: use this levain at 20% to 30% of the total flour weight. Example: for 500 g total flour, use 100 g to 150 g of active date yeast levain.","Adjust the water: this 200 g of levain contains 100 g of water, so subtract 100 g from the water added directly to your main dough."],"tips":["100g active date yeast water (shaken and strained)","100g flour (bread flour, whole wheat, or a 50/50 mix)"]},"g1278":{"title":"Pickled Mustard Greens Beef Noodle Soup","intro":"For a Taiwanese pickled mustard greens beef noodle soup with a rich broth and tender beef, the soul is frying the aromatics for the broth and dry-frying the pickled greens. If the pickled greens go straight into the soup, the sourness turns flat and bitter; frying them first with garlic and sugar releases their fresh aroma and sweetness.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients (serves about 4)","items":[["Beef shank or brisket","900g","main"],["Handmade noodles","4 portions","main"],["Bok choy","as needed","main"],["Green onions","3 (cut into lengths)","broth aromatics"],["Ginger slices","6","broth aromatics"],["Garlic","8 cloves (smashed)","broth aromatics"],["Chilies","1-2","broth aromatics"],["Onion","half (chunks)","broth aromatics"],["Tomato","1 (chunks)","broth aromatics"],["Spicy chili bean paste (doubanjiang)","2 Tbsp","seasoning and spice packet"],["Soy sauce","100ml","seasoning and spice packet"],["Rice wine","50ml","seasoning and spice packet"],["Rock sugar","1.5 Tbsp","seasoning and spice packet"],["Store-bought beef braising spice packet","1","seasoning and spice packet"],["Water","about 1800-2000ml","seasoning and spice packet"],["Hakka pickled mustard greens","200g","fried pickled greens"],["Minced garlic","1 Tbsp","fried pickled greens"],["Chopped chili","a little","fried pickled greens"],["Sugar","1 Tbsp","fried pickled greens"],["Sesame oil","1 tsp","fried pickled greens"]]}],"steps":[],"tips":[]},"g1267":{"title":"Lotus Root Chicken Soup (Tatung Cooker)","intro":"Lotus root chicken soup in a Tatung electric cooker is very easy — just remember to blanch the chicken first and get the outer-pot water right, and you'll have a clear, sweet broth with soft, starchy lotus root.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients (serves about 3–4)","items":[["Bone-in chicken","about 600 g (free-range-style thighs hold up to stewing without drying out)"],["Lotus root","about 300–400 g (choose short, thick segments without dark spots)"],["Ginger slices","4–5"],["Red dates (jujubes)","6–8 (rinsed and cut open or scored for more flavor)"],["Goji berries","a small handful (added just before serving)"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Rice wine","2 Tbsp"],["Salt","to taste"]]}],"steps":[],"tips":[]},"g690":{"title":"Tomato and Egg Noodles (serves 5)","intro":"The classic tomato and egg noodles for five — the keys are a rich sauce and silky egg. 500 g dried noodles with 6 eggs and 4 large tomatoes is the perfect ratio.","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Dried noodles","500 g (handmade or medium-thick noodles are best)","main"],["Tomatoes","4 (cut into chunks)","main"],["Eggs","6 (beaten with a pinch of salt)","main"],["Garlic","4 cloves (minced)","aromatics"],["Green onions","2 (chopped)","aromatics"]]},{"name":"Seasoning","items":[["Ketchup","3 Tbsp"],["Soy sauce","2 Tbsp"],["Salt","1 tsp"],["Sugar","1.5 Tbsp"],["Water or stock","about 800-1000 mL"]]}],"steps":[],"tips":[]},"g4308":{"title":"Pasteurizing Fermented Rice (Jiuniang)","intro":"Pasteurizing your finished fermented rice (jiuniang) stops the fermentation, preserving its flavor and extending its shelf life. Here are the steps:","groups":[],"steps":["Heat a water bath: fill a pot with enough water to come about two-thirds up the jars. Heat the water to 60～70°C — this is the key temperature range for pasteurizing.","Add the jars: put the filled jars of fermented rice in the pot. If they float, press them down gently or weigh them down.","Time it: once the water holds steady between 60～70°C, start timing 20～30 minutes. Keep checking with a thermometer so it doesn't get too hot and damage the flavor.","Remove and cool: carefully take out the jars. If they're sealed, let them cool naturally; if not, let them cool before putting on the lids.","Refrigerate: once cooled, put the fermented rice in the fridge right away to keep its quality and flavor."],"tips":["Equipment: a pot large enough for all your jars, a thermometer, and the bottled fermented rice.","Containers: use heat-resistant glass or plastic bottles with lids that seal. Clean and sterilize the bottles and lids before pasteurizing.","Temperature control: too hot (over 75°C) kills all the yeast and enzymes but may also destroy the wine aroma and sweetness; too cool may not fully stop fermentation. Precise temperature control is very important.","Containers: make sure they're clean and sealed — this keeps other microbes out and extends shelf life.","Flavor change: pasteurized fermented rice tastes a little different from fresh. Once fermentation stops, the sweetness becomes stable and the alcohol won't get stronger."]},"g622":{"title":"Olive Oil and Balsamic Vinaigrette","intro":"A classic olive oil and balsamic vinaigrette is easy — just learn the golden ratio and how to emulsify. The basic golden ratio:","groups":[{"name":"Ingredients","items":[["Olive oil","balsamic vinegar = 3 : 1 (e.g. 3 Tbsp olive oil, 1 Tbsp balsamic vinegar)"],["Lemon juice","a few drops to 1/2 tsp (adds a fresh, fruity tang)"],["Salt & black pepper","a small pinch each (to taste)"]]}],"steps":["Mix the acid and seasoning first: pour the balsamic vinegar and lemon juice into a bowl, add salt and freshly ground black pepper, and stir until the salt dissolves in the acid.","Add the olive oil slowly: whisk quickly with a whisk (or fork) while pouring the olive oil in a thin stream.","Emulsify fully: keep whisking until the dressing is even and slightly thick."],"tips":["Quick shake method: put everything in a small glass jar with a lid, close tightly and shake hard for 15–20 seconds — easy emulsifying.","Extra flavor (optional): for a more stable or layered dressing, whisk in 1/2 tsp Dijon mustard or a little minced garlic.","Great with mixed greens, arugula, cherry tomatoes or mozzarella."]},"g2280":{"title":"My Usual Lemon Vinaigrette","intro":"This is the lemon vinaigrette recipe you asked about in our earlier conversation — perfect with your favorite quinoa salad:","groups":[{"name":"Lemon vinaigrette","items":[["Extra-virgin olive oil","2 parts"],["Fresh lemon juice","1 part"],["Seasoning","salt and black pepper to taste"],["Optional (for depth)","a teaspoon of honey or Dijon mustard, which helps the oil and lemon emulsify more evenly"]]}],"steps":["Mix: put everything in a small jar and shake hard until slightly thick and emulsified.","Pairing: this dressing goes perfectly with cucumber, tomato, carrot and feta cheese.","Storage: if you make a big batch, it keeps in the fridge for about 3-5 days; shake again before using."],"tips":["This uses your preferred 2:1 ratio (olive oil : lemon juice) — lighter than the standard ratio, with a Mediterranean sweet-tart brightness."]},"g20261001a":{"title":"Chicken and Bell Pepper Tomato Pasta (645 g chicken + 500 g pasta, serves 4)","intro":"A hearty 4-serving version built around 645 g of chicken breast and a whole 500 g pack of pasta. Yellow and green bell peppers give the red sauce a red-yellow-green look and balance the tomato acidity. 15 minutes prep, about 20 minutes cooking.","groups":[{"name":"1. Main ingredients and vegetables","items":[["Chicken breast","645g (bite-size pieces)"],["Pasta","500g (spaghetti or penne)"],["Yellow bell pepper","1 (bite-size chunks)"],["Green bell pepper","1 (bite-size chunks)"]]},{"name":"2. Tomato sauce base","items":[["Canned chopped tomatoes","2 cans (about 800g total)"],["Tomato paste","2.5 Tbsp (the key to rich, fruity umami)"],["Onion","1 (finely diced)"],["Garlic","6–8 cloves (minced)"]]},{"name":"3. Seasoning and marinade","items":[["Salt","1.25 tsp","chicken marinade"],["Black pepper","1 tsp","chicken marinade"],["Olive oil","2 tsp","chicken marinade"],["Olive oil","about 4 Tbsp (for the chicken, peppers and sauce)"],["Italian seasoning","2 tsp"],["Salt and black pepper","to taste (adjust at the end)"],["Pasta water","save 200–250ml (about 1 large bowl)"],["Parmesan, fresh basil","as you like (optional, to finish)"]]}],"steps":["Marinate the chicken: cut into bite-size pieces and mix with 1.25 tsp salt, 1 tsp black pepper and 2 tsp olive oil. Marinate 10–15 minutes.","Cook the pasta: bring 4–5 liters of water to a boil in a deep pot, add 2 Tbsp salt and the 500g pasta, and cook 2 minutes less than the package says. Before draining, always save 200–250ml of pasta water.","Sear the chicken in two batches: heat olive oil in a skillet and sear each batch on medium-high until golden on both sides (about 70–80% cooked), then remove. Adding it all at once cools the pan, and the chicken releases water and stews.","Sear the peppers: in the chicken fat left in the pan, stir-fry the peppers on high for 30–45 seconds until glossy and fragrant, then remove — don't leave them cooking.","Start the sauce: add a little more olive oil, cook the onion until soft and translucent, add the garlic until fragrant, then stir in the tomato paste for 1–2 minutes.","Simmer the sauce: add the canned tomatoes and Italian seasoning and cook until thickened; season with salt and black pepper.","Toss and emulsify: add the cooked pasta and seared chicken to the sauce, pour in half the pasta water (about 100ml) and toss quickly on high heat; if it looks dry, add the rest gradually so the sauce coats every strand smoothly.","In the last 30 seconds, return the peppers and toss, then serve topped with Parmesan and basil."],"tips":["Sear the chicken in two batches: 645g is a lot, and searing hot in batches keeps it from releasing water, locking in juices and flavor.","Add the peppers in the last 30 seconds: sear and remove them first, then return them at the end to keep the bright colors and crunch.","500g of pasta absorbs a lot: add the pasta water in stages so the sauce emulsifies perfectly — never dry, never separated.","Tomato paste is the key to richness: canned tomatoes alone can taste thin and sour; fried tomato paste adds deep fruity umami.","Like it creamy? Stir 30–50ml cream into the finished sauce for a smooth rosa (pink) sauce."]}};
